{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'.kibitz\\\\comfyui.local.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"    1: # Local ComfyUI Profile for Kibitz\r\n    2: \r\n    3: Refreshed for portability QA: `2026-09-01`\r\n    4: Repo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio`\r\n    5: \r\n    6: This is the compact form of the profile generated by\r\n    7: `kibitz/scripts/comfyui_profile.py`. The generator's raw package scan included\r\n    8: old `.claude/worktrees` and exceeded the Windows process argument limit, so only\r\n    9: current, review-relevant facts are retained here.\r\n   10: \r\n   11: ## Hardware and runtime\r\n   12: \r\n   13: - OS: Windows 11; Python 3.12.11.\r\n   14: - GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 Laptop GPU, 16303 MiB physical VRAM.\r\n   15: - Project review gate: 14.5 GiB.\r\n   16: - ComfyUI root: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI`.\r\n   17: - Production headless port: `127.0.0.1:8000`.\r\n   18: - Live output base: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\output`.\r\n   19: - Live published episodes: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\output\\otr\\obs`.\r\n   20: - The separate ComfyUI-Installs `otr\\obs` tree is historical and must not be\r\n   21:   moved, deleted, or accepted as the live publish receipt.\r\n   22: \r\n   23: ## Canonical workflow\r\n   24: \r\n   25: - Source of truth: `workflows/otr_canonical.json`.\r\n   26: - Current shape: 23 nodes, 60 links, 132 widget slots.\r\n   27: - Any node/input/widget/wiring change must land in this exact JSON in the same\r\n   28:   change as code.\r\n   29: - `widgets_values` is positional. Append new optional widgets; do not insert\r\n   30:   into the middle of existing values.\r\n   31: - Every production API/headless/soak acceptance run must load the canonical\r\n   32:   workflow. Bench graphs cannot qualify a shipping path.\r\n   33: - Required post-edit gates: JSON round-trip, `OTR_WorkflowValidator`, widget\r\n   34:   count versus live `INPUT_TYPES`, wired input-name checks, link integrity, and\r\n   35:   live `/object_info` class checks.\r\n   36: \r\n   37: ## LTX 2.5 review facts\r\n   38: \r\n   39: - Production CPU-pins the LTX text encoder via\r\n   40:   `nodes/_otr_video_engines/eng_ltx25.py`; lab GPU-residency measurements must\r\n   41:   not be transferred into production without re-proof.\r\n   42: - The settled operator decision is to ship the two-stage quality path. Do not\r\n   43:   re-run the visual, grid-score, VRAM-arm, or face-drift comparisons.\r\n   44: - Acceptance must publish through the canonical workflow to the live `otr/obs`\r\n   45:   tree and positively prove stage two executed.\r\n   46: - Positive proof must use the loader/node's own log evidence: the latent\r\n   47:   upsampler ran per shot and VAE decode occurred at the upscaled canvas, not\r\n   48:   832x480. Adapter self-reporting is insufficient.\r\n   49: - Real production cost materially above 2.6x is a hard stop requiring operator\r\n   50:   approval.\r\n   51: - Reset selectively before every headless run. Never blanket-kill Python\r\n   52:   processes; confirm port 8000 is free and GPU memory is at desktop baseline.\r\n   53: \r\n   54: ## Reviewer discipline\r\n   55: \r\n   56: - Read the real Windows repo files and cite exact file:line evidence.\r\n   57: - Treat any unverified runtime or VRAM statement as `verify:` rather than fact.\r\n   58: - Check node registration, `INPUT_TYPES`, return contracts, tensor layout,\r\n   59:   caching, model residency, workflow wiring, and acceptance instrumentation as\r\n   60:   one integrated change.\r\n   61: - Ignore `.claude/worktrees`, generated graphs, and stale workflow copies.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'kibitz-runs\\\\2026-09-02-still-in-peer\\\\r2\\\\input.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"    1: # Driver anchor -- item 2, the still-in LAB PEER for the AnimateDiff v3 lane (2026-09-02)\r\n    2: \r\n    3: Campaign: `docs/2026-09-02-animatediff-ledger-experiments/` (PROBLEM_STATEMENT.md, fresh-eyes/,\r\n    4: instrument/). Judged order: (0) the instrument -- CODED and merged 2026-09-02 (969f2578, dc4beea5);\r\n    5: (1) the adapter sweep -- running; **(2) this item**; (3) pack language under the 77-token budget;\r\n    6: (4) one timeline per shot. This anchor is the driver's code-grounded position BEFORE the panel;\r\n    7: the four readers' fact digest it rests on is reproduced in section 2 with file:line.\r\n    8: \r\n    9: Operator rulings that bind this item, verbatim where recorded:\r\n   10: * \"it is paramount that the prompting for this new AnimateDiff workflow must obey the visual\r\n   11:   style ... I think we lost the visual style.\" (PROBLEM_STATEMENT 5B)\r\n   12: * \"anime is not the only target; all visual styles need to craft the episode as well when\r\n   13:   selected.\" (GO_FORWARD_PLAN 1.14, 2026-09-02 evening)\r\n   14: * \"always remember when adding a new video pack to follow the preflight video guide\" --\r\n   15:   `docs/VIDEO_LANE_PREFLIGHT.md` gates 1-8 + `tests/test_lane_preflight_matrix.py` BEFORE the id\r\n   16:   is registered or a profile names it.\r\n   17: * No damping words in motion prompts; flicker is a recipe problem, never a wording one; story\r\n   18:   quality is done; every arm is an ADDITION judged against the shipped recipe with a same-seed\r\n   19:   A/A null first; recipes are hard-won and never traded for VRAM or speed.\r\n   20: \r\n   21: ## 1. What the item is, in one paragraph\r\n   22: \r\n   23: A NEW engine id beside the shipping `animatediff15_v3_haunted_video`, subclassing it, that renders\r\n   24: the same recipe (v3 module + adapter, 20 / 8.0 / euler / normal, 512x288 hold-2, static 16/4\r\n   25: pyramid, the live negative) but starts the sampler from a STILL instead of an empty latent. The\r\n   26: still is the lane's own **in-family plate**: one 512x288 frame minted in the same graph from the\r\n   27: same SD1.5 checkpoint with the adapter at 0.0, prompted with the style pack's full language and\r\n   28: the ledger's setting, palette and lighting, then VAE-encoded, repeated to the sampler batch and\r\n   29: sampled at denoise < 1.0 (E1). The plate carries the STYLE into every frame at the pixel level,\r\n   30: which the two-word cue cannot; the motion recipe is untouched. ADE image injection (E2) is a\r\n   31: later knob behind its own VRAM probe. Nothing about the shipping engine, its graph, its tests,\r\n   32: its receipt id or the 4060 provisioning set moves.\r\n   33: \r\n   34: ## 2. Code facts the design rests on (readers 2026-09-02, verified file:line)\r\n   35: \r\n   36: **The registry contract and the gates a new lane owes.**\r\n   37: * Registration is `@register` + a unique `name` (`nodes/_otr_shared/engine_registry_base.py:141-150`);\r\n   38:   the pack's own gates then demand a `CAPABILITIES` row (`registry.py:273-275, 688-721`), an explicit\r\n   39:   `accepts_still`, a declared `still_plan` tuple (`()` valid), exactly one live menu option\r\n   40:   (`exact_menu_option_for`), and a row in the generated `docs/ENGINE_MATRIX.md`\r\n   41:   (`tests/test_lane_preflight_matrix.py:940-964`, `tests/test_engine_matrix_doc.py:43-49`).\r\n   42: * The preflight suite iterates `all_engine_names()` with `EXPECTED_RED` empty, so registration IS\r\n   43:   the gate (`test_lane_preflight_matrix.py:176-273, 383-393`). Files a new lane touches for the\r\n   44:   matrix to pass: `eng_<name>.py`, `registry.py` (CAPABILITIES), `_otr_video_engines/__init__.py`\r\n   45:   (guarded import), `scripts/build_video_evidence_manifest.py` (a >= 6-word `admission_unenforced`\r\n   46:   sentence, then regenerate the manifest -- never hand-edit it), `docs/ENGINE_MATRIX.md`\r\n   47:   (`tools/engine_matrix.py`), and a `tests/test_<lane>.py` naming the id AND its exact canvas\r\n   48:   (G2.2 pin, `:627-636, 711-731`). G8 (the solo smoke) is the only live gate and is prose-only\r\n   49:   (`docs/VIDEO_LANE_PREFLIGHT.md:234-243`).\r\n   50: * A sibling of an existing lane also owes the G1.3 per-artifact CLASS attributes with byte floors\r\n   51:   below and within 15% of the real files (`tests/test_ghost_signal_peers.py:106-127`).\r\n   52: \r\n   53: **The shipping lane's seams.**\r\n   54: * `GhostSignalV3HauntedEngine` is the only registered Ghost engine; base classes are unregistered\r\n   55:   and their ids tombstoned (`eng_ghost_signal_official.py:68-175`). The graph is built INLINE in\r\n   56:   `render_clip` (`eng_ghost_signal.py:781-970`) from a closed `GHOST_NODE_CANDIDATES` dict of seven\r\n   57:   classes (`:154-162`); `ADE_ADE_OMITTED_SOCKETS` pins `sample_settings` and friends as OMITTED\r\n   58:   (`:166-172`); tests pin 7 resolver names, 8 executed node instances on the haunted lane, the\r\n   59:   omitted sockets, `sampler_inputs.latent == 'EmptyLatentImage'`, `init_image is None`, and\r\n   60:   `model_artifacts` == [checkpoint, motion_module, adapter] (`tests/test_ghost_signal_lane.py:226-243,\r\n   61:   416-520`; `tests/test_ghost_signal_haunted.py:165-172`; `tests/test_render_receipts.py:129-143`).\r\n   62:   => the peer is a NEW subclass that overrides `name`, `recipe_receipt_id`, `_node_candidates`,\r\n   63:   `render_clip`, `sampler_inputs_for`, `model_artifacts`, `session_identity`, `shot_cache_identity`;\r\n   64:   it never edits the shipping class.\r\n   65: * `_build_render_request` reads only shot/request ids, text_prompt, negative_prompt,\r\n   66:   timing.target_frame_count and seed_bundle.request_seed (`eng_ghost_signal.py:732-778`); the seed\r\n   67:   is `_seed_from_hash(render_request_hash, shot_id)` and does not move when a still, a denoise or an\r\n   68:   adapter strength is added (`render_driver.py:548-553, 3768-3772`) -- same-seed A/Bs stay valid.\r\n   69: * `GHOST_CHECKPOINT_NAME` is a module constant read inside methods (`:411, 535-536, 715, 466, 497`),\r\n   70:   not a class seam; the peer keeps the checkpoint, so this stays a note (promote to a class seam\r\n   71:   only when a per-style checkpoint arm E11 is opened).\r\n   72: * `prepare()` runs only the `ckpt` node and registers the base MODEL patcher; STAGE 2 encodes the\r\n   73:   two prompts and releases the CLIP; STAGE 3 builds context -> lora -> ADE loader -> EmptyLatentImage\r\n   74:   -> KSampler; STAGE 4 decodes with the external `vae` handle (`eng_ghost_signal.py:678-729,\r\n   75:   849-945`). `run_graph` accepts `external_results` as legal Wire sources (`wrapper_bridge.py:460-560`).\r\n   76:   The plain checkpoint MODEL stays usable beside the ADE-cloned one (ADE clones,\r\n   77:   `ADE nodes_gen1.py:85`), so a single-frame plate branch on `Wire('base_model')` is legal in the\r\n   78:   same STAGE 3 graph.\r\n   79: * No image node (LoadImage, VAEEncode, ImageScale, RepeatLatentBatch, LatentFromBatch) is in the\r\n   80:   lane's Phase-0 `/object_info` capture (`docs/2026-08-22-ghost-signal-object-info.json` holds the\r\n   81:   seven shipping classes); the peer needs its own live capture before pinning any class id -- ONE\r\n   82:   NAME PER ALIAS, no probing at render time.\r\n   83: \r\n   84: **How a still reaches an engine today, and why the peer does not go that way first.**\r\n   85: * A still reaches an engine ONLY as `request['asset_refs']['init_image']` (`render_driver.py:342-380,\r\n   86:   4389-4410`); `render_driver` joins the beat's SCENE still when the family is image_to_video /\r\n   87:   static_motion or `init_image` is in `required_inputs` (`:2240-2275, 1307-1313`), and raises\r\n   88:   `DeferredImageGapError` with no portrait fallback when the row is missing. Whether the image\r\n   89:   phase MINTS anything is `engine_consumes_still` = `accepts_still` (`otr_image_gen_dispatcher.py:640-657`).\r\n   90:   Scene stills are 1472x832 from a foreign image family (`otr_meta_brief_image_prompt.py:471-484`).\r\n   91: * The lane is in `_NO_STILL_VIDEO_ENGINES` (`scripts/otr_provision.py:1546-1551`) precisely so the\r\n   92:   8 GB box is not gated on the ~11 GB Klein bundle (PROBLEM_STATEMENT 7; judgment 1.2). A peer that\r\n   93:   flips `accepts_still=True` would be pulled out of that set on the 4060 unless its id is excluded\r\n   94:   by hand, and would have to declare the G3.7-scoped ownership fields differently\r\n   95:   (`test_lane_preflight_matrix.py:1036-1038`).\r\n   96: * The receipt already hashes `still_sha256` from `asset_refs.init_image` content\r\n   97:   (`render_driver.py:4095, 4132`); an engine-minted plate is NOT on `asset_refs`, so the peer\r\n   98:   must put the plate's identity into `sampler_inputs` itself (section 4.5).\r\n   99: \r\n  100: **Conditioning mechanics available on this install (ComfyUI 0.34.3, ADE 1.6.0).**\r\n  101: * Stock init-latent path: `VAEEncode(pixels, vae)`; `RepeatLatentBatch(amount <= 64)`\r\n  102:   (`ComfyUI/nodes.py:1304-1330`); `LatentFromBatch(samples, batch_index, length)`; `KSampler.denoise`\r\n  103:   0-1. `ADE_UpscaleAndVAEEncode(image, vae, latent_size, scale_method, crop)` resizes + encodes in\r\n  104:   one node (`animatediff/nodes_animatelcmi2v.py:133-159`). `VHS_DuplicateLatents` exists\r\n  105:   (`comfyui-videohelpersuite`) but VHS is NOT in `otr_provision.py`'s pinned set -- not a dependency\r\n  106:   the peer may take.\r\n  107: * Ghost source batch = `max(ceil(T / 2), 16)` latents with `max_frames = 0` (`eng_ghost_signal.py:\r\n  108:   201-211, 385-392`): a beat over 128 delivered frames needs more than one RepeatLatentBatch.\r\n  109: * `ADE_NoisedImageInjection` needs `ADE_AnimateDiffSamplingSettings` on the loader's\r\n  110:   `sample_settings` socket (the socket the shipping lane pins OMITTED) and decodes + re-encodes the\r\n  111:   WHOLE batch through the VAE at each injection point (`animatediff/sampling.py:645-693`) -- E2 is\r\n  112:   a second arm behind a VRAM probe, exactly as the judgment ordered.\r\n  113: * ComfyUI-Advanced-ControlNet is not installed; SparseCtrl (E7) stays deferred.\r\n  114: \r\n  115: **Stills, layout, style packs.**\r\n  116: * Episode stills live at `<output>/otr/episodes/<episode_id>/stills/{object_id}_{hash12}.png`,\r\n  117:   with a `stills_manifest.json` beside them (`nodes/_otr_shared/portrait_ledger.py:63-108`,\r\n  118:   `otr_image_gen_dispatcher.py:2099-2139`). The replay bundle freezes `stills/` and `portraits/`\r\n  119:   whole (`scripts/otr_freeze_replay_bundle.py`, FROZEN_DIRS).\r\n  120: * A planned shot carries `ghost_prompt` (with `mode` in figure / object / signal and the bookend),\r\n  121:   `beat_id`, `role`, `char_id`, `subject_sigil` -- no still path (`otr_shot_lock.py:1662-1690`).\r\n  122:   `GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE = ('figure','object','figure','signal')` (`ghost_signal_author.py:101,\r\n  123:   472-505`).\r\n  124: * Packs: nine files under `nodes/visual_styles/` (+ the run-time `visual_storybased`); prompt\r\n  125:   LANGUAGE only -- `positive_tail`, `image_grade_tail`, `broadcast_tail`, `era_tail`,\r\n  126:   `negative_tail`, eleven look strings incl. `plate_look`, `scene_instruction_look`,\r\n  127:   `portrait_look`, four dicts (`_otr_visual_styles.py:559-618`; PROBLEM_STATEMENT 154-162).\r\n  128:   The video cue is `compact_style_cue()` = two words (`_otr_visual_styles.py:632-653`); the leaf\r\n  129:   validator bans medium/camera/quality words -- \"style is Python's job on this lane\"\r\n  130:   (`ghost_signal_author.py:316-330`); the finalizer enforces one 77-token window and 320 chars on\r\n  131:   BOTH prompts (`:1348-1367`).\r\n  132: \r\n  133: ## 3. Decisions (the driver's position; the panel pressure-tests these)\r\n  134: \r\n  135: **D1. A new lane, id `animatediff15_v3_stillin_lab_video`, menu label \"AnimateDiff v3 still-in lab\r\n  136: (512x288)\", recipe receipt `animatediff_sd15_v3_haunted_stillin_e1_512x288_lab_v1`.** Subclass of\r\n  137: `GhostSignalV3HauntedEngine`; every G1.3 constant a class attribute it inherits or overrides;\r\n  138: `status: lab` in its profile; 5080 only until measured. The word \"lab\" is in the id on purpose:\r\n  139: it is never a default, never in a shipping profile, and its receipt id can never be mistaken for\r\n  140: the shipping one.\r\n  141: \r\n  142: **D2. The plate is minted IN-GRAPH by the peer, not taken from the image phase.** Consequences,\r\n  143: all deliberate: `accepts_still` stays `False`, `required_inputs` stays `('text_prompt',)`,\r\n  144: `still_plan` stays `()`, `subject_ownership` stays `'prompt'`, the id stays OUT of any dispatcher\r\n  145: minting and is added to `_NO_STILL_VIDEO_ENGINES` alongside its parent so the 4060 is never gated\r\n  146: on an image bundle by it. The plate is a recipe cell of the VIDEO lane, hashed by its receipt, not\r\n  147: an asset of the image phase. The dispatcher-fed still (portrait-derived `scene_character` at\r\n  148: 1472x832 through `resolve_aspect_transform`, `motion_common.py:135-166`) is arm E1b, a LATER knob\r\n  149: on the same peer, opened only after E1a is judged; it is the one that would need\r\n  150: `accepts_still=True` and its own provisioning exclusion, and it is not built now.\r\n  151: \r\n  152: **D3. Plate prompt = the pack's language, the ledger's world, never the leaf.** Positive:\r\n  153: `<positive_tail>. <plate_look or scene_instruction_look>. <setting phrase from the ledger:\r\n  154: place, era, palette, lighting>. <mode subject>` measured by `measure_clip_tokens` to <= 77 with\r\n  155: the pack clauses first (they are the point) and the ledger phrase trimmed last; negative = the\r\n  156: composed lane negative (`LANE_HYGIENE_NEGATIVE` + pack `effective_negative`). NO character name,\r\n  157: no dialogue, no camera words, no lettering: the plate owns MEDIUM and WORLD; motion and framing\r\n  158: stay with the video prompt (mode law) exactly as today. The plate branch runs with the adapter at\r\n  159: 0.0 (plain checkpoint MODEL): the plate is the pristine image-domain picture of the style, the\r\n  160: adapter then haunts the motion on top of it.\r\n  161: \r\n  162: **D4. Mode routing by `ghost_prompt.mode`, not a frozen cycle.** The plan's one-liner (\"cycle\r\n  163: frozen to figure\") predates the r2/r3 folds that preferred routing; the plate makes routing free:\r\n  164: figure -> a figure plate (a person of the character's described build in the world, no name),\r\n  165: object -> the object with no person, signal -> the environment with no person, bookend ->\r\n  166: `scene_open`. Freezing the cycle would change the shipping author's schedule for the peer only and\r\n  167: hide a variable; routing keeps the schedule identical between the two lanes so the A/B isolates\r\n  168: the still. GO_FORWARD_PLAN 1.14's one-liner is corrected in this change.\r\n  169: \r\n  170: **D5. E1 mechanics, stock nodes only.** STAGE 3 gains, before the sampler:\r\n  171: `plate_latent = EmptyLatentImage{512x288, 1}` -> `plate_sampler = KSampler{model: Wire('base_model'),\r\n  172: seed: plate_seed, steps 20, cfg 8.0, euler, normal, denoise 1.0, positive: plate_positive, negative:\r\n  173: negative}` -> `plate_decode = VAEDecode{vae}` (the plate PNG is written to\r\n  174: `<episode>/stills/plate_<shot_id>_<sha12>.png` for the record, section D7) -> `plate_encode =\r\n  175: VAEEncode{pixels, vae}` -> `repeat = RepeatLatentBatch{amount: min(U, 64)}` (+ a second\r\n  176: `RepeatLatentBatch` and `LatentFromBatch{0, U}` when `U > 64`; U = the lane's `source_request`) ->\r\n  177: `sampler.latent_image`, with `sampler.denoise = 0.75` (class attribute `E1_DENOISE`, sweep 0.6 /\r\n  178: 0.75 / 0.9 later; 0.9 only if motion is damped). The plate prompt is a THIRD CLIPTextEncode in\r\n  179: STAGE 2 before the CLIP is released. Node candidates: the seven shipping classes + `VAEEncode`,\r\n  180: `RepeatLatentBatch`, `LatentFromBatch` (one name per alias, pinned from a fresh live capture).\r\n  181: Resize policy: none needed -- the plate is minted at the canvas. `ADE_UpscaleAndVAEEncode` and\r\n  182: `VHS_DuplicateLatents` are rejected: the first is an ADE-private helper for a foreign-size still\r\n  183: (E1b's problem, not E1a's), the second is an unprovisioned dependency.\r\n  184: \r\n  185: **D6. The seed.** `plate_seed = (request_seed * 1000003 + 0x5EED) & 0x7FFFFFFF` -- derived, so a\r\n  186: same-ledger replay reproduces the plate by construction and a changed request seed changes it; the\r\n  187: video sampler keeps `request_seed` untouched so the shipping lane and the peer share the video\r\n  188: seed on the same ledger (the A/B isolates the init latent).\r\n  189: \r\n  190: **D7. Reproducibility and the record.** The plate is deterministic from (checkpoint, plate prompt,\r\n  191: plate seed, canvas, steps/cfg/sampler) on one box, and the peer ALSO writes it to\r\n  192: `<episode>/stills/plate_<shot_id>_<sha12>.png` and records `plate_sha256` on the clip -- so the\r\n  193: replay bundle carries the exact frame, `otr_verify_replay.py` can compare plate hashes across the\r\n  194: A/A, and a bitwise-unstable GPU kernel shows up as a plate-hash disagreement rather than as an\r\n  195: unexplained video difference. The peer does not READ the file back on a replay in the first build\r\n  196: (the plate is re-minted; the hash is the check) -- reading it back is a second knob if the null\r\n  197: proves noisy.\r\n  198: \r\n  199: **D8. The receipt (the r4 pin, applied to the peer).** `sampler_inputs_for` returns the parent's\r\n  200: dict plus `latent: 'VAEEncode'`, `init_image: <plate_sha256>`, `denoise: E1_DENOISE`, `plate_seed`,\r\n  201: `plate_prompt`, `plate_negative`, `plate_steps/cfg/sampler/scheduler`, `plate_adapter_strength: 0.0`,\r\n  202: `repeat_amount: U`; `model_artifacts` is the parent's (no new weight). Two A/A replays agree by\r\n  203: construction (every field derives from the ledger + seed); a changed plate prompt, denoise, seed\r\n  204: or canvas disagrees. `session_identity` / `shot_cache_identity` gain the plate sha so a cache hit\r\n  205: can never hand a still-free render to the peer.\r\n  206: \r\n  207: **D9. The proof.** All legs on the canonical graph, published to `otr/obs/`, clean runner, no\r\n  208: harness title (the 17:45 lesson), engine selected per role by a `lab` profile\r\n  209: `otr_stillin_lab_5080.json` (copy of the haunted profile with the three video roles on the peer,\r\n  210: `status: lab`, variants regenerated). Sequence:\r\n  211: 1. G8 solo smoke on one beat: canvas probed 512x288, frame count exact, silence probed, VRAM peak\r\n  212:    receipted (the plate branch adds one 512x288 single-frame sample and one VAE encode; expected\r\n  213:    well under the lane's 13.3-14.2 GB peak -- MEASURED, not assumed).\r\n  214: 2. Same-ledger A/B per style via the replay mode: freeze one episode rendered on the SHIPPING lane\r\n  215:    (anime), replay it on the peer; repeat for `storybook_engraving` and `paper_origami` (three\r\n  216:    styles, two non-anime). Each pair carries its A/A null (the peer replayed twice).\r\n  217: 3. Verdict, in the operator's words: \"looks like <the selected style>\", stills and video the same\r\n  218:    show, name / gender / portrait / voice agreeing -- judged as radio drama, by his eye, with the\r\n  219:    render trace to attribute any difference.\r\n  220: Stop the item if the null is not a null (plate hashes or seeds differ across two replays), or if\r\n  221: E1 at 0.75 damps motion visibly on all three styles (then 0.9 is tried once; a second failure ends\r\n  222: E1 and opens E2's probe).\r\n  223: \r\n  224: **D10. Not in this item.** E2 (image injection) and its VRAM probe; E1b (dispatcher-fed still);\r\n  225: E7 (SparseCtrl, pack not installed); per-style checkpoints; any change to the shipping engine,\r\n  226: its graph, tests, receipt, or the 4060 provisioning set; any prompt change on the video prompt\r\n  227: (that is item 3).\r\n  228: \r\n  229: ## 4. The build list (what one window codes after r4)\r\n  230: \r\n  231: 1. `nodes/_otr_video_engines/eng_ghost_signal_stillin_lab.py`: `GhostSignalV3StillInLabEngine(\r\n  232:    GhostSignalV3HauntedEngine)` with `name`, `recipe_receipt_id`, `E1_DENOISE = 0.75`,\r\n  233:    `PLATE_*` class constants, `_node_candidates()` (ten names), `render_clip()` (STAGE 2 with the\r\n  234:    plate prompt, STAGE 3 with the plate branch), `_plate_prompt(request, vstyle, ledger_world)`,\r\n  235:    `_plate_seed(request_seed)`, `sampler_inputs_for`, `session_identity`, `shot_cache_identity`,\r\n  236:    `canonicalize` adding `plate_sha256` to the clip (schemas.py: one new optional key, since\r\n  237:    `CanonicalClip` is `extra='forbid'`).\r\n  238: 2. `registry.py` CAPABILITIES row (copy of the haunted row, `status: lab`); `__init__.py` guarded\r\n  239:    import; `scripts/otr_provision.py` `_ANIMATEDIFF_ENGINES` gains the id (so it stays no-still on\r\n  240:    the 4060); `scripts/build_video_evidence_manifest.py` sentence + regenerate;\r\n  241:    `tools/engine_matrix.py` regenerate `docs/ENGINE_MATRIX.md`.\r\n  242: 3. `config/profiles/otr_stillin_lab_5080.json` + `scripts/build_variants.py --all/--check`.\r\n  243: 4. Tests: `tests/test_ghost_signal_stillin_lab.py` (canvas pin, node candidates, exactly N node\r\n  244:    instances, `sampler_inputs` pins, plate-seed derivation, plate-prompt budget <= 77 on all nine\r\n  245:    packs, no name / camera / lettering word in any plate prompt, `session_identity` moves with the\r\n  246:    plate sha); `test_render_receipts.py` gains the \"plate changes the sha\" case; the preflight\r\n  247:    matrix and `test_ghost_signal_peers.py` floors run green.\r\n  248: 5. A `docs/evidence/lane_receipts/animatediff15_v3_stillin_lab_video.md` G8 receipt after the smoke.\r\n  249: \r\n  250: ## 5. Open questions for the panel (r1)\r\n  251: \r\n  252: * Q1. Is the in-graph plate (D2) the right FIRST build, or should E1b (dispatcher still) go first\r\n  253:   because it reuses the existing still spine? The driver says D2: it isolates the style variable,\r\n  254:   needs no image model, cannot gate the 4060, and cannot pull a foreign-family look into the lane.\r\n  255: * Q2. The plate prompt's ledger phrase: is \"setting, era, palette, lighting\" the right slice of the\r\n  256:   ledger, and which existing composer (the still lane's scene prompt builder in\r\n  257:   `otr_meta_brief_image_prompt.py`) should the peer borrow from rather than write new prose?\r\n  258: * Q3. `denoise 0.75` first -- or 0.6, given the adapter at 1.0 already pulls toward its domain?\r\n  259: * Q4. Should the plate be minted at the adapter's LIVE strength instead of 0.0 (the plate would\r\n  260:   already carry the haunt)? The driver says 0.0: the plate is the style's own picture; the adapter\r\n  261:   is a motion-domain knob and the sweep is measuring it separately.\r\n  262: * Q5. Mode routing (D4) versus the plan's frozen cycle -- any reason routing hides a variable?\r\n  263: * Q6. Anything in `render_driver`'s coverage / jump-still / segment path that assumes an\r\n  264:   EmptyLatentImage lane and would break a subclass that repeats latents (multi-segment beats,\r\n  265:   `U > 64`)?\r\n  266: \r\n  267: ## 6. r1 fold (Fable 5.1 cold read + Antigravity `agy` Gemini 3.8 Flash (High); judgment in `kibitz-runs/2026-09-02-still-in-peer/r1/judgment.md`)\r\n  268: \r\n  269: The two reviews split on the still source. Antigravity: reverse D2 to the dispatcher still\r\n  270: (identity continuity, unity with the episode's portraits; \"the 4060 justification contradicts\r\n  271: D1\"). Fable: keep the in-graph plate (on an all-Ghost policy the dispatcher mints nothing, so\r\n  272: \"reuse the still\" is a new image phase; at denoise 0.75 SD1.5 repaints the medium and an init\r\n  273: survives as layout, palette, lighting). What decided it is a document neither the anchor nor\r\n  274: the 09-02 judgment had folded: **`docs/SPEC_haunted_image_to_video.md` (2026-08-30) already\r\n  275: took this lane through r1 (Antigravity) and r2 (Codex)** and closed on two findings, both\r\n  276: re-confirmed at the file today:\r\n  277: \r\n  278: 1. **Route A -- a still VAE-encoded and repeated as the init latent -- plausibly fails BY\r\n  279:    CONSTRUCTION**: identical cross-frame keys in the motion module's temporal attention\r\n  280:    suppress the trajectory while high-frequency texture boils. So Route A is a PROBE, not a\r\n  281:    build: denoise 0.35 / 0.50 / 0.65 / 0.80, disqualified if <= 0.50 shows no macro-motion with\r\n  282:    texture boil, or >= 0.65 loses the still within 2-3 frames. The mechanism is the same\r\n  283:    whichever still is fed, which makes the still SOURCE a secondary question for the probe.\r\n  284: 2. **Upstream recommends the animated image come from the SAME SD1.5 model used for\r\n  285:    animation**; a Z-Image still into a haunted SD1.5 checkpoint invites cross-model drift\r\n  286:    (spec 11.5). This is the in-family plate's strongest reason, and it replaces the 4060\r\n  287:    reasoning the anchor gave.\r\n  288: \r\n  289: The 08-30 spec's own recommendation (\"measure the image phase on the 4060 first, qualify\r\n  290: Advanced-ControlNet, build SparseCtrl\") is superseded by the 09-02 campaign order, which\r\n  291: deferred SparseCtrl (E7: a new pack pin, 1.99 GB, 323 MB of overnight headroom) and put the\r\n  292: still-in probe here. Its Route A probe grid and disqualification rule are adopted verbatim.\r\n  293: CLAUDE.md 0A forbids a standalone stock-node probe graph, so the probe VEHICLE is the lab\r\n  294: peer itself rendering canonical episodes with a denoise knob, published to `otr/obs/`.\r\n  295: \r\n  296: ## 7. Revised decisions after r1 (what r2 plans the code for)\r\n  297: \r\n  298: **D1 (id, receipt, lab status) -- unchanged.** `animatediff15_v3_stillin_lab_video`,\r\n  299: recipe `animatediff_sd15_v3_haunted_stillin_e1_512x288_lab_v1`, `status: lab`, 5080 only,\r\n  300: never a default, never a tombstoned id (`public_engines.py:398-402`).\r\n  301: \r\n  302: **D2 (in-family plate) -- STANDS, reasoning replaced.** The plate is minted in-graph from the\r\n  303: same checkpoint because (a) upstream says the animated image should come from the animating\r\n  304: model, (b) it needs no image phase (on 8 GB the image role is a native abort,\r\n  305: PBUG-20260829-03; on the 5080 it is a cost the probe does not need to pay), and (c) it is the\r\n  306: cheapest vehicle for the Route A probe, whose outcome is the same for either still source.\r\n  307: `accepts_still` stays `False`, `required_inputs` `('text_prompt',)`, `still_plan` `()`,\r\n  308: `subject_ownership` `'prompt'`, `prompt_profile` stays `GHOST_PROMPT_PROFILE` (or ShotLock\r\n  309: stamps no `ghost_prompt` and render falls to v1, `otr_shot_lock.py:2137, 2246-2247`); the id\r\n  310: joins `_ANIMATEDIFF_ENGINES` (`otr_provision.py:1516-1522`) so it stays in the no-still set.\r\n  311: **E1b (dispatcher still, `accepts_still=True`, the 08-30 spec's contract corrected to\r\n  312: `required_inputs=('text_prompt','init_image')` and a truthful non-empty `still_plan`) is the\r\n  313: SECOND engine id, opened only if Route A survives the probe.**\r\n  314: \r\n  315: **D3 (plate prompt) -- replaced by the plate-prompt composer, modelled on\r\n  316: `_compose_background_plate_prompt` (`otr_meta_brief_image_prompt.py:1839-1865`).** Protected\r\n  317: head, in order: the FULL `positive_tail` (never `compact_style_cue` / `prefix_style_cue`,\r\n  318: `_otr_visual_styles.py:632-685` -- that IS the defect), `plate_look`, `_read_setting` top-2\r\n  319: (`:40-49`), `get_era_tail(profile=\"still\")` (`_otr_story_brief_helpers.py:321-341`, capped\r\n  320: 120 chars). Droppable in fixed order: `era_tail`, `image_grade_tail`, atmosphere terms, the\r\n  321: second setting term. NEVER: `scene_instruction_look` (an instruction sentence),\r\n  322: `broadcast_tail`, any `motion_registers` entry (camera words; storybook's carry \"gently\" --\r\n  323: a damping word), `open_subjects` / `announcer_*` / `portrait_*` (subject, identity), the leaf,\r\n  324: the mode law, `NO_TEXT_CLAUSE`. Negative: the request's own composed Ghost negative\r\n  325: (`ghost_signal_prompt.py:622-651`), verbatim. Budget: COUNTED with `measure_clip_tokens`\r\n  326: (`ghost_signal_author.py:1234-1249`), `GhostBudgetError` if the protected head exceeds 69,\r\n  327: pinned by a test over all nine packs plus a synthetic long `visual_storybased`. **The\r\n  328: composition lives in `render_driver`'s Ghost branch (where `_vstyle` and ledger meta exist,\r\n  329: `:2881-2950`) as a declared optional `VideoRequest` field `plate_prompt`** -- the engine has\r\n  330: no ledger, `sampler_inputs_for(request)` is pure over the request, and `observability` is\r\n  331: never conditioning (`eng_ghost_signal_official.py:144-149`); this is the schema design item\r\n  332: that file names, taken deliberately.\r\n  333: \r\n  334: **D4 (mode routing) -- DROPPED: one subject-free scene plate per beat, plan untouched.**\r\n  335: `mode` is planned by ShotLock and reused on replay; an engine-side override would make the\r\n  336: rendered prompt differ from the planned row and break the same-prompt A/B. The plate carries\r\n  337: WORLD and MEDIUM only; the figure keeps coming from motif + leaf + law exactly as today\r\n  338: (`ghost_signal_prompt.py:863-867`), so Antigravity's \"random face per beat\" cannot occur --\r\n  339: there is no face in the plate. Figure plates are knob two. GO_FORWARD_PLAN 1.14's \"cycle\r\n  340: frozen to figure\" is corrected to this in the same change.\r\n  341: \r\n  342: **D5 (E1 mechanics) -- latent-direct, stock only, zero new node classes.** STAGE 2 encodes\r\n  343: the plate prompt as a third CLIPTextEncode before the CLIP is released. STAGE 3a (a bounded\r\n  344: `run_graph`): `plate_latent = EmptyLatentImage{512x288, 1}` -> `plate_sampler = KSampler{model:\r\n  345: Wire('base_model'), seed: request_seed, steps 20, cfg 8.0, euler, normal, denoise 1.0,\r\n  346: plate_cond, negative_cond}` -> the LATENT object returned in-process. Python:\r\n  347: `init = samples.repeat(U, 1, 1, 1)` with `RepeatLatentBatch`'s `batch_index` rule\r\n  348: (`ComfyUI/nodes.py:1314-1330`), U = `source_request`; no cap, fixed topology. STAGE 3b: the\r\n  349: shipping sampler graph with `latent_image` = the repeated latent as an `external_results`\r\n  350: entry and `denoise = E1_DENOISE`. STAGE 4: decode the beat as today; decode the PLATE once,\r\n  351: off the critical path, for the PNG. No `VAEEncode`, no `RepeatLatentBatch`, no\r\n  352: `VHS_DuplicateLatents` (installed, unprovisioned), no `ADE_UpscaleAndVAEEncode`. The seven-class\r\n  353: object_info capture stands; the peer gets its own instance-count pin (the haunted lane pins 8,\r\n  354: `tests/test_ghost_signal_haunted.py:165-170`).\r\n  355: \r\n  356: **D6 (seed) -- the plate uses `request_seed` itself.** Nothing new on the request; the plate is\r\n  357: a pure function of (checkpoint, plate prompt, seed, canvas, plate steps/cfg/sampler).\r\n  358: \r\n  359: **D7 (record and reuse).** The plate PNG is written to\r\n  360: `<episode>/stills/ghost_plates/<shot_id>_<sha8 of plate INPUTS>.png`; the replay bundle\r\n  361: already freezes `stills/` whole, so a replay REUSES a present sha-named plate (encoding it\r\n  362: instead of re-sampling) and the A/A is exact rather than exact-up-to-GPU-noise. Never a\r\n  363: fabricated dispatcher `images[]` row (those carry `content_hash` / `pool_path` / `provenance`\r\n  364: and node 91 verifies them). Verify at build that node 91 tolerates the unlisted file.\r\n  365: \r\n  366: **D8 (receipt) -- inputs only in the causal hash.** `sampler_inputs_for` returns the parent's\r\n  367: dict with `latent: 'ghost_plate_init'`, `init_image: None` (kept -- `accepts_still` is False),\r\n  368: `denoise: E1_DENOISE` (overriding the parent key), `plate_prompt` (full text), `plate_seed`,\r\n  369: `plate_steps / cfg / sampler / scheduler`, `plate_adapter_strength: 0.0`, `plate_canvas`,\r\n  370: `init_repeat_method: 'torch_repeat'`, `init_repeat_count: U`. NEVER the plate's rendered\r\n  371: sha, a path, a timestamp or minted/reused -- an OUTPUT in the causal hash makes the A/A false\r\n  372: by construction the first time SD1.5 is not bit-stable. `plate_sha256` rides beside the hash\r\n  373: like `vram_peak_mb` and in `CanonicalClip.qc` (`schemas.py:244`); no schema key on the clip.\r\n  374: `shot_cache_identity` gains plate-inputs sha8 + denoise. `E1_DENOISE` is a class attribute\r\n  375: with an env override read inside a method through the guarded parser (like `lora_strength`,\r\n  376: `eng_ghost_signal_official.py:158-174`), stamped on the receipt.\r\n  377: \r\n  378: **D9 (the proof) -- the 08-30 probe, run through the canonical graph.**\r\n  379: 1. G8 solo smoke (one beat): canvas probed, frame count exact, silence probed, VRAM peak\r\n  380:    receipted; split STAGE 3a/3b further if the peak exceeds 14.0 GB (Antigravity S2).\r\n  381: 2. Per style (anime, storybook_engraving, paper_origami): one canonical episode rendered on the\r\n  382:    lab profile at denoise 0.65 becomes the frozen source; replays: peer x2 at 0.65 (its OWN\r\n  383:    null -- the plate adds sampler noise), peer x1 each at 0.35 / 0.50 / 0.80, and the SAME\r\n  384:    bundle rendered on the SHIPPING lane via the replay engine override (D11) as the baseline.\r\n  385:    Every leg publishes to `otr/obs/`, clean runner, no harness title; `otr_verify_replay.py`\r\n  386:    on every pair.\r\n  387: 3. Measurement beside the eye: per-beat mean inter-frame difference (motion energy) for  390:    energy stays inside the null band; below the band is damping.\r\n  391: 4. Stop rules, in order: the plate PNGs do not read as the style (SD1.5 base with the full pack\r\n  392:    language is E1's ceiling; the next arm is E11, per-style checkpoints); no denoise both\r\n  393:    moves and keeps the plate (the 08-30 disqualification; go to the E2 probe); peak over\r\n  394:    14.5 GB (a 64x36 plate cannot do that; it would mean a patcher held twice).\r\n  395: \r\n  396: **D10 (not in this item) -- unchanged**, plus: no figure plates, no E1b, no ADE upgrade, no\r\n  397: Advanced-ControlNet.\r\n  398: \r\n  399: **D11 (NEW, on the instrument): a replay-time engine override.** The reused plan's shot rows\r\n  400: carry `engine_id`, so a replay renders on the FROZEN engine and a same-ledger cross-engine A/B\r\n  401: is impossible today. `otr_canonical_api_run.py --replay-engine <id>` (plus the writer's\r\n  402: `replay_from` path) re-stamps `engine_id` on every reused shot row for the roles the\r\n  403: override names, records `meta.replay_engine_override`, and leaves prompts, seeds, beats and\r\n  404: the frozen audio untouched. Default (no flag) stays the pure A/A. r2 designs where the\r\n  405: re-stamp lives (ShotLock's replay reuse branch is the natural seat) and how ShotLock's\r\n  406: per-engine prompt policy is re-applied without re-authoring.\r\n  407: \r\n  408: ## 8. What r2 (coding plan, Codex) must settle\r\n  409: \r\n  410: * The `VideoRequest.plate_prompt` field: optional str, default \"\", composed only for engines\r\n  411:   that declare `wants_plate_prompt = True`; where in `render_driver`'s Ghost branch; the\r\n  412:   budget test's fixture set.\r\n  413: * STAGE 3a/3b split: `run_graph` return shape for the LATENT, the `batch_index` rule, patcher\r\n  414:   ownership across the two calls (the base MODEL patcher is registered in `prepare`; the plate\r\n  415:   sampler must not detach it).\r\n  416: * D11's seat and its receipt; whether the override is per role or whole-plan.\r\n  417: * The instance-count pin and the `_node_candidates` map (no new classes; the map is the\r\n  418:   parent's).\r\n  419: * Tests: plate-prompt budget over ten packs; plate-prompt word bans (no name / camera /\r\n  420:   lettering / damping word); `sampler_inputs` pins; cache identity moves with plate inputs;\r\n  421:   receipts test \"plate inputs change the sha, plate output does not\".\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'CLAUDE.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"    1: # OTR -- Project Operating Rules (Claude in Cowork)\r\n    2: **Operator directives -- these win over any handoff, doc, or memory that disagrees.**\r\n    3: - **Fix bugs properly, at the root cause -- never a shim or band-aid.** Don't wait for me to\r\n    4:   fix anything: make the fix yourself, without asking. If it works, it's fine.\r\n    5: - **NO CONTENT GUARDRAILS ON GENERATED EPISODES (operator directive 2026-08-03 -- supersedes\r\n    6:   the older \"no curse words anywhere / SFW always\" line for EPISODE CONTENT).** Operator:\r\n    7:   \"we [have] too [many] guardrails, no violence or swearing guardrails, they just cause\r\n    8:   problems\" and \"I've given up chasing profanity.\" Do not add profanity or violence filtering\r\n    9:   to the generation path, and do not reintroduce prompt clauses that forbid the source's own\r\n   10:   content -- the adaptation packs were literally instructing the model to avoid \"blood, guns,\r\n   11:   knives, and graphic violence\" while adapting MACBETH and KING LEAR, which is a fidelity\r\n   12:   defect, not a safety win. On the adaptation lanes the author's own language is carried as\r\n   13:   written; Wells' Editor may shout \"Story be damned!\" because Wells wrote it.\r\n   14:   **Still true, and a different thing:** authoring style stays clean -- no curse words in\r\n   15:   CODE, COMMENTS, LOGS or commit messages, and never the name \"dummy\". That rule is about what\r\n   16:   WE write, not about what the pipeline generates from a source.\r\n   17: - **STORY QUALITY IS DONE. STOP CHASING IT (operator directive 2026-08-04 -- hard).**\r\n   18:   Operator: \"I am not chasing story quality anymore. It works. It works. I will publish it\r\n   19:   as open source, and if someone else wants to do it, or in six months I wanna chase it\r\n   20:   again when I've got better tools, I will.\" The scripts are ACCEPTED as they are. Do not\r\n   21:   open writing-quality work, do not propose prompt-craft passes for better prose, do not\r\n   22:   benchmark writer models for story quality, and do not spend on panels or cloud models to\r\n   23:   improve scripts. This also settles the cloud-writer question: local stays the default and\r\n   24:   no paid writer is adopted to raise prose quality.\r\n   25:   **What this does NOT cover** -- these are CORRECTNESS defects, not quality chasing, and\r\n   26:   remain open: a character's gender/voice contradicting the source, a character's face\r\n   27:   changing between beats, voice-pool staleness, and any structural or ledger fault. Fixing\r\n   28:   \"Malvolio speaks with a woman's voice\" is a bug fix; rewriting Malvolio's dialogue to be\r\n   29:   better is not.\r\n   30: - **NO WORD-COUNT CHASING (operator directive 2026-08-03).** \"We never chase word count.\"\r\n   31:   The target words value is a REQUEST, not a gate: no refusals, no hard caps, no shunts. The\r\n   32:   manifest `recommended_word_budget` upper bound is removed for exactly this reason. The only\r\n   33:   real ceiling is structural (the beat topology tops out near 1,520 spoken words), and a\r\n   34:   request beyond it simply delivers the closest performable episode.\r\n   35: - **EVERY CODING ITEM GETS A FULL KIBITZ (operator directive 2026-08-04 -- hard, stands until\r\n   36:   the operator withdraws it).** Operator: \"for the next coding sprint be sure any coding work\r\n   37:   has a full `/kibitz-plugin:kibitz` review.\" **FULL means the default four-round arc** --\r\n   38:   r1 arc -> r2 coding -> r3 wiring -> r4 convergence, 8 external calls (two reviewers x four\r\n   39:   rounds). Not a scoped tail, not one round, not a continuation receipt; a partial campaign\r\n   40:   may never be reported as a full arc. **Invoke the PLUGIN skill by name:\r\n   41:   `kibitz-plugin:kibitz`** -- `anthropic-skills:kibitz` is the older duplicate and is not what\r\n   42:   the operator asked for. Panel is driver-aware: Claude drives from Cowork, so **Codex +\r\n   43:   Antigravity** review and you do NOT launch a second `claude -p` lane against your own family.\r\n   44:   Use the ComfyUI profile overlay already in the repo, `.kibitz/comfyui.local.md` (written\r\n   45:   2026-07-11 -- regenerate with `kibitz/scripts/comfyui_profile.py` if the tree has moved past\r\n   46:   it). You still write the code-grounded `driver_anchor.md` FIRST and remain the sole judge:\r\n   47:   the panel proposes, Claude disposes, and every panel claim is checked against the real\r\n   48:   Windows files before it is folded in. This RAISES the two-strikes rule below -- a first-try\r\n   49:   root fix now gets a panel too. It is local, it is $0, and it costs a wait, not a budget.\r\n   50: - **AMENDED 2026-08-17 (operator): MATCH THE REVIEW TO THE TASK -- a full arc is not the\r\n   51:   answer to everything.** Operator: *\"we aren't running a full kibitz on everything, it needs\r\n   52:   to choose the best path for the right task.\"* The 08-04 directive above stands for what it\r\n   53:   was aimed at; it was being applied to work that has no design in it, which wastes a wait and\r\n   54:   teaches nobody anything.\r\n   55:   **THE TEST, and it is one question: is there a design choice with more than one defensible\r\n   56:   answer?**\r\n   57:   * **YES -- new capability, architecture, a schema or ledger change, anything touching the\r\n   58:     canonical workflow, anything where reasonable people would disagree** -> FULL four-round\r\n   59:     arc BEFORE code, exactly as above. This is where the panel earns its keep: on the\r\n   60:     2026-08-17 style build the panel killed a build-breaker (a required pack key would have\r\n   61:     bricked every frozen embedded pack via its sha256 receipt), deleted the riskiest mechanism\r\n   62:     outright, and corrected the driver's own execution-order claim.\r\n   63:   * **NO -- one verifiable right answer: wiring conformance, a grep-and-fix, a stale comment,\r\n   64:     a rename, an `/object_info` check, a deterministic edit** -> NO arc. An arc pressure-tests\r\n   65:     DESIGN; there is nothing to pressure-test. Sonnet 5 QA on the finished diff before the\r\n   66:     push is the correct and sufficient gate.\r\n   67:   * **ONE CLEAN FINISHED-DIFF REVIEW IS ENOUGH (operator directive 2026-08-20).** Run one\r\n   68:     independent reviewer after code. If it returns clean and the driver grounds that result\r\n   69:     against the real files, STOP reviewing and move to tests/live proof. Add another reviewer\r\n   70:     only when the first reports a blocker, two reviewers disagree, a material claim remains\r\n   71:     unverifiable, or the two-strikes rule below fires. An available internal subagent is a\r\n   72:     valid reviewer; do not wait on or multiply CLI/cloud lanes merely to increase the count.\r\n   73:     Save the real roster and result in the handoff receipt.\r\n   74:   * **UNSURE** -> treat it as YES. The arc is $0 and a missed design flaw is not.\r\n   75: - **`otr/obs/` IS THE SUCCESS SIGNAL. ALWAYS PUBLISH TO IT (operator directive 2026-08-17 --\r\n   76:   hard, and it OVERRIDES the tidiness instinct).** Operator, in his words: *\"always publish to\r\n   77:   obs -- a test is not complete unless published to obs (or it's just testing one part). If I\r\n   78:   see it in obs then it's somewhat a success.\"* And the failure half: *\"if I don't see it in\r\n   79:   obs and it took more than 5 minutes, it's a fail.\"*\r\n   80:   * **A leg that does not reach `otr/obs/` did not pass**, however green its logs are. The\r\n   81:     published artifact IS the receipt -- that is how HE reads success without reading a log.\r\n   82:   * **NEVER move, hide, sort or clean harness runs out of `otr/obs/`.** Soak, banksweep,\r\n   83:     probe, acceptance and bank-gate legs BELONG there; seeing them is the point. **This rule\r\n   84:     exists because I moved 17 of them into a `_diagnostics/` subfolder the same day, reading\r\n   85:     them as pollution. They were his proof that the full path worked. Restored within\r\n   86:     minutes; nothing was deleted.** The narrow, real complaint was only that the harness RUN\r\n   87:     LABEL becomes the on-screen TITLE CARD -- cosmetic, and not a reason to touch the folder.\r\n   88:   * **The `_bench_4arm` carve-out is NOT a precedent for this.** That exemption is scoped to\r\n   89:     isolated stock-node BENCH graphs that never run the canonical workflow. A soak or bank-gate\r\n   90:     leg DOES run the real canonical path end to end, which is exactly why its publication\r\n   91:     proves something.\r\n   92:   * **The 5-minute rule is a real gate:** if a leg has run longer than 5 minutes with nothing\r\n   93:     in `otr/obs/`, treat it as failing and go read the leg log rather than waiting it out.\r\n   94:   * **THE PRIORITY ORDER, in his words:** *\"long term yeah I want the title right, but for my\r\n   95:     dailies it keeps me going to see episodes.\"* So the title-card label IS a real want and it\r\n   96:     is a LONG-TERM one; the daily stream of published episodes is the thing that must never be\r\n   97:     interrupted to get it. **Fix the title at the source (stop reusing `episode_title` as the\r\n   98:     harness's scratch field) -- never by suppressing, relocating or gating the publish.** If a\r\n   99:     proposed fix would reduce how many episodes he sees in `otr/obs/` tomorrow, it is the wrong\r\n  100:     fix no matter how tidy it is.\r\n  101: - **A MISSING REVIEWER NEVER BLOCKS THE ARC -- SUBSTITUTE AND KEEP GOING (operator directive\r\n  102:   2026-08-17 -- hard).** Operator: *\"if out of budget then a kibitz reviewer is not needed,\r\n  103:   ask Fable, Sonnet, or Opus, or `/anthropic-skills:roundtable` -- ANY model in lieu.\"*\r\n  104:   **A quota-held, timed-out, or unavailable lane is NOT a reason to stop, to defer the item,\r\n  105:   or to park a finished diff waiting for a lane to come back.** Fill the seat and run the\r\n  106:   round.\r\n  107:   * **Eligible substitutes, freely interchangeable:** Fable, Sonnet, Opus (as Cowork\r\n  108:     subagents), the other `agy` model lanes (Gemini 3.1 Pro / 3.7 Flash are different\r\n  109:     reviewers, not one), and `/anthropic-skills:roundtable` for a cloud panel. Any model in\r\n  110:     lieu of any other.\r\n  111:   * **WHAT STILL DOES NOT CHANGE:** you write the code-grounded `driver_anchor.md` FIRST and\r\n  112:     you remain the sole judge; every panel claim is verified against the real Windows files\r\n  113:     before it is folded in; and **the PROVENANCE is stated exactly** -- name which lanes\r\n  114:     actually reviewed which rounds. A campaign a reviewer short is described as a campaign a\r\n  115:     reviewer short, never as a full arc. Substituting is honest; misreporting the roster is\r\n  116:     not.\r\n  117:   * **This was written because it cost real time on 2026-08-17 (item F).** Codex was\r\n  118:     quota-held to 08-19 and the driver treated that as a hard gate on the whole item --\r\n  119:     \"F is not secretly cheap ... it wants the whole arc once Codex is back\". The operator's\r\n  120:     correction: run it with who is available. Item F then ran r1-r4 on two agy lanes plus a\r\n  121:     Fable gate and a Sonnet QA pass, caught three driver errors (two of them build-breakers:\r\n  122:     an `UnboundLocalError` on four of six banks, and a lane collision that would have\r\n  123:     announced an invented play on 57% of `media_archive`), and shipped proven on a live leg\r\n  124:     -- **with Codex never participating in a single round.** The seat mattered; which model\r\n  125:     sat in it did not.\r\n  126:   * **A TIMEOUT IS NOT A QUOTA BLOCK.** `agy` failing with `Error: timeout waiting for\r\n  127:     response` means the print timeout, not exhaustion -- `kibitz.py --timeout` does NOT reach\r\n  128:     agy; the knob is **`KIBITZ_AGY_PRINT_TIMEOUT`** (default `5m`; `15m` fixed it first try).\r\n  129:     Check `agy models` returns rc=0 before calling a lane dead.\r\n  130:   **THE TWO-STRIKES FLOOR BELOW IS UNTOUCHED AND NEVER LAPSES.** A bug that survived two of\r\n  131:   your fixes gets a panel on the third attempt no matter how mechanical it looks -- two failed\r\n  132:   fixes IS the evidence that your model of the problem is wrong, which is a design problem\r\n  133:   wearing mechanical clothes.\r\n  134:   **Fable routing (section 9) is unchanged and is a separate axis:** never spend Fable on\r\n  135:   mechanical work. Reserve it for narrative judgment and the final gate on a high-stakes,\r\n  136:   hard-to-unwind structural change. Do not burn a scalpel on a screw.\r\n  137: - **TWO STRIKES, THEN THE PANEL (operator directive 2026-07-14 -- hard).** You get **two**\r\n  138:   attempts to fix a given problem on your own. If the **third** attempt is about to begin --\r\n  139:   i.e. the same bug/failure survived two of your fixes -- you MUST `/kibitz` (local, $0,\r\n  140:   file-grounded) BEFORE writing more code. No third solo swing, ever. Two failed fixes means\r\n  141:   the model of the problem is wrong, and a third guess from the same wrong model just burns a\r\n  142:   live roll. The panel's job is to break your framing, not to bless your patch. You remain the\r\n  143:   judge: ground every panel claim against the real files and discard what does not survive.\r\n  144:   (This is the FLOOR and it never lapses -- a third try ALWAYS gets a panel. The 2026-08-04\r\n  145:   directive above is stricter while it stands: coding work gets a full panel on the first\r\n  146:   swing too. The older \"kibitz on EVERY live failure\" wording is what this replaced.)\r\n  147: - **RIPPING AN LLM IS ALLOWED. A HOLE IN THE LEDGER IS NOT (operator directive 2026-07-14 --\r\n  148:   hard, non-negotiable).** Removing or repurposing an LLM pass is a legitimate and sometimes\r\n  149:   necessary hard decision. But the LEDGER MUST STILL BE FILLED COMPLETELY for downstream\r\n  150:   consumers -- TTS, per-beat audio slicing, video/shot direction, captions, credits,\r\n  151:   `obs_publish`. They read FIELDS, not intentions. Before you remove a pass: (1) enumerate\r\n  152:   EVERY ledger field it wrote; (2) give each field a new owner -- deterministic Python,\r\n  153:   another pass, or an explicit default -- exactly one owner each; (3) only then delete the\r\n  154:   call; (4) prove it on a LIVE leg (RESULT SUCCESS + obs_publish OK + the asset on disk).\r\n  155:   A green unit suite does NOT prove the ledger is complete. A ripped pass with an unowned\r\n  156:   field is a broken render, not a simplification.\r\n  157: - **When genuinely torn between approaches, run the roundtable LIVE for convergence\r\n  158:   (ChatGPT + Gemini + DeepSeek).** Skip the dry-run / cost estimate -- just run it, pronto.\r\n  159:   You are the judge. Escalate to me only if the panel still leaves it unresolved.\r\n  160: - `/kibitz` and `/roundtable` are also welcome for IDEAS at any time -- pressure-testing a\r\n  161:   plan, hunting a defect class, hardening a doc -- not only as the two-strikes backstop.\r\n  162: - This file is hard rules + the real Cowork operating model for this repo + hard-won gotchas.\r\n  163: ## 0. WORKFLOW SOURCE OF TRUTH (hard)\r\n  164: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\workflows\\otr_canonical.json`\r\n  165: IS my workflow.\r\n  166: - ANY json / node / wiring / widget change MUST be made IN that file, in the SAME change as the code.\r\n  167:   Code that is not wired into this JSON is DEAD -- \"your updates are for naught\" (the 2026-06-13 A\u00154D\r\n  168:   miss: a node + a new blend input shipped + tested but UNWIRED -> ran dormant in production).\r\n  169: - EVERY API / headless / soak run MUST LOAD this real JSON -- never a stale copy, a generated\r\n  170:   `.gen.json`, an ad-hoc graph, or the Linux-mount snapshot.\r\n  171: - Schema: litegraph. Top level = `nodes[]` + `links[]` + `last_node_id` + `last_link_id`; a link is\r\n  172:   `[link_id, src_node, src_slot, dst_node, dst_slot, type]`; one output fans out via its `links` list.\r\n  173:   `widgets_values` is POSITIONAL -- only ever APPEND a new optional widget at the END (inserting mid-list\r\n  174:   shifts every saved value -> silent drift, BUG-LOCAL-097). A widget converted to an input keeps its\r\n  175:   value slot AND gains an input with `\"widget\": {\"name\": ...}`.\r\n  176: - **REMOVING A WIDGET TOUCHES THREE THINGS, NOT ONE (learned the hard way 2026-08-28).** An inert widget\r\n  177:   SHOULD be removed -- operator: *\"that's being lazy not to remove an inert widget\"* -- and the positional\r\n  178:   problem is WORK, not a veto. But the work is bigger than it looks, and I corrupted all 63 workflows by\r\n  179:   doing only the first two:\r\n  180:   1. **`widgets_values`** -- drop the value at the widget's index in every saved graph.\r\n  181:   2. **The `inputs` DESCRIPTOR array** -- drop the `{\"widget\": {\"name\": ...}}` entry.\r\n  182:   3. **EVERY LINK TARGETING A LATER SLOT.** `dst_slot` in `[link_id, src_node, src_slot, dst_node,\r\n  183:      dst_slot, type]` is an INDEX INTO THAT SAME `inputs` ARRAY, which holds link sockets and widget\r\n  184:      descriptors together. Remove a descriptor and every link past it is off by one. This is invisible to\r\n  185:      a widget-count check and to `build_variants --check`; `tests/test_workflow_link_target_indexes.py`\r\n  186:      is what catches it. **Repair by IDENTITY, not arithmetic:** set each `dst_slot` to the index whose\r\n  187:      `inputs[i].link` equals that link's id -- self-correcting and impossible to double-apply.\r\n  188:   **A TRAILING widget is nearly free; a mid-list one costs the re-index everywhere.** Check which you have\r\n  189:   before estimating.\r\n  190:   **VARIANTS ARE GENERATED, NEVER HAND-EDITED.** Fix `otr_canonical.json`, then run\r\n  191:   `python scripts/build_variants.py --all` and confirm with `--check`. Editing the variants directly\r\n  192:   passes a naive diff and then fails regeneration.\r\n  193:   **VERIFY WITH ALL FOUR:** `build_variants.py --check`, `tests/test_widget_value_alignment.py` (one node\r\n  194:   type declares one widget ORDER everywhere -- catches a migration that updated canonical and missed a\r\n  195:   variant), `tests/test_canonical_widget_input_parity.py` (count parity), and\r\n  196:   `tests/test_workflow_link_target_indexes.py` (link integrity). The first three all passed while the\r\n  197:   links were broken.\r\n  198: - After editing it, re-validate: `OTR_WorkflowValidator` + a JSON round-trip + a link/widget audit\r\n  199:   (widget-count vs live INPUT_TYPES, every wired input-name in INPUT_TYPES, link referential integrity).\r\n  200: ### 0A. THE ISOLATED-BENCH CARVE-OUT IS STRUCK -- THERE IS NO EXEMPTION (operator, 2026-08-23)\r\n  201: Operator: *\"I think I am done with all bakeoffs.\"* Every bake-off runner, builder,\r\n  202: pinned graph and the vendored bench helper were retired that day, so the ONE exemption\r\n  203: to the rule above no longer has any machinery to license.\r\n  204: \r\n  205: **THE RULE IN SECTION 0 IS NOW ABSOLUTE: every API / headless / soak run loads\r\n  206: `workflows/otr_canonical.json`. There is no second path.** Do not reconstruct a bench\r\n  207: harness to get around it -- if a measurement genuinely needs a stock-node graph, that is\r\n  208: a new operator decision, not a revival of this paragraph.\r\n  209: \r\n  210: What the carve-out used to permit, kept only so a reader of the git history understands\r\n  211: what was removed: `run_video_arm_bakeoff.py` and `run_wan_ti2v_bakeoff.py` submitted\r\n  212: SHA-256-pinned API graphs from `scripts/bench_graphs/`, wrote only to\r\n  213: `otr/episodes/_bench_4arm/<arm>/`, and were forbidden from qualifying anything -- a bench\r\n  214: result could never be worded as qualification. That last clause is the part worth\r\n  215: remembering even now: a measurement is not a proof, and only the canonical path ships.\r\n  216: \r\n  217: ### 0B. START EVERY SESSION BY PULLING, AND NEVER FIX ONE BOX BY BREAKING THE OTHER\r\n  218: **Operator directive 2026-08-29. Two rules, one cause: there are now TWO boxes writing this\r\n  219: repo (section 1's split-by-area), so YOUR CHECKOUT IS NOT AUTHORITATIVE AND YOUR HARDWARE IS\r\n  220: NOT THE ONLY HARDWARE.**\r\n  221: \r\n  222: **FIRST ACTION OF EVERY SESSION, BEFORE READING OR EDITING ANYTHING:**\r\n  223: ```\r\n  224: git fetch origin v2.0-alpha\r\n  225: git log --oneline HEAD..origin/v2.0-alpha     # what the other box did while you were away\r\n  226: git pull --rebase origin v2.0-alpha\r\n  227: ```\r\n  228: Then SAY what came down. A session that opens `workflows/otr_canonical.json` without doing this\r\n  229: is reading a file that may be hours stale, and the moment it edits and pushes, **the other box's\r\n  230: work is reverted by a change that looks like a clean commit.** That is the single most expensive\r\n  231: way to lose work here and it leaves no conflict and no error -- just a quiet revert with a\r\n  232: plausible message on top of it. The `merge=union` guard protects the append-only LOGS; it does\r\n  233: NOT protect the workflow JSON, `nodes/`, or the profiles, and nothing can.\r\n  234: \r\n  235: **THE SECOND RULE, AND IT IS THE ONE WITH TEETH: A FIX FOR ONE MACHINE MUST PROVE THE OTHER\r\n  236: MACHINE IS UNCHANGED -- MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED.**\r\n  237: The 4060 exists to find what the 5080 cannot see about itself. That is its whole value, and it\r\n  238: is also the hazard: a change that makes an 8 GB card work can silently degrade the 16 GB card\r\n  239: that renders the actual episodes, and the 5080 window will not notice because its tests still\r\n  240: pass. \"Passes the suite\" is not the same claim as \"the 5080's numbers did not move.\"\r\n  241: * **Confine it if you can.** Per-machine choices belong in PROFILES and VARIANTS, not in\r\n  242:   `otr_canonical.json` and not in shared code. A change that lives entirely inside a 4060\r\n  243:   profile CANNOT reach the 5080, and that is the preferred shape of every portability fix.\r\n  244: * **When shared code genuinely must change, run the OTHER box's path and show the number\r\n  245:   before and after.** The worked example is PBUG-20260829-07 (2026-08-29): the 12B writer was\r\n  246:   being handed the 2B VRAM budget on 8 GB cards. The fix touched `_plan_max_memory`, which every\r\n  247:   machine calls. Before pushing, the 5080's own path was executed and printed both sides --\r\n  248:   `gemma-4-12b-it @ 15.99 GB -> {0: '13.5GiB'}` before AND after, byte-identical -- because the\r\n  249:   `>= 12 GiB` branch returns before any size tag is read. That single printed line is what made\r\n  250:   the fix safe to push, and it took under a minute.\r\n  251: * **State the blast radius in the commit message.** Name which machines' behaviour changes and\r\n  252:   which is provably untouched. A commit that silently alters both boxes while claiming to fix one\r\n  253:   is the defect this rule exists to prevent.\r\n  254: * **Unsure whether it reaches the other box? It reaches the other box.** Treat it as shared and\r\n  255:   measure.\r\n  256: \r\n  257: ## 1. HOW COWORK ACTUALLY WORKS HERE (read this first)\r\n  258: - **Two separate filesystems.** The file tools (Read / Write / Edit) operate on the REAL Windows files --\r\n  259:   that is your primary editor. **Desktop Commander** (`mcp__Desktop_Commander__*`) runs PowerShell on the\r\n  260:   same real Windows box -- use it for git, the venv python, tests, and process control. The\r\n  261:   `mcp__workspace__bash` Linux sandbox is a DIFFERENT machine: its mount LAGS the file-tool writes (shows\r\n  262:   stale/truncated copies -> phantom \"corruption\") and has NO torch. Use bash only for quick greps of\r\n  263:   UNCHANGED files; never trust it for current state and never run the suite there.\r\n  264: - **The loop:** edit with the file tools -> verify/test with the Windows venv via Desktop Commander ->\r\n  265:   commit AND push via DC git -> verify HEAD == origin.\r\n  266: - **Test runner:** `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe` (torch 2.10). Run with\r\n  267:   `$env:PYTHONUTF8=1`; `pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider`. The conftest sets `OTR_TEST_MODE`/`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=''`.\r\n  268:   Bug Bible lives in a SEPARATE repo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide`\r\n  269:   -- `cd` to its root and use the RELATIVE path `tests\\bug_bible_regression.py` (an absolute forward-slash\r\n  270:   path fails to collect).\r\n  271: - **Knowledge gate before implementation or diagnosis:** read `docs\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md`, then the\r\n  272:   relevant entries in `docs\\PROD_BUG_LOG.md`, and the matching portable rules in\r\n  273:   `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide\\BUG_BIBLE.yaml`. The lessons are\r\n  274:   mandatory project context, the production log is the staging record, and the Bible is the reusable\r\n  275:   cross-project contract. A newly fixed, repeatable production failure must be recorded in the log and\r\n  276:   promoted with a Bible entry plus executable coverage whenever its verify condition is automatable.\r\n  277: - **Admission rule:** only a bug verified by a live production artifact, headless run, smoke, soak, or\r\n  278:   published episode may enter `PROD_BUG_LOG.md` or be promoted to the Bug Bible. A review observation,\r\n  279:   static-audit finding, or invented test fixture may verify a known production bug, but never creates a\r\n  280:   new PBUG or Bible rule on its own.\r\n  281: - **Bible delta-scrape discipline (2026-08-07):** the Bible repo carries `otr_coverage_index.yaml` --\r\n  282:   all 369 OTR bug records through 2026-08-07 (BUG_LOG.md, BUG_LOG_2026-06.md, docs/handoffs, kibitz\r\n  283:   runs, loose logs, git fix history) mapped to Bible ids against the 261-entry Bible (HEAD 3759ae5).\r\n  284:   NEVER re-scrape indexed history -- the full scrape cost ~4M tokens once and the index exists so it\r\n  285:   is never paid again. At session wrap-up / handoff, if the session recorded a NEW bug (admission rule\r\n  286:   above): check it against the index + Bible, promote a genuinely uncovered one with a Bible entry,\r\n  287:   and append its row to the index in the same change. Any cached Bible copy, entry count, or vendored\r\n  288:   test snapshot re-syncs to the Bible repo's origin/main -- do not pin a stale local copy of the\r\n  289:   Bible or its tests.\r\n  290: - **PowerShell reality (DC runs powershell.exe):** use `;` to chain, NOT `&&`. Do NOT use\r\n  291:   `python -c \"...\"` with nested quotes -- PowerShell mangles them; instead WRITE A TEMP `.py` file, run\r\n  292:   it, then delete it. `2>&1` makes stderr render as scary red text -- that is NOT a failure; check the\r\n  293:   exit code / output. Pipe noisy output through `Select-Object -Last N`.\r\n  294: - **QUOTING / `$`-INTERPOLATION RULE (hard -- this bites EVERY session that ignores it):** any command\r\n  295:   that would need NESTED quotes, backtick-escaped quotes (`` `\" ``), a `$` variable inside a quoted\r\n  296:   argument, a here-string fed to another program, or `cmd.exe /c \"<...>\"` -- DO NOT attempt it inline\r\n  297:   and DO NOT iterate on escaping (\"one more tweak\" never works; PowerShell mangles `$var` and eats\r\n  298:   quote layers). IMMEDIATELY write a temp LAUNCHER SCRIPT instead -- a `.ps1` (or `.py`/`.cmd`) written\r\n  299:   via the FILE TOOLS (Write, not echo/Set-Content with its own quoting problem), with all variables and\r\n  300:   quoting inside the script where they are literal and safe -- run it via\r\n  301:   `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <tmp.ps1>` (or `Start-Process -FilePath`), then delete it.\r\n  302:   First quoting error = STOP escaping, switch to the script. Zero exceptions.\r\n  303: - **The ~60s MCP ceiling:** any single DC command that blocks longer (a `Start-Sleep` > ~45s, a big render\r\n  304:   loop, the full ~4200-test suite, a slow boot wait) TIMES OUT and orphans the process. Background it to a\r\n  305:   log and poll the log, or shrink the job (a test subset, fewer frames). DC itself is NOT flaky -- the\r\n  306:   command was too long.\r\n  307: - **Subagents (Agent tool)** are excellent for read-only fan-out audits, but TELL them to use Desktop\r\n  308:   Commander + the Windows venv/path -- left to default they read the lagging Linux mount and report\r\n  309:   phantom truncation/corruption (happened 2026-06-13; the third agent that used the Windows path was right).\r\n  310: - **Stale git `index.lock`:** if `git add`/`commit` fails with \"index.lock: File exists\" AND\r\n  311:   `Get-Process git` is empty, the lock is STALE (a real git op finishes in seconds) -- remove\r\n  312:   `.git\\index.lock` and retry. Do NOT remove it while a git process is actually running.\r\n  313: - **TWO WINDOWS, SPLIT BY AREA, BOTH PUSH (operator decision 2026-08-29 -- supersedes the older\r\n  314:   \"one coder window at a time\" line).** The 5080 and the 4060 both run coder windows and both push\r\n  315:   to `v2.0-alpha` directly. Serialization by turn-taking was the old answer and it cost more than it\r\n  316:   bought: on 2026-08-29 the two boxes landed ~25 commits with zero lost work, and the 4060 found\r\n  317:   three defects in the 5080's own files (a fetcher pulling the wrong motion module, an h3 profile\r\n  318:   declaring a canvas its engine overruled, a re-run that could not have tested what it claimed)\r\n  319:   while the 5080 fixed a loader bug that was killing the 4060's legs. Neither would have happened\r\n  320:   quickly behind a single-writer queue.\r\n  321:   **THE SPLIT -- every file has exactly one owner, and the owner pushes it:**\r\n  322:   * **The 4060 (MRKT) owns the portability surface:** any profile IT has proven on 8 GB hardware,\r\n  323:     `docs/4060_DRILL_LOG.md`, and the fresh-install / least-friction path. It is the only box that\r\n  324:     can answer \"does this work somewhere other than where it was written\", which is the one question\r\n  325:     the dev box structurally cannot answer about itself.\r\n  326:   * **The 5080 (IDREAM) owns the shipping surface:** `pyproject.toml` and anything registry-facing,\r\n  327:     `workflows/otr_canonical.json` and its variants, `nodes/`, and profile `status` promotions.\r\n  328:   * **`docs/PROD_BUG_LOG.md` is shared and append-only** -- both boxes write it, by appending.\r\n  329:   **WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE IS STRUCTURAL, NOT ETIQUETTE.** `.gitattributes` marks the append-only logs\r\n  330:   `merge=union`, so a tail collision keeps BOTH sides automatically instead of raising conflict\r\n  331:   markers that a tired window resolves by picking one. That resolution was performed BY HAND twice\r\n  332:   on 2026-08-29 before the guard existed. Union can duplicate; it cannot lose. Never extend\r\n  333:   `merge=union` to `.json` or `.py` -- it yields invalid JSON and code that parses and is wrong.\r\n  334:   **STILL TRUE AND UNCHANGED:** two windows editing THE SAME FILE at once is how it gets corrupted,\r\n  335:   and the workflow JSON is the worst case. The split exists precisely so that does not happen; when\r\n  336:   work genuinely crosses the boundary, the owner of the file makes the edit, or the two windows\r\n  337:   agree in-message first. `docs/GO_FORWARD_PLAN.md` remains the place to serialize a genuinely\r\n  338:   shared multi-step effort.\r\n  339: - Use **AskUserQuestion** for genuine operator decisions; use the **task list** for any multi-step work.\r\n  340: ## 2. AUTONOMY / PRIME DIRECTIVE\r\n  341: - NEVER ask me to run scripts, commands, or anything. YOU run it: Desktop Commander first; if DC can't,\r\n  342:   Windows MCP; then the filesystem tools. Never hand me a bat/cmd/PowerShell block and say \"run this.\"\r\n  343: - You can drive the 5080 GPU yourself -- spin up the headless ComfyUI API (port 8000) and run it; don't\r\n  344:   ask me. (Reset the box first -- section 4.)\r\n  345: - If a senior pair-programmer would just do it, just do it. Stuck choosing between options? Roundtable\r\n  346:   2-3 panels (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek) for opinions BEFORE asking me -- run it LIVE (skip the dry-run),\r\n  347:   you are the judge.\r\n  348: ## 3. CODING DISCIPLINE\r\n  349: - Keep coding until all sprints are done unless you genuinely need me.\r\n  350: - Run the regression suite + the Bug Bible after EVERY code change (don't wait to be asked). Commit AND\r\n  351:   push per green chunk (section 7).\r\n  352: - Prefer editing the file you're already in; don't spray new throwaway files (and delete any temp probe\r\n  353:   scripts before committing). Keep handoff files current.\r\n  354: - Names: never \"dummy\" -- use \"placeholder\", \"stub\", or a descriptive name (\"dummy\" makes me feel bad).\r\n  355:   SFW always. UTF-8, no BOM. Clean logs, meaningful names -- the reader matters.\r\n  356: ## 4. RESET BEFORE EVERY HEADLESS RUN (hard)\r\n  357: The soak/quick-smoke harness boots ONE server, runs all legs against it, and does NOT tear it down -- it  450:   `Comfy-Org/registry-backend`. Waiting, or asking Comfy-Org, are the only moves.  461:   setuptools' `dynamic = [\"dependencies\"]` + `[tool.setuptools.dynamic]`. Proven the hard way on  462:   2026-08-22: alpha.3 shipped with the dynamic form and the registry still recorded\r\n  463:   `dependencies: []`; alpha.4 with a static list recorded all 12. A pack published with `[]`\r\n  464:   installs its code with NONE of its libraries. Verify after every publish:\r\n  465:   `curl https://api.comfy.org/nodes/comfyui-old-time-radio/versions` and check the count.\r\n  466: - **\"No nodes found\" on a registry node PAGE is NOT a signal that the pack is broken.**\r\n  467:   Established 2026-08-22 by comparing against working packs: ComfyUI-DramaBox -- Active, correct\r\n  468:   static deps, a real shipped pack -- displays no nodes either, and `/nodes/<id>/comfy-nodes`\r\n  469:   returns 404 for EVERY pack sampled (propost, kjnodes, rgthree, dramabox, cache-cleaner). That\r\n  470:   panel is fed by a separate extraction service that evidently does not populate for most packs.\r\n  471:   **The only trustworthy test is the LOCAL one: install, restart ComfyUI, and look for the OTR\r\n  472:   nodes in the node menu / the ComfyUI console for `[OldTimeRadio]` lines.** Do not diagnose from\r\n  473:   the registry page, and do not send the operator chasing a phantom because of it.\r\n  474: - **`__init__.py` loads each node in its OWN try/except, and that is deliberate** (partial-install\r\n  475:   resilience). Consequence for debugging: a missing dependency SKIPS the affected node and prints\r\n  476:   `[OldTimeRadio] Skipped '<name>': <reason>` -- it does NOT zero out the pack. Proven by loading\r\n  477:   the real published zip with every requirements.txt dep blocked: 32/34 nodes still registered.\r\n  478:   So **a TOTAL zero-node outcome is NOT explained by missing dependencies** -- if you ever see a\r\n  479:   true zero, look for the pack not being loaded at all (wrong dir, prestartup death, ComfyUI never\r\n  480:   scanning it), not for a missing library.\r\n  481: ## 8. ROUNDTABLE DEFAULTS (operator directive 2026-06-22)\r\n  482: Standing shape for EVERY `/roundtable` in this repo. These OVERRIDE the skill's stock\r\n  483: \"Claude is judge-only / panel only critiques\" and \"dry-run estimate first\" defaults.\r\n  484: - **Panel = 2-3 FRONTIER models per round** (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek/Grok class; `~latest`\r\n  485:   aliases, record the resolved model in the manifest). Lean panel of genuinely different\r\n  486:   families beats many near-duplicates -- diversity is the point, correctness comes from the\r\n  487:   grounding step.\r\n  488: - **Cowork (Claude) is ALWAYS a code-aware grounded PANELIST *and* the sole judge.** Write your\r\n  489:   own grounded review FIRST (every claim checked against the real Windows files via Desktop\r\n  490:   Commander, never the lagging Linux mount), THEN ground the panel's reviews, discard the\r\n  491:   misreads, and synthesize. The panel proposes; Claude disposes -- never outsource synthesis.\r\n  492: - **Four-round campaign, in order:** R1 high-level arc / creative approach -> R2 coding plan ->\r\n  493:   R3 wiring (workflow JSON / nodes / widgets + any re-baseline procedure) -> R4 final\r\n  494:   convergence (confirm no new must-fix). Re-loop a round only if it surfaces new material; stop\r\n  495:   at convergence (don't grind passes to hear \"looks good\" in more accents).\r\n  496: - **Never dry-run, never pre-compute the cost -- just spend and run it LIVE,** then state the\r\n  497:   actual spend after. (Only backstop = the global >= $20-or-irreversible gate, which roundtable\r\n  498:   passes never reach in practice.)\r\n  499: - **ARC ROUTING (operator directive 2026-07-09): R1 (ideas/high-level) = cloud `/roundtable`\r\n  500:   on the highest frontier models (that's where paid diversity earns its keep); R2-R4 (coding /\r\n  501:   wiring / convergence) = `/kibitz` (local Codex + Antigravity) ALWAYS PREFERRED -- $0, file-\r\n  502:   grounded. R1 via kibitz is also fine when economy matters. OpenRouter is paid, so default mechanical review to the local panel.\r\n  503: - Artifacts under `docs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<topic>/roundtable/` (pass00..passNN_plan.md +\r\n  504:   passNN_judgment.md), UTF-8 no BOM, ASCII where practical.\r\n  505: ## 9. MODEL ROUTING -- when to spend Fable (operator directive 2026-07-03)\r\n  506: Fable is a scalpel, not a default. Spawn it ONLY when output quality depends on narrative\r\n  507: judgment; keep everything mechanical off it. This is about WHICH MODEL a subagent runs --\r\n  508: orthogonal to the filesystem rule in section 1 and the frontier roundtable panel in section 8\r\n  509: (that panel is external cloud models via OpenRouter; this is the Fable model as a Cowork subagent).\r\n  510: - **Use Fable for:** generative creative work where voice/taste matter -- story spine, character\r\n  511:   interiority, dialogue passes, pitch-room ideation, style/tone calls. Divergent fanout when you\r\n  512:   want real variance, not one answer (e.g. 3 subagents each pitching a different take on a scene --\r\n  513:   only fan out on a genuine fork you'll actually select between). Judgment on already-generated\r\n  514:   narrative -- \"which brief holds together,\" \"where does this arc sag.\"\r\n  515: - **Do NOT use Fable for:** mechanical/deterministic work -- repo grep, mapping references, editing\r\n  516:   JSON, validation, wiring checks, git. Route those to general-purpose / Explore, or just do them in\r\n  517:   the main window. Also skip Fable for one-shot factual/structural questions with a single right answer.\r\n  518: - **Keep the spawn count down:** batch context so each Fable call does a meaningful chunk (a whole\r\n  519:   scene, not one line); gate the spawn on a real fork -- if you'd just take the first answer, use one\r\n  520:   call or none.\r\n  521: - **REALITY EXCEPTION -- Fable's grounded FAN-OUT audit DOES catch real code build-breakers the\r\n  522:   mechanical panels miss (proven 2026-07-03).** On the VRAM-tier rip, a Fable end-to-end fan-out found\r\n  523:   a CHUNK-ORDER KeyError (the workflow validator reads a profile key that was being removed mid-rip ->\r\n  524:   every production render would KeyError) that BOTH codex and general-purpose review missed; it also did\r\n  525:   the frame-budget arithmetic (29->33 frames) that broke two unlisted tests, and spotted a grep-invisible\r\n  526:   hyphenated `--vram-ceiling` flag. So Fable is NOT only-narrative. Reserve it as the FINAL GATE on a\r\n  527:   HIGH-STAKES, hard-to-unwind, production-touching STRUCTURAL change (a big rip/refactor about to be\r\n  528:   executed / merged) -- ONE grounded pass, AFTER codex + general-purpose have gone first, when a missed\r\n  529:   thread would break the build and cost a debugging-and-revert loop. This is the \"insight\" worth the\r\n  530:   spend. It does NOT reopen Fable for ROUTINE review: everyday grep / wiring / validation / JSON still\r\n  531:   defaults to general-purpose/Explore/codex. Fable = the expensive last set of eyes on the make-or-break,\r\n  532:   not the default reviewer.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'docs\\\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"    1: # Production Sprint Lessons\r\n    2: \r\n    3: ## Mandatory use\r\n    4: \r\n    5: Before an implementation, live diagnosis, source-bank change, or workflow edit,\r\n    6: read this document and the relevant entry in `PROD_BUG_LOG.md`. Then consult the\r\n    7: portable counterpart in the shared Bug Bible at\r\n    8: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide\\BUG_BIBLE.yaml`.\r\n    9: This document retains the OTR-specific causal history; the Bible carries the\r\n   10: generalized rule and its executable guard. Record a newly discovered production\r\n   11: failure in the log first, and promote it to the Bible when it is repeatable and\r\n   12: generalizable. Do not admit an invented fixture, code-review observation, or\r\n   13: static-audit finding as a new production bug; those may only verify a failure\r\n   14: already observed in a live run, smoke, soak, or published artifact.\r\n   15: \r\n   16: Use this guide when planning any OTR coding sprint, including story banks,\r\n   17: dynamic visual direction, audio/SFX layers, model integrations, and workflow\r\n   18: changes. The project rules in `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` remain authoritative.\r\n   19: \r\n   20: ## 1. Define ownership before implementation\r\n   21: \r\n   22: For every artifact and field, name:\r\n   23: \r\n   24: - the one authoritative writer;\r\n   25: - every consumer;\r\n   26: - whether it is authored, derived, or measured;\r\n   27: - its lifecycle boundary (draft, accepted, frozen, or post-freeze extension);\r\n   28: - its durable storage and replay receipt.\r\n   29: \r\n   30: If an input list becomes output rows, state the mapping exactly: one row per\r\n   31: input item, one singular owned value per row, exact reference coverage, and\r\n   32: which collections live at the top level. List the exact fields allowed in each\r\n   33: nested row. Do not assume a model infers ownership or nesting from a JSON\r\n   34: schema.\r\n   35: \r\n   36: ## 2. Keep five representations in lockstep\r\n   37: \r\n   38: Every structured LLM pass has five representations that must agree:\r\n   39: \r\n   40: 1. base prompt;\r\n   41: 2. typed schema;\r\n   42: 3. worked fixture/example;\r\n   43: 4. parser and deterministic validator;\r\n   44: 5. repair prompt.\r\n   45: \r\n   46: A change to one requires an audit of the other four. Explicitly forbid common\r\n   47: pseudo-shapes: numbered fields, `_secondary`/`_tertiary` variants, schema-path\r\n   48: keys, singular-vs-list aliases, and valid collections nested at the wrong\r\n   49: depth.\r\n   50: \r\n   51: ## 3. Separate authored decisions from mechanical repair\r\n   52: \r\n   53: Python may safely repair only facts already determined by accepted artifacts:\r\n   54: IDs, ordering, references, exact enums, hashes, routing metadata, duplicate\r\n   55: references with an unambiguous first owner, and relocation of values whose\r\n   56: destination is uniquely declared.\r\n   57: \r\n   58: Python must not invent story ownership, grounding, or authoritative narrative\r\n   59: decisions: motives, clues, causality, visual taste, sound-design decisions, or\r\n   60: source claims. If placement or meaning is ambiguous, return the defect to the\r\n   61: owning model and fail closed when the bounded ladder exhausts.\r\n   62: \r\n   63: The one narrow exception is a terminal spoken-surface hygiene floor after every\r\n   64: bounded model repair rung has exhausted on a non-safety craft defect. It may\r\n   65: strip action/markup, normalize speech-only tokens, trim a clause, or substitute\r\n   66: a small generic SFW utterance whose value is delivery rather than story\r\n   67: authority. It must not alter immutable evidence, grounding, graph ownership, or\r\n   68: factual suffixes; it must validate the exact TTS projection, stamp the gate and\r\n   69: resolving rung, and isolate an empty result to that row. Content safety and\r\n   70: ambiguous structural defects remain fail-closed.\r\n   71: \r\n   72: A deterministic repair must validate the complete downstream contract before\r\n   73: returning. A partially valid schema object can consume the repair rung and\r\n   74: prevent the intended LLM fallback.\r\n   75: \r\n   76: ## 4. Design retry ladders by failure class\r\n   77: \r\n   78: - JSON syntax failure: same prompt at lower temperature may help.\r\n   79: - Typed/schema failure: send the failed artifact, exact error, schema, and\r\n   80:   explicit correction rules to typed repair.\r\n   81: - Semantic/graph failure: name the objective invariant and the owning item;\r\n   82:   do not ask a lower-temperature retry to guess.\r\n   83: - Incomplete repair JSON: retry the exact cached repair prompt only when the\r\n   84:   configured budget permits it.\r\n   85: \r\n   86: Log which rung ran and why. Never silently truncate, silently coerce authored\r\n   87: content, or fall back to a canned artifact.\r\n   88: \r\n   89: ## 5. Size context from the real artifact\r\n   90: \r\n   91: Do not derive output or context budgets from `target_words` alone. Include the\r\n   92: actual size drivers: accepted line count, evidence rows, graph width, schema\r\n   93: overhead, prior artifact size, and repair envelope size. The repair prompt is\r\n   94: often the largest call.\r\n   95: \r\n   96: Resolve the true context cap for the selected model and fail loudly when a\r\n   97: provenance-sensitive prompt cannot fit. A model's native context and the\r\n   98: project's configured safe context are separate facts; measure both.\r\n   99: \r\n  100: ## 6. Test model diversity, not just repeatability\r\n  101: \r\n  102: A prompt that works on one local LLM is not production-qualified. Different\r\n  103: families fail differently: integer IDs, extra fields, renamed forbidden keys,\r\n  104: wrong nesting, copied request envelopes, enum synonyms, or over-wide output.\r\n  105: \r\n  106: Qualification ladder for model-sensitive work:\r\n  107: \r\n  108: 1. unit fixtures and full regression gates;\r\n  109: 2. canonical 30-word end-to-end smoke on at least two different local model\r\n  110:    families and one configured cloud/frontier creative lane;\r\n  111: 3. the same pairings at 120 words;\r\n  112: 4. only then, 720-word qualification or bakeoff.\r\n  113: \r\n  114: Record concrete model labels, slot assignments, prompt IDs, repair counts,\r\n  115: ledger paths, and final asset paths for every leg.\r\n  116: \r\n  117: ## 7. Prove the real workflow end to end\r\n  118: \r\n  119: Always load `workflows/otr_canonical.json`. Code that is not wired into that\r\n  120: file is dead. Any node, input, widget, or link change updates and validates the\r\n  121: canonical JSON in the same commit.\r\n  122: \r\n  123: An API `SUCCESS`, idle VRAM, or a resident server is not final proof. Verify:\r\n  124: \r\n  125: - the saved ledger and required receipts;\r\n  126: - the canonical episode asset under `otr/episodes/<ep>/`;\r\n  127: - `obs_publish OK`;\r\n  128: - the final file exists under `otr/obs/`.\r\n  129: \r\n  130: Conversely, resident VRAM after `Prompt executed` is not evidence that a run is\r\n  131: still alive. Check the queue, runner exit code, history result, log, and file.\r\n  132: \r\n  133: ## 8. Keep GPU experiments clean and comparable\r\n  134: \r\n  135: Before every headless run, selectively stop only ComfyUI and its harnesses,\r\n  136: clear port 8000, and confirm baseline VRAM. Never blanket-kill Python. Boot with\r\n  137: UTF-8 through the canonical launcher. Save one server log and one leg log per\r\n  138: run, and use the watchdog for long renders.\r\n  139: \r\n  140: Change one meaningful variable per comparison. Do not mix a model change, a\r\n  141: prompt change, a quant change, and a context change into one unexplained result.\r\n  142: \r\n  143: ## 9. Treat live failures as reusable project knowledge\r\n  144: \r\n  145: Every bug that actually fails a live smoke, soak, or published episode gets an\r\n  146: append-only entry in `docs/PROD_BUG_LOG.md` with symptom, root cause, fix,\r\n  147: verification idea, and Bug Bible candidacy. Dev-only catches are fixed and\r\n  148: tested but do not enter the production log.\r\n  149: \r\n  150: Promote recurring, machine-checkable failure classes to the Bug Bible later.\r\n  151: Also update the relevant planning guide and preflight so the next sprint avoids\r\n  152: the bug before implementation.\r\n  153: \r\n  154: ## 10. Preserve concurrency boundaries\r\n  155: \r\n  156: One window owns code and the canonical workflow. Parallel windows may perform\r\n  157: read-only investigation or docs-only scoping, but they do not edit active code,\r\n  158: tests, registries, or workflow JSON. Preserve unrelated dirty-tree files and\r\n  159: stage only the owned change.\r\n  160: \r\n  161: After every code change: focused tests, full Windows suite, Bug Bible, AST/JSON\r\n  162: checks, commit, push to `v2.0-alpha`, and verify `HEAD == origin` before live\r\n  163: qualification.\r\n  164: \r\n  165: ## 11. Prove semantic provenance, not just structural references\r\n  166: \r\n  167: An ID can remain valid while its meaning disappears. If artifact A carries a\r\n  168: typed clue and artifact B carries only that clue's ID, a later model can emit a\r\n  169: self-consistent but unrelated story while every graph/reference validator\r\n  170: passes.\r\n  171: \r\n  172: For content-owned pipelines, build a small immutable grounding contract from\r\n  173: the accepted source/draw. Carry exact positive anchors through every authoring\r\n  174: and retake seam, and verify their spoken evidence at the coordinates that own\r\n  175: them. Structural coverage, an LLM audit, a frozen ledger, and a published video\r\n  176: cannot override a deterministic semantic-fidelity failure.\r\n  177: \r\n  178: ## 12. Test channel isolation by changing one channel\r\n  179: \r\n  180: Comments saying �?ovisual style does not affect story�?? are not proof. Run the\r\n  181: same mocked story pipeline twice while changing only the visual-style value and\r\n  182: assert every captured story-model message is byte-identical. Apply the same\r\n  183: method to SFX, voice, render, and other downstream channels.\r\n  184: \r\n  185: The story may feed visual direction; visual direction must not feed back into\r\n  186: story authorship unless a separately named, explicitly designed mode owns that\r\n  187: decision. Store selectors in separate ledger namespaces so a forensic audit can\r\n  188: distinguish correlation from causation.\r\n  189: \r\n  190: ## 13. Persist enough accepted artifacts to locate first drift\r\n  191: \r\n  192: Response hashes prove identity only when the response is still available. They\r\n  193: cannot reveal where meaning first changed after a production run. Persist a\r\n  194: bounded receipt of accepted typed artifacts and their grounding evidence:\r\n  195: selected input, truth/plan, score/manifest, final authored output, audit, and\r\n  196: line/span coordinates.\r\n  197: \r\n  198: Do not retain raw prompts, rejected outputs, or unlimited retry prose by\r\n  199: default. The goal is replayable accepted-state forensics, not an unbounded model\r\n  200: transcript.\r\n  201: \r\n  202: ## 14. Close every nested object, not only the artifact root\r\n  203: \r\n  204: Listing required schema paths tells a model what must exist; it does not tell\r\n  205: the model which plausible bookkeeping fields must not exist. For every strict\r\n  206: structured artifact, state the exact key set at each nested ownership boundary,\r\n  207: especially music, media, provenance, delivery, and file/path-shaped objects.\r\n  208: \r\n  209: Pair that prompt contract with a narrow structural normalizer only where Python\r\n  210: can prove the removed fields are non-authoritative metadata. Such a normalizer\r\n  211: must preserve all required authored values byte-for-byte and rerun the complete\r\n  212: strict schema plus semantic/graph validators. Never project an arbitrary model\r\n  213: object onto a schema when an unknown key could carry story meaning.\r\n  214: \r\n  215: ## 15. Make ordered graph topology executable\r\n  216: \r\n  217: An array can contain every required typed row and still violate the graph's\r\n  218: meaning through order alone. State sequence rules with a concrete valid and\r\n  219: invalid example (for example A, A, B versus A, B, A), identify which manifest\r\n  220: owns the canonical group order, and enforce the invariant in Python.\r\n  221: \r\n  222: Never reorder authored chronological rows as a structural repair: sequence is\r\n  223: story, even when every row object remains byte-identical. Repair only mechanical\r\n  224: identifier topology when it is provably unambiguous -- for example, split a\r\n  225: reopened A/B/A shot into A/B/A-return by cloning the shot metadata and retagging\r\n  226: the later run -- while holding the authored row sequence fixed. Then rerun the\r\n  227: complete graph, semantic, grounding, and landmark validators. Reject or fall\r\n  228: through to typed repair whenever the identifier split is ambiguous.\r\n  229: \r\n  230: ## 16. Run deterministic projections at every attempt boundary\r\n  231: \r\n  232: A typed-repair prompt factory sees the failed base response, but it does not\r\n  233: necessarily see or normalize the model's typed-repair response. If a safe\r\n  234: projection exists only inside that factory, a model can repeat the identical\r\n  235: mechanical defect on the final repair attempt and bypass the projection.\r\n  236: \r\n  237: Hash the actual raw model response first, then apply the same narrow projection\r\n  238: at the slot-output boundary for every base, syntax-retry, and typed-repair\r\n  239: response. Return the projected artifact only when its strict schema and complete\r\n  240: semantic/grounding validators pass; otherwise retain the raw response and let\r\n  241: the ordinary ladder report or repair the real remaining defect.\r\n  242: \r\n  243: ## 17. Put typed structural repair at the accepted-object boundary\r\n  244: \r\n  245: Raw-string cleanup is appropriate for unambiguous JSON-shape defects, such as\r\n  246: lifting a declared collection from the wrong nesting level. It is not enough\r\n  247: for a projection whose safety depends on the fully parsed artifact and its\r\n  248: complete graph/grounding validator. Apply that kind of repair in the\r\n  249: schema-validated post-check over the same typed object that will be returned to\r\n  250: the caller.\r\n  251: \r\n  252: Test the boundary itself: deliberately disable or bypass the earlier raw\r\n  253: normalizer, then prove both a base response and a typed-repair response still\r\n  254: accept only after the typed projection preserves authored values and clears all\r\n  255: validators. This prevents a duplicated pre-parse helper from becoming a\r\n  256: false-green proxy for the production acceptance path.\r\n  257: \r\n  258: ## 18. Group safe graph ownership repairs at one typed boundary\r\n  259: \r\n  260: For a schema-valid score, related exact invariants such as contiguous shot\r\n  261: ownership and one-to-one clue ownership should share the same typed\r\n  262: post-validation boundary. The model owns the first authored placement and all\r\n  263: meaningful prose; Python may only derive a later mechanical identity or remove\r\n  264: an exact repeated reference when the complete typed graph proves that no story\r\n  265: meaning is lost.\r\n  266: \r\n  267: Keep raw cleanup limited to defects that prevent parsing at all. Test each\r\n  268: typed repair with that raw path disabled, including a typed-repair response,\r\n  269: so a pre-parse helper cannot falsely appear to qualify the production guard.\r\n  270: \r\n  271: ## 19. Compose independent safe projections before global validation\r\n  272: \r\n  273: Do not make each narrow mechanical repair demand that every other invariant is\r\n  274: already clean. A score can contain two independently provable defects, such as\r\n  275: a reopened shot run and a duplicate clue reference. Factor a projector from its\r\n  276: full-validation wrapper, apply only a small declared set of disjoint projectors\r\n  277: in deterministic order, and then run the complete graph, grounding, and safety\r\n  278: checks once over their shared result.\r\n  279: \r\n  280: The composition must be bounded, must preserve authored content and chronology,\r\n  281: and must remain fail-closed for an unknown, missing, ambiguous, or still-invalid\r\n  282: condition. Add a regression where the base and typed-repair responses contain\r\n  283: the complete defect combination; testing each repair in isolation is not\r\n  284: evidence that the production boundary can combine them.\r\n  285: \r\n  286: ## 20. Repair localized semantic omissions with bounded typed patches\r\n  287: \r\n  288: Do not resend an entire accepted artifact merely because one LLM-owned leaf\r\n  289: misses an immutable semantic anchor. Whole-document regeneration increases\r\n  290: context pressure, expands the failure surface, and can force the model to\r\n  291: recreate already-valid structure instead of correcting the actual omission.\r\n  292: \r\n  293: Define a minimal patch schema that names the allowed targets and fields. Python\r\n  294: may derive those targets from the immutable contract and must verify exact\r\n  295: one-for-one coverage, literal anchor inclusion, and no changes outside the\r\n  296: declared patch scope. The model still authors the replacement prose. After the\r\n  297: merge, rerun the complete artifact, grounding, and authored-surface validators;\r\n  298: an unknown or broader defect must remain fail-closed rather than being squeezed\r\n  299: through the narrow tool.\r\n  300: \r\n  301: ## 21. Preserve accepted invariants in every replaced patch field\r\n  302: \r\n  303: A patch that replaces an entire leaf value can remove a different invariant that\r\n  304: was already correct in that same value. Include both the newly required facts\r\n  305: and every immutable fact currently present in a selected target. This matters\r\n  306: when one beat carries multiple contracts, such as a reveal or closure beat that\r\n  307: also owns a clue.\r\n  308: \r\n  309: Validate the *merged canonical artifact* in the structured-call post-validator,\r\n  310: not only the patch's local key set. A local patch can be schema-valid and carry\r\n  311: all newly requested literals while still breaking a graph, grounding, safety, or\r\n  312: authorship invariant elsewhere in the target field. Return the precise merged\r\n  313: error to the typed repair ladder and remain fail-closed if it cannot be cleared.\r\n  314: \r\n  315: ## 22. Apply localized semantic repair at every artifact boundary\r\n  316: \r\n  317: Do not stop the bounded-patch pattern at a planning artifact. A complete\r\n  318: performance script can be just as expensive and unreliable to regenerate for a\r\n  319: single missing immutable phrase as a complete score. Keep the authoring model\r\n  320: responsible for replacement prose, but create a separate typed patch seam for\r\n  321: each artifact whose individual leaves have clear ownership and a full validator.\r\n  322: \r\n  323: For spoken-script patches, target line IDs only; retain every valid literal\r\n  324: already spoken by that line, require every newly required literal, and rerun the\r\n  325: complete script graph, safety, and grounding checks after merge. The tool must\r\n  326: not become a broad retake in disguise: no title, roster, ordering, or unplanned\r\n  327: line edits are permitted.\r\n  328: \r\n  329: ## 23. Put the guarded artifact boundary behind every reauthoring route\r\n  330: \r\n  331: An initial authoring pass is not the only place an artifact can regress. Blind\r\n  332: listener retakes, optional polish retakes, and final-audit retakes all create a\r\n  333: fresh complete artifact and can reintroduce the same localized defect. Do not\r\n  334: wire a bounded repair at only the first call site.\r\n  335: \r\n  336: Factor one guarded authoring helper that first accepts structural/safety-valid\r\n  337: output, invokes the narrow patch only for a localized full-contract failure,\r\n  338: then validates the merged canonical artifact. Declare the repair seam on every\r\n  339: pipeline pass that can traverse this helper and use pass-specific journal IDs so\r\n  340: production receipts show where the correction occurred.\r\n  341: \r\n  342: ## 24. Restoring an input is not authoring -- the \"lost anchor\" class\r\n  343: \r\n  344: (2026-07-13: this class cost twelve live rolls before it was named. Causal\r\n  345: record: PBUG-20260713-15..18 in `PROD_BUG_LOG.md`; moved here from\r\n  346: GO_FORWARD_PLAN in the 2026-07-15 baseline.)\r\n  347: \r\n  348: A pass hands an LLM an IMMUTABLE string Python already owns -- a constraint-draw\r\n  349: field, a dealt card, a locked speaker, a coordinate from an accepted artifact --\r\n  350: and asks for it back verbatim. The model paraphrases. Python compares exactly\r\n  351: and kills the episode over a copy of its own input.\r\n  352: \r\n  353: Restore when the correction is FORCED (exactly one value possible); return it to\r\n  354: the model when it is not. Three further laws proven live: a repair prompt that\r\n  355: does not fit is worse than no repair (PROMPT_GUARD truncates the contract\r\n  356: silently); a bounded repair must ask for the unit the model can deliver (batch a\r\n  357: patch and a partial success becomes a total failure); and \"it is broken\" is not\r\n  358: a repair prompt -- name the missing object, the unassigned clue, the exact\r\n  359: string.\r\n  360: \r\n  361: ## 25. A bank lives in ~10 wired surfaces -- rip by the Teardown protocol\r\n  362: \r\n  363: (2026-07-18: the Sonnet bake-off retired 4 banks; the hard part was rediscovering where a bank is\r\n  364: wired, not the decision. Runbook: the Teardown protocol in `SOURCE_BANK_PREFLIGHT.md`; proven by the\r\n  365: `499386aa` roster trim and `docs/2026-07-18-rip-4-banks-plan.md`. 2026-07-31: the runner table left\r\n  366: the writer for `nodes/_otr_lane_specs.py` -- same surface, new address.)\r\n  367: \r\n  368: A source bank is not just its `banks.json` row -- it is wired across ~10 surfaces: the row, the pack\r\n  369: dir, the `story_rules` file, the runner entry in `_otr_lane_specs.LANE_SPECS`, the pipeline object in\r\n  370: `pipelines.json` (a SECOND registry -- the one hand-removal always forgets), the lane runner module, a\r\n  371: possible `if base == \"<family>\"` route, the runnable<->executable registry law in `_otr_story_routing`,\r\n  372: the roster/bijection + bank-enumerating guard tests, and any PBUG the bank's live failures earned. Two\r\n  373: rules that end the re-derivation: (1) removal DEPTH is set by whether a sibling version survives -- a  460: post-audio wire join, prove episode identity again, merge render-owned music  475: \r\n  476: A keyed merge does not preserve producer-owned rows that exist only on disk.\r\n  477: Add a narrow row join for each such producer instead of copying arbitrary stale\r\n  478: content. Prove the immutable run receipt first; then prove the parent object,\r\n  479: recompute its authored hash on both sides, validate producer-specific timing\r\n  480: and role fields, and reject duplicates. If either side has a freeze receipt,\r\n  481: both must carry the same value; episode-name fallback belongs only to two truly\r\n  482: legacy records with no receipt.\r\n  483: \r\n  484: Post-rename consumers must join through the active durable owner, never through\r\n  485: the newest sibling by modification time. Where a stale pre-rename value is\r\n  486: already on a wire, add an explicit graph dependency on rename completion and\r\n  487: let same-receipt durable identity replace stale episode/path fields. Consumer\r\n  488: rescues should validate the durable episode against its directory and use the\r\n  489: freeze receipt whenever the caller carries it. A render is not qualified until\r\n  490: the final ledger can locate every asset without referring to the retired root.\r\n  491: \r\n  492: ## 31. Derived ledger metrics need one lexical owner and one final boundary\r\n  493: \r\n  494: Word count is not `len(text.split())` once authored dialogue contains em dashes,\r\n  495: smart apostrophes, or punctuation glued for performance. Pick one explicit\r\n  496: lexical contract and put it in a dependency-free leaf that every producer,\r\n  497: repair pass, editor, readiness normalizer, aggregate writer, and freeze auditor\r\n  498: can import. A text mutation is one atomic operation over `text`, `char_count`,\r\n  499: and `word_count`; never rely on a later caller remembering the other two.\r\n  500: \r\n  501: The durable save is still the ownership backstop. Re-derive row metrics from\r\n  502: canonical text before rolling them into cast, scene, root, and role-specific\r\n  503: meta totals, and reset empty aggregates instead of leaving values from a prior\r\n  504: composition. Preserve the incoming audit before self-healing so production\r\n  505: diagnostics still identify the offending producer. After the last legal text\r\n  506: mutator, refresh derived metrics once more before the final freeze audit.\r\n  507: \r\n  508: Counts are derived state, not authorship. A count-only refresh must not rewrite\r\n  509: canonical text, delivery projections, accepted-line hashes, content-authorship\r\n  510: receipts, or seals. Enforce the boundary mechanically: scan production modules\r\n  511: for direct ledger-text assignments so a new sibling writer cannot silently\r\n  512: reintroduce split ownership.\r\n  513: \r\n  514: ## 32. Transport only model-owned fields across a bounded LLM context\r\n  515: \r\n  516: An LLM should not reserialize a compiler-owned graph merely to author its leaf\r\n  517: text. Every repeated ID, parent, speaker, boundary, cue, neutral default, and\r\n  518: scene field spends output tokens, creates a fresh drift opportunity, and is\r\n  519: usually echoed again beside the failed artifact during typed repair. A response\r\n  520: can therefore be semantically small yet impossible to complete inside the\r\n  521: model's real context and output limits.\r\n  522: \r\n  523: Define the wire artifact at the authoring boundary, not at the downstream\r\n  524: storage boundary. Give the model a closed list of stable IDs and only the prose\r\n  525: fields it owns. Require exact unique ID coverage, map by ID rather than response\r\n  526: order or fuzzy matching, and compile every mechanically derivable field from\r\n  527: the already accepted graph. Then run the complete downstream validator against\r\n  528: the compiled artifact; a compact transport must not become a validation bypass.\r\n  529: \r\n  530: Repairs need the same discipline. Reinject a complete failed compact draft only\r\n  531: when it parsed, plus the smallest trusted authority needed to correct it. Drop a\r\n  532: malformed prefix instead of paying context to repeat truncation. Require both\r\n  533: the prompt and the full output reservation to fit before inference. A liveness\r\n  534: ladder should be finite and flat: a bounded same-slot ladder, then at most one\r\n  535: fresh independent slot, followed by an explicit deterministic floor only for\r\n  536: advisory craft defects. Record every attempt and terminal exhaustion truthfully.\r\n  537: Prove the maximum supported input and repair envelopes with the exact production\r\n  538: tokenizer, not a character estimate or a smaller surrogate prompt.\r\n  539: \r\n  540: ## 33. Separate render work from positioned timeline ownership\r\n  541: \r\n  542: Full per-shot frame requests describe render work, not necessarily final video\r\n  543: duration. Once a post-audio ledger positions rows, opening, interstitial, and\r\n  544: closing cues may intentionally overlap dialogue at crossfades. Summing those\r\n  545: requests counts the overlap twice and creates body drift that a strict terminal\r\n  546: mux should reject.\r\n  547: \r\n  548: Carry two explicit quantities: the sum of full render requests for workload and\r\n  549: the authoritative positioned timeline boundary for output. Quantize the accepted\r\n  550: audio-ledger duration upward to a complete CFR frame. In the planner, sort rows\r\n  551: stably by position and give a row only the visible interval before the next row\r\n  552: owns the boundary. End that interval at the earliest of its requested end, the\r\n  553: next start, or the timeline end. This trims overlap without stretching a short\r\n  554: clip across a genuine gap; equal-start collisions must be loud.\r\n  555: \r\n  556: Quality telemetry must compare rendered frames with the planned visible slot,\r\n  557: not blindly with the original render request. Preserve requested, rendered,\r\n  558: visible, and overlap-trimmed counts so intentional edits do not masquerade as\r\n  559: engine underruns. A filesystem master probe is a cross-check and fallback: it\r\n  560: may shrink or grow a positioned timeline, while an unpositioned legacy sequence\r\n  561: keeps its historical grow-only behavior. Never widen mux tolerance or charge\r\n  562: body drift to a valid credits declaration.\r\n  563: \r\n  564: ## 34. Explicit delivery length is a producer contract, not a quality opinion\r\n  565: \r\n  566: A requested word count can allow an inclusive tolerance without becoming\r\n  567: optional. Express the tolerance once as integer bounds, persist the target,\r\n  568: bounds, owner, canonical count, and exact character-text hash, and require every\r\n  569: producer family to finish inside that same contract before its final artifact is\r\n  570: sealed. A 180-word request accepts 163..200 words; the same law accepts 289..356\r\n  571: for 320. Floating ratios are planning aids, not the final delivery authority.\r\n  572: \r\n  573: Length repair must be small, fresh, and progressive. Target one owned spoken row\r\n  574: at a time, alternate creative and technical writer slots, validate the complete\r\n  575: candidate, and retain only a strict move toward the band. A malformed call, an\r\n  576: unchanged valid response, or one bad sibling row must not discard prior accepted\r\n  577: progress or consume the remaining dynamic budget. Reject padding, repetition,\r\n  578: fake commercials or products, invented numeric claims, markup, stage directions,\r\n  579: and new visual/canon claims. Subjective taste remains fail-open; measurable\r\n  580: delivery exhaustion raises a typed error before hashes, readiness, or media.\r\n  581: \r\n  582: **Amended 2026-07-30 (A-4).** \"Exhaustion raises a typed error\" is the end of\r\n  583: the ladder, not the first thing that goes wrong on it. A capacity failure now\r\n  584: carries a PHASE and only one of the two is terminal: a PRE-CALL refusal, where\r\n  585: the measured prompt leaves no room for the artifact, is deterministic and stays\r\n  586: loud on the spot; a call that RAN and used its whole output allowance without\r\n  587: stopping is stochastic and advances the ladder instead. The typed error is what\r\n  588: the ladder raises when it has actually spent its attempts.\r\n  589: \r\n  590: Place each adapter at its last safe authoring boundary. Content-owned lanes fit\r\n  591: and rebuild their own proofs and hashes before assembly. Shared inline lanes fit\r\n  592: after story QA and spoken hygiene, re-scour the accepted rows, then stamp the\r\n  593: actual receipt and build reflections from those exact final rows. Preserve older\r\n  594: pass receipts as history instead of relabeling a later repair as an earlier pass.\r\n  595: After the final readiness normalization, freeze performs a read-only hash-bound\r\n  596: recount before video readiness. A miss becomes `needs_full_rerun`; freeze never\r\n  597: authors prose or mutates a content-owned seal.\r\n  598: \r\n  599: ## 35. Candidate exhaustion is not episode exhaustion\r\n  600: \r\n  601: A bounded model call is healthy; a bounded episode-wide output ladder is not.\r\n  602: Keep each repair attempt and each producer candidate finite, but separate that\r\n  603: local liveness from the episode's acceptance state. Four consecutive calls that\r\n  604: make no strict distance progress retire the current candidate. They do not make\r\n  605: the requested episode impossible, and an LLM verdict never acquires that power.\r\n  606: \r\n  607: Escalation is explicit: repair one owned row, try the alternate writer slot, then\r\n  608: discard the whole candidate and ask one producer to author a fresh complete\r\n  609: candidate. Alternate the producer priority on successive rerolls.\r\n  610: Freshness must be model-observable: when two logical slots resolve to one seeded\r\n  611: backend, vary a producer-owned prompt nonce or another validated generation input\r\n  612: so Candidate N cannot replay Candidate N-2 byte for byte forever.\r\n  613: Preserve only candidate-local diagnostics from discarded work; never let its text,\r\n  614: seal, hash,\r\n  615: readiness, or subjective score become authoritative. There is no fixed outer\r\n  616: model-output ceiling. Temporary provider failure stays pending, retryable, and\r\n  617: non-ready until a legal candidate arrives or the operator cancels.\r\n  618: \r\n  619: Only deterministic impossibility may fail loud: invalid configuration, a graph\r\n  620: whose declared capacity cannot reach the band, corrupt schema ownership, or a  647: JSON, schema, content-validation, safety, or output-limit defect exhausts and  807:   improvement and the temporary neutralize as the reliable diagnostic.\r\n  808: - The durable fix is to set the exit status without raising, or to emit the\r\n  809:   guard's verdict from a hook that runs after the terminal summary. Not yet\r\n  810:   done; recorded so the next window does not rediscover the blindness.\r\n  811: - General rule: **a guard that hides evidence costs more than the failure it\r\n  812:   guards.** Any hook that raises must be checked against what else the\r\n  813:   framework still had to do.\r\n  814: \r\n  815: ## Sprint receipt\r\n  816: \r\n  817: Record this at the end of every production sprint:\r\n  818: \r\n  819: ```text\r\n  820: SPRINT RECEIPT: PASS | FAIL\r\n  821: scope:\r\n  822: authoritative_writers:\r\n  823: durable_artifacts:\r\n  824: canonical_workflow_hash:\r\n  825: focused_tests:\r\n  826: full_suite:\r\n  827: bug_bible:\r\n  828: model_pairings:\r\n  829: 30_word_receipts:\r\n  830: 120_word_receipts:\r\n  831: 720_word_receipts:\r\n  832: live_ledgers:\r\n  833: published_assets:\r\n  834: prod_bug_entries:\r\n  835: head:\r\n  836: origin:\r\n  837: remaining_risks:\r\n  838: ```\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
