{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'git grep -n \"def _render_one\" -- nodes/_otr_video_engines/render_driver.py; $start=3850; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'nodes\\\\_otr_video_engines\\\\render_driver.py' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 180 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"nodes/_otr_video_engines/render_driver.py:3859:def _render_one(engine_name, request, *, force_oom, host_caps=None,\n 3850:         return\r\n 3851:     missing = [t for t in required if t not in present]\r\n 3852:     if missing:\r\n 3853:         raise FamilyInputGap(\r\n 3854:             \"candidate %r (family %s) requires input(s) %s the request does \"\r\n 3855:             \"not carry -- LOUD skip down the chain (never feed a wrong-shaped \"\r\n 3856:             \"request to an engine)\" % (engine_name, fam, missing))\r\n 3857: \r\n 3858: \r\n 3859: def _render_one(engine_name, request, *, force_oom, host_caps=None,\r\n 3860:                 profile=None, segment=None):\r\n 3861:     \"\"\"Attempt ONE candidate engine: assert_usable -> prepare -> render_clip ->\r\n 3862:     canonicalize, always teardown. ``force_oom`` raises BEFORE any work (the\r\n 3863:     soak's deterministic mid-episode OOM -- it precedes the family-input check\r\n 3864:     so the soak's expected OOM trail is exactly preserved). Raises on failure;\r\n 3865:     returns the canonical clip dict on success.\r\n 3866: \r\n 3867:     S4 platform-portability (2026-07-10): ``host_caps``/``profile`` carry\r\n 3868:     REAL host facts (build_host_caps) + the ledger-stamped v2 device/dtype\r\n 3869:     policy down to the adapter boundary -- the empty-dict boundary here was\r\n 3870:     the campaign's most consequential wiring catch. ``None`` (direct debug\r\n 3871:     callers) degrades to the old empty dicts.\r\n 3872: \r\n 3873:     CHUNK 5 (2026-07-26): the prepare/teardown bracket now belongs to a\r\n 3874:     :class:`beat_session.BeatSession`. ``segment`` is a\r\n 3875:     :class:`beat_session.SegmentSlot` -- the beat's OPEN session plus WHICH\r\n 3876:     segment this render is -- when a caller is walking a coverage plan. Every\r\n 3877:     segment then renders from the handles the first one loaded, and the\r\n 3878:     teardown happens once, in that caller's outer ``finally``. The slot is one\r\n 3879:     argument rather than three so that \"a session with no segment index\" is a\r\n 3880:     state nobody can construct; the session itself refuses anything that is\r\n 3881:     not the next segment, in range, on the beat the slot claims.\r\n 3882: \r\n 3883:     Passing no session keeps the historical bracket: one prepare, one render,\r\n 3884:     one teardown, in that order, with teardown gated on handles having been\r\n 3885:     taken. ONE deliberate delta -- ``prepare`` now receives a populated\r\n 3886:     ``session_ctx`` (``beat_id``/``segment_count``/``multi_clip``) where it\r\n 3887:     used to receive ``{}``, so an adapter can size for four clips before it\r\n 3888:     loads. Every shipped adapter accepts and ignores that argument today.\"\"\"\r\n 3889:     if force_oom:\r\n 3890:         raise OomSignal(\"forced soak OOM on %s\" % engine_name)\r\n 3891:     _assert_family_inputs_satisfiable(engine_name, request)\r\n 3892:     # A RETIRED id must fail with the NAMED policy error BEFORE the generic\r\n 3893:     # not-registered LookupError -- this is the boundary the shipped\r\n 3894:     # /otr/video_render_single HTTP endpoint reaches (rip-sfx 2026-08-06).\r\n 3895:     # Resolved FOR THE GUARD ONLY (idempotent on internal ids), so a future\r\n 3896:     # retired id that also carries a public alias cannot slip past; the\r\n 3897:     # registry checks below still see the caller's own name unchanged.\r\n 3898:     from .._otr_shared.public_engines import (\r\n 3899:         check_retired_engine, resolve_engine_id)\r\n 3900:     check_retired_engine(resolve_engine_id(engine_name))\r\n 3901:     if not _vreg.is_registered(engine_name):\r\n 3902:         raise LookupError(\"engine %r is not registered\" % engine_name)\r\n 3903:     eng = _vreg.get_engine(engine_name)\r\n 3904:     _caps = host_caps if host_caps is not None else {}\r\n 3905:     _prof = profile if profile is not None else {}\r\n 3906:     try:\r\n 3907:         from . import beat_session as _bs  # type: ignore\r\n 3908:     except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n 3909:         import beat_session as _bs  # type: ignore\r\n 3910:     # M3 delta (d): pass the request as the assert_usable template so an\r\n 3911:     # engine that validates request-shaped inputs (the LTX-AV av_dims gate\r\n 3912:     # on request.canvas) sees the real canvas. Guarded -- a legacy adapter\r\n 3913:     # whose assert_usable predates the request_template kwarg keeps working\r\n 3914:     # (TypeError -> retry without it); real usability rejections raise\r\n 3915:     # EngineUnusable, never TypeError, so this never masks one.\r\n 3916:     #\r\n 3917:     # It runs PER SEGMENT, before the handles are touched: it validates THIS\r\n 3918:     # request against the engine, and a later segment's canvas is not the\r\n 3919:     # first one's.\r\n 3920:     try:\r\n 3921:         eng.assert_usable(host_caps=_caps, profile=_prof,\r\n 3922:                           request_template=request)\r\n 3923:     except TypeError:\r\n 3924:         eng.assert_usable(host_caps=_caps, profile=_prof)\r\n 3925:     if segment is not None:\r\n 3926:         if getattr(segment.session, \"engine\", None) is not eng:\r\n 3927:             raise RenderError(\r\n 3928:                 \"beat session holds engine %r but this segment renders on \"\r\n 3929:                 \"%r -- one session, one engine. Reusing handles across a \"\r\n 3930:                 \"route change is how a beat ends up half in one model and \"\r\n 3931:                 \"half in another. NO FALLBACK.\"\r\n 3932:                 % (getattr(getattr(segment.session, \"engine\", None),\r\n 3933:                            \"name\", \"?\"), engine_name))\r\n 3934:         prepared = segment.begin()\r\n 3935:         raw = eng.render_clip(request, prepared)\r\n 3936:         return eng.canonicalize(raw, request, _prof)\r\n 3937:     # Name the beat even on the single-clip path: the adapter's session_ctx\r\n 3938:     # and the teardown log both read better with a real id than with \"?\"\r\n 3939:     # (2026-07-26 QA panel).\r\n 3940:     _rid = (request.get(\"shot_id\") if isinstance(request, dict)\r\n 3941:             else getattr(request, \"shot_id\", \"\"))\r\n 3942:     with _bs.BeatSession(eng, host_caps=_caps, profile=_prof,\r\n 3943:                          beat_id=str(_rid or \"\")) as sess:\r\n 3944:         raw = eng.render_clip(request, sess.prepared)\r\n 3945:         return eng.canonicalize(raw, request, _prof)\r\n 3946: \r\n 3947: \r\n 3948: def _assert_beat_affordable(shot, prebuilt):\r\n 3949:     \"\"\"ONE admission boundary for a coverage-planned beat, before any load.\r\n 3950: \r\n 3951:     THE HOLE THIS CLOSES. ``_planned_length`` deliberately never consults the\r\n 3952:     VRAM predictor -- a planned segment's length is arithmetic the rest of the\r\n 3953:     beat was built around, not a preference it may re-negotiate. That was\r\n 3954:     survivable while planned segments were rare. Once ``wan_ti2v``,\r\n 3955:     ``fastwan_8gb`` and ``ltx_8gb`` became planning-capped it meant the only\r\n 3956:     ENFORCING guard was bypassed on the path doing most of the rendering, and\r\n 3957:     ``VramPeakProbe`` does not cover it (\"sampled + logged, never enforced\").\r\n 3958:     An in-process CUDA OOM corrupts the allocator, so this is the direction that\r\n 3959:     must never be left open.\r\n 3960: \r\n 3961:     WHY HERE. Every segment's request now exists with its final frame count and\r\n 3962:     canvas, and nothing has been loaded yet. So free VRAM is read ONCE, clean,\r\n 3963:     with no hoist correction -- there is nothing hoisted to correct for. That is\r\n 3964:     a different accounting from ``_floor_length``, which runs AFTER ``prepare()``\r\n 3965:     and must add ``hoisted_vram_mb`` back precisely because the weights it is\r\n 3966:     about to use are already resident and would otherwise be charged twice.\r\n 3967:     Mixing those two conventions is what produced a refusal at \"affordable 20\r\n 3968:     frames (free=9675 MB)\" on an engine that had shipped a published episode.\r\n 3969: \r\n 3970:     WHY A MISSING COST ROW DOES NOT SILENTLY PASS. ``_DEFAULT_FRAME_COST`` makes\r\n 3971:     an unmeasured engine look exactly like a calibrated one: the prediction runs,\r\n 3972:     returns a limit, and the limit describes a different model. Enforcing a\r\n 3973:     borrowed number is worse than not enforcing -- it refuses good renders and\r\n 3974:     admits bad ones with equal confidence. So engines WITHOUT a measured row are\r\n 3975:     reported as unenforced rather than judged against someone else's cost.\r\n 3976: \r\n 3977:     Returns the admission record for the beat receipt. Raises\r\n 3978:     :class:`motion_common.MotionBudgetError` when a measured row says a planned\r\n 3979:     segment does not fit.\r\n 3980:     \"\"\"\r\n 3981:     engine_id = str(shot.get(\"engine_id\") or \"\")\r\n 3982:     record = {\"engine_id\": engine_id, \"checked\": [], \"enforced\": False,\r\n 3983:               \"reason\": \"\", \"free_vram_mb\": None}\r\n 3984:     if not prebuilt:\r\n 3985:         record[\"reason\"] = \"no segments\"\r\n 3986:         return record\r\n 3987: \r\n 3988:     if not _mc.cost_row_may_refuse(engine_id):\r\n 3989:         # Named, visible, and NOT a refusal.\r\n 3990:         #\r\n 3991:         # This asks whether a row may REFUSE, not whether one