{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=7650; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'docs\\\\PROD_BUG_LOG.md' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 190 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 7650: - **STILL WORTH KNOWING (not owed, and not a task):** a live leg on the\r\n 7651:   post-fix prompts would tell us how much residue remains. `scripts/otr_audit_joint_av_stems.py`\r\n 7652:   answers it in minutes on any future episode, for free, whenever one happens\r\n 7653:   to render. Read it if curious; do not schedule work against it. The fix removes the nouns that WERE spoken; it does not\r\n 7654:   prove the model will not vocalise the setting, the motion clause, or the\r\n 7655:   sound names instead. A kibitz r1 panel (codex + antigravity) returned NO on\r\n 7656:   the first problem statement and reframed the goal correctly: this is not\r\n 7657:   \"possible vs impossible\" for a stochastic generator, it is a declared\r\n 7658:   TOLERANCE plus a CONTAINMENT. Panel-verified constraints on any future arm:\r\n 7659:   CFG is locked at 1.0 by the VRAM contract, the negative prompt is hard-coded\r\n 7660:   empty with a drift test, there is NO beat-level routing (engine choice is\r\n 7661:   role-wide), and the mixer cannot separate generated speech from generated\r\n 7662:   foley because they are the same stem -- so muting mime's audio would make it\r\n 7663:   topologically identical to `ltx25_video`.\r\n 7664: - bible-worthy: STRONG CANDIDATE, distinct from `12.137` (foley audibility)\r\n 7665:   and from PBUG-20260828-01 (an unnamed sound CATEGORY inviting voice). The\r\n 7666:   reusable class here is sharper: *on a joint audio-video model the prompt is\r\n 7667:   also an audio script, so any text placed in it may be SPOKEN -- and identity\r\n 7668:   text is doubly wrong because the conditioning image already carries it.*\r\n 7669:   Promote after the live proof, not before.\r\n 7670: \r\n 7671: ## PBUG-20260829-01 -- `-vsync` was REMOVED in ffmpeg 9, killing every episode at the scopes stage\r\n 7672: \r\n 7673: - **found:** 2026-08-29, live leg on the 4060 (MRKT), prompt `02835636` FAIL.\r\n 7674:   Reported by the Fable session driving that box; verified from the 5080 by\r\n 7675:   reading the pushed fix and probing this box's own ffmpeg.\r\n 7676: - **artifact:** a real canonical render. Everything upstream PASSED, including\r\n 7677:   every haunted AnimateDiff beat (v3 + sliding context window, ~9-10 s/step at\r\n 7678:   4.9 GB with SD1.5 fully resident -- that lane genuinely fits 8 GB).\r\n 7679:   `OTR_SceneAwareScopes` then died instantly at argv parse: ffmpeg 9 rejects\r\n 7680:   `-vsync` with \"Unrecognized option 'vsync'\" before reading a single frame.\r\n 7681: - **blast radius:** FIVE call sites passed `-vsync` unconditionally --\r\n 7682:   `_otr_shared/scope_draw.py`, `_otr_shared/encode_sink.py`,\r\n 7683:   `otr_silent_composite.py` (x2), `otr_caption_burn.py`. Any box on ffmpeg 9\r\n 7684:   loses every episode at the same spot; the failure is not lane-specific.\r\n 7685: - **why it hid:** `-vsync` was deprecated in ffmpeg 5.1 but still ACCEPTED, and\r\n 7686:   the 5080 runs 8.0.1, which takes both spellings. So the bug is invisible on\r\n 7687:   the development box and appears the day anyone upgrades ffmpeg -- or on any\r\n 7688:   new user's machine that ships a current build. Same shape as the kokoro\r\n 7689:   `repo_id` catch earlier the same night (PBUG candidate below): the dev box's\r\n 7690:   older dependency masks a break that hits every fresh install.\r\n 7691: - **fix:** `f88a0179`. `scope_draw.cfr_flags(ffmpeg)` PROBES the installed\r\n 7692:   binary once with a 1-frame lavfi null encode and caches per binary path,\r\n 7693:   returning `-fps_mode cfr` when accepted and legacy `-vsync cfr` otherwise;\r\n 7694:   all five sites route through it. `-fps_mode` only exists from 5.1 up, so\r\n 7695:   neither spelling is safe unconditionally -- probing the artifact actually\r\n 7696:   installed is the only correct answer, exactly as the kokoro fix does.\r\n 7697:   Verified on the 5080: `cfr_flags('ffmpeg')` returns `['-fps_mode', 'cfr']`\r\n 7698:   on 8.0.1.\r\n 7699: - **self-caught trap worth keeping:** `-fps_mode` is an OUTPUT option. Placed\r\n 7700:   before `-i` the probe fails and silently falls back to the legacy spelling --\r\n 7701:   a probe that always reports \"old ffmpeg\" would have shipped looking correct.\r\n 7702:   It sits after `-i`, and was verified selecting `-fps_mode` on the ffmpeg 9\r\n 7703:   box.\r\n 7704: - bible-worthy: STRONG CANDIDATE. The reusable class is not about ffmpeg at\r\n 7705:   all: *a dependency flag the dev box still accepts is not a flag the ecosystem\r\n 7706:   still accepts -- probe the installed artifact, never the version you happen\r\n 7707:   to run.* Two independent instances landed the same night (this and kokoro\r\n 7708:   `repo_id`), which is the argument for a rule rather than two fixes. Check\r\n 7709:   against `otr_coverage_index.yaml` before promoting.\r\n 7710: \r\n 7711: ## PBUG-20260829-02 -- `kokoro` 0.7.16's `KPipeline` has no `repo_id` kwarg; every cold install died at its first spoken line\r\n 7712: \r\n 7713: - **found:** 2026-08-29, live leg on the 4060 (MRKT), prompt `82e70344` FAIL\r\n 7714:   at 439.35 s. First render ever attempted on that box.\r\n 7715: - **artifact:** a real canonical render. The writer wrote 6 lines / 70 words,\r\n 7716:   the ledger froze `frozen_with_warns`, casting assigned kokoro voices -- then\r\n 7717:   the very first TTS clip raised\r\n 7718:   `TypeError: KPipeline.__init__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'repo_id'`.\r\n 7719:   No obs publish, correctly.\r\n 7720: - **root cause:** `nodes/_otr_audio_engines/eng_kokoro.py::load` passed\r\n 7721:   `repo_id=\"hexgrad/Kokoro-82M\"` UNCONDITIONALLY. The newest release on PyPI is\r\n 7722:   `kokoro` 0.7.16, whose signature is `KPipeline(lang_code, model, trf, device)`\r\n 7723:   -- no such parameter. Verified by inspecting the installed signature, not by\r\n 7724:   reading docs.\r\n 7725: - **blast radius:** every stock `pip install kokoro` install, on the lane that\r\n 7726:   is the DEFAULT announcer voice in the shipped canonical workflow. The pack\r\n 7727:   reached the first spoken line and died, on a machine where nothing was wrong.\r\n 7728: - **why it hid:** the 5080 dev box runs `kokoro` 0.9.4 -- a build NEWER than the\r\n 7729:   published PyPI line -- which does accept `repo_id`. So the call had always\r\n 7730:   worked there and always would.\r\n 7731: - **fix:** `8b041034`. `inspect.signature(KPipeline.__init__)` is consulted once\r\n 7732:   and `repo_id` is passed ONLY if the installed build declares it; on an\r\n 7733:   unprobeable signature the legacy kwarg is kept, preserving existing behaviour.\r\n 7734:   Forward-compatible in both directions.\r\n 7735: - **companion defect, same night:** `kokoro` was declared in NEITHER\r\n 7736:   `requirements.txt` NOR `pyproject` -- the pack's default voice engine was an\r\n 7737:   undeclared dependency. Fixed by the 5080 in `03a8cb43` (requirements); the\r\n 7738:   pyproject half rides the next deliberate version bump, since editing\r\n 7739:   pyproject auto-fires a registry publish.\r\n 7740: - bible-worthy: STRONG CANDIDATE, and the same reusable class as\r\n 7741:   PBUG-20260829-01: *a dependency signature the dev box accepts is not the\r\n 7742:   signature the ecosystem ships -- probe the installed artifact.* Two\r\n 7743:   independent instances the same night. Check `otr_coverage_index.yaml` first.\r\n 7744: \r\n 7745: ## PBUG-20260829-03 -- ComfyUI DynamicVRAM (`comfy_aimdo`) ABORTS THE PROCESS on an 8 GB OOM instead of raising\r\n 7746: \r\n 7747: - **found:** 2026-08-29, live leg on the 4060 (MRKT), immediately after the\r\n 7748:   kokoro fix above. Prompt id not captured -- the server process died, so the\r\n 7749:   poller never observed a terminal state, which is itself part of this report.\r\n 7750: - **artifact:** a real canonical render, further along than any before it. All\r\n 7751:   six voice clips generated, ledger frozen, visual-direction pass complete.\r\n 7752:   Then, at the z_image sampler's step 0/8 (\"Model Initializing\"):\r\n 7753: \r\n 7754:       aimdo: src/hostbuf.c:283:ERROR:hostbuf_read_file_slice: device copy\r\n 7755:       failed result=2 device_ptr=0x0E00000000 device=0 size=39321600\r\n 7756:       Fatal Python error: Aborted\r\n 7757: \r\n 7758:   CUDA error 2 is out-of-memory, hit while DynamicVRAM streamed the 6.2 GB\r\n 7759:   z_image UNET onto a card still holding OTR's HF-side residents.\r\n 7760: - **TWO defects, and the second is the worse one:**\r\n 7761:   1. **Ours:** the pack's residency discipline does not evict the writer before\r\n 7762:      the image phase. Harmless at 16 GB, fatal at 8 GB.\r\n 7763:   2. **ComfyUI's:** DynamicVRAM's failure mode is a NATIVE PROCESS ABORT, not a\r\n 7764:      Python exception. Nothing can catch it, no node can log it, no retry or\r\n 7765:      loud degradation is possible, and the whole server dies mid-episode. Every\r\n 7766:      diagnostic and recovery mechanism this codebase has is bypassed. A raised\r\n 7767:      OOM would have been catchable; an abort is not.\r\n 7768: - **blast radius:** any 8 GB card on a current ComfyUI (DynamicVRAM is ON by\r\n 7769:   default) running a lane that loads a large image UNET after the writer.\r\n 7770: - **workaround, PROVEN not assumed:** launch with `--disable-dynamic-vram`. The\r\n 7771:   legacy loader partial-loads the same UNET (5.9 GB offloaded to RAM, 211 MB\r\n 7772:   buffer) and was actively SAMPLING at the exact step where aimdo aborted --\r\n 7773:   verified on the next leg (prompt `5de1bf46`), which was then killed by\r\n 7774:   operator order for unrelated reasons, not by memory.\r\n 7775: - **fix:** NOT FIXED -- workaround only, carried in the 4060 headless launch\r\n 7776:   script. The two real fixes are (1) evict the writer before the image phase,\r\n 7777:   which is ours and is a residency-discipline change, and (2) ComfyUI's abort\r\n 7778:   behaviour, which is not.\r\n 7779: - bible-worthy: CANDIDATE, and the reusable class is not about ComfyUI: *a\r\n 7780:   memory manager that aborts the process instead of raising removes every\r\n 7781:   diagnostic, fallback and recovery path the application has -- treat an\r\n 7782:   abort-on-OOM dependency as a hard portability constraint, not a tuning knob.*\r\n 7783: \r\n 7784: ## PBUG-20260829-04 -- `pyloudnorm` is undeclared, so every cold install masters by PEAK instead of LUFS\r\n 7785: \r\n 7786: - **found:** 2026-08-29, in the log of the SUCCESSFUL 4060 leg (prompt\r\n 7787:   `be8d016d`, published episode\r\n 7788:   `signal_lost_the_ledgers_whisper_20260829_045225`).\r\n 7789: - **artifact:** a published episode. `[EpisodeAssembler] LUFS measurement\r\n 7790:   unavailable (No module named 'pyloudnorm'); falling back to the legacy peak\r\n 7791:   master.` The render did NOT fail -- this degraded loudly and correctly, which\r\n 7792:   is why it needs a log entry rather than a crash report.\r\n 7793: - **root cause:** `pyloudnorm` is declared in NEITHER `requirements.txt` NOR the\r\n 7794:   `pyproject` static dependency list. Verified by grepping both. The\r\n 7795:   loudness-normalisation path is written to tolerate its absence, so nothing\r\n 7796:   ever complained on a box that happened to have it.\r\n 7797: - **blast radius:** every registry and cold install masters to peak rather than\r\n 7798:   the intended -14 LUFS. The dev box has the package incidentally, so episodes\r\n 7799:   rendered there are correct and episodes rendered anywhere else are quieter and\r\n 7800:   inconsistent with them -- a difference nobody sees in a log and everybody\r\n 7801:   hears in a playlist. Directly relevant now that two boxes publish into one\r\n 7802:   broadcast folder.\r\n 7803: - **fix:** NOT YET. Must ride the next DELIBERATE pyproject version bump\r\n 7804:   (alpha.9-12 were Flagged/Pending at the time, and editing pyproject\r\n 7805:   auto-fires a publish), alongside the `kokoro` declaration from\r\n 7806:   PBUG-20260829-02.\r\n 7807: - bible-worthy: CANDIDATE. Class: *a dependency that is present on the dev box\r\n 7808:   by accident is not a declared dependency, and a graceful fallback around it\r\n 7809:   hides the omission indefinitely.*\r\n 7810: \r\n 7811: ## PBUG-20260829-05 -- `_plan_max_memory`'s QUANTIZED budget is still a hardcoded tag table (the other half of PBUG-20260825-03)\r\n 7812: \r\n 7813: - **found:** 2026-08-29, four live headless legs on the 4060 (MRKT), driven by\r\n 7814:   the operator asking why the writer was `gemma-4-E2B` and not `gemma-4-12b`.\r\n 7815:   The 12B is fully cached on that box -- not gated, never selected.\r\n 7816: - **artifact / the measurement chain, all live:**\r\n 7817:   1. `otr_4060_haunted_12b` (12B, `bnb_nf4`, `vram_ceiling_gb: 6.8`): died in\r\n 7818:      **0.10 s** with `VRAMFitFailedError: estimated 11.9 GB peak resident vs\r\n 7819:      6.8 GB ceiling`. An ESTIMATE killed the render before any allocation --\r\n 7820:      precisely what the operator's 2026-08-29 directive forbids (below).\r\n 7821:   2. Ceiling raised to 12.0 so physics could rule: bnb's\r\n 7822:      `quantizer_bnb_4bit.validate_environment` raised `ValueError: Some modules\r\n 7823:      are dispatched on the CPU or the disk...` (prompt `41f34374`).\r\n 7824:   3. `otr_4060_haunted_e4b` (`gemma-4-E4B`, ~4-5 GB of NF4 weights against\r\n 7825:      ~7.1 GB free): the **same** refusal in 2.17 s. That cannot be explained by\r\n 7826:      model size.\r\n 7827:   4. `otr_4060_viz_12b` -- `viz_camera` on all four video roles (a procedural\r\n 7828:      lane holding ZERO video-model VRAM), on a freshly booted server with\r\n 7829:      ~7.4 GB free: the **same** refusal again. The video lanes are irrelevant;\r\n 7830:      the cap fires first.\r\n 7831: - **root cause:** `nodes/_otr_model_loader.py::_plan_max_memory` (~L496-509)\r\n 7832:   returns, for `bnb_nf4`/`bnb_8bit` on a sub-12 GB card, a HARDCODED\r\n 7833:   `{0: \"6.8GiB\", \"cpu\": \"32GiB\"}` for any model id tagged `9b`/`12b`/`e4b`/\r\n 7834:   `4b-it` -- **regardless of how much VRAM is actually free**. The explicit\r\n 7835:   `cpu` key then invites `device_map=\"auto\"` to plan an offload for anything\r\n 7836:   over 6.8 GiB on-GPU, and bitsandbytes' 4-bit path REFUSES a plan containing\r\n 7837:   CPU-dispatched modules. `E4B` fails identically because its MatFormer\r\n 7838:   per-layer embeddings stay unquantized and clear the 6.8 GiB cap even though\r\n 7839:   its `Linear4bit` modules fit the card easily.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_87","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=8180; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'docs\\\\PROD_BUG_LOG.md' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 330 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 8180:   (PASS), `otr_g4_*` 12.23 (PASS), `otr_amd16_rocm` at Q8_0@4096 14.60 (WARN --\r\n 8181:   correctly still cautious for the larger quant).\r\n 8182: - **BLAST RADIUS, stated per CLAUDE.md 0B -- this changes BOTH boxes.** The\r\n 8183:   4060/Mac/AMD tiers move FAIL -> WARN/PASS, which is a real behaviour change:\r\n 8184:   they can now load. The 5080's `otr_g4_*` rows move WARN -> PASS. Only FAIL\r\n 8185:   raises, so **no 5080 render behaviour changes** -- what changes there is one\r\n 8186:   caution log line, and the estimate becoming honest (12.23 rather than 17.40).\r\n 8187:   Not claimed as \"5080 untouched\", because it isn't.\r\n 8188: - **pinned by** `tests/test_gguf_fit_prices_requested_quant.py` (6 cases), which\r\n 8189:   also asserts the oversize guard still FAILs a 70B-on-8GB pick so the fix\r\n 8190:   cannot turn the gate into a rubber stamp.\r\n 8191: - **bible-worthy: YES, once proven on a live leg.** The portable rule is \"a\r\n 8192:   preflight estimate must price the REQUEST, not the resource's worst case\" --\r\n 8193:   a guard that ignores the caller's own parameters refuses work it should\r\n 8194:   admit, and does it silently on exactly the hardware the dev box cannot see.\r\n 8195:   Not yet promoted: no live render has been driven through the corrected gate.\r\n 8196: \r\n 8197: ## PBUG-20260829-09 -- the SHIPPING 8 GB profile needs a third-party node pack that is declared NOWHERE\r\n 8198: \r\n 8199: - **found:** 2026-08-29 on the 4060 (MRKT), while measuring the friction of the\r\n 8200:   least-friction path per the operator's request (\"how frictionless is our best\r\n 8201:   frictionless setup on the 4060\"). Found by measurement, not by review: the\r\n 8202:   question \"how many GB and how many manual steps before a first render\" forced\r\n 8203:   an inventory of what actually had to be true on this disk.\r\n 8204: - **artifact:** `otr_nvidia_8gb_haunted` -- `status: \"shipping\"` as of\r\n 8205:   `5987b336`, and the ONLY shipping profile in the repo whose evidence comes\r\n 8206:   from non-dev-box hardware (three published episodes on this card). Its three\r\n 8207:   visual roles and its `video_render_engine` all resolve to\r\n 8208:   `animatediff15_v3_haunted_video`, which is implemented by\r\n 8209:   `nodes/_otr_video_engines/eng_ghost_signal.py` and executes through the\r\n 8210:   **`ComfyUI-AnimateDiff-Evolved`** custom node pack (`ADE_*` nodes).\r\n 8211: - **the defect:** that node pack is declared in NONE of the places a user or an\r\n 8212:   installer would look. Measured:\r\n 8213: \r\n 8214:       README.md         mentions of AnimateDiff-Evolved : 0\r\n 8215:       requirements.txt  mentions                        : 0\r\n 8216:       pyproject.toml    mentions                        : 0\r\n 8217:       scripts/otr_fetch_lane_weights.py                 : does not install it\r\n 8218: \r\n 8219:   `requirements.txt` and `pyproject.toml` could not carry it in any case -- it\r\n 8220:   is a ComfyUI custom node pack, not a pip distribution -- which is precisely\r\n 8221:   why it needs a DOCUMENTED step instead. There is currently no such step.\r\n 8222: - **user-visible consequence:** a user installs OTR from the registry, selects\r\n 8223:   the profile the repo says is *shipping* for their 8 GB card, and the video\r\n 8224:   engine cannot execute. The pack's own `__init__.py` per-node try/except\r\n 8225:   (documented in CLAUDE.md 7A) means this does NOT zero out the pack -- it\r\n 8226:   degrades to a missing engine, which is harder to diagnose, not easier.\r\n 8227: - **why the drill did not catch it sooner, and this is the instructive part:**\r\n 8228:   this box HAS the node pack, so all three episodes rendered. It is present\r\n 8229:   only because the driver `git clone`d it by hand during the 03:00 race while\r\n 8230:   assembling the lane from engine constants. **The proven path and the\r\n 8231:   documented path are not the same path**, and the proof does not transfer to\r\n 8232:   anyone following the docs. Every friction number recorded for this lane was\r\n 8233:   measured on a box that had been hand-prepared.\r\n 8234: - **fix:** NOT FIXED. It is a documentation/installer change on the\r\n 8235:   fresh-install surface (4060-owned under the 2026-08-29 split), and the right\r\n 8236:   shape needs an operator call: either the README states the node-pack\r\n 8237:   prerequisite explicitly for the haunted lane, or `otr_fetch_lane_weights.py`\r\n 8238:   grows a node-pack step, or the lane declares it in a machine-readable\r\n 8239:   preflight so `check_*` fails loudly with the install command instead of the\r\n 8240:   engine going missing. Recorded before choosing, because \"which of the three\"\r\n 8241:   is a shipping decision.\r\n 8242: - **companion finding, same measurement, no separate entry:** the profile's\r\n 8243:   `display_name` advertises \"~3.7GB weights\". Measured total that must land on\r\n 8244:   disk before a first render on a clean box: **16.03 GB** --\r\n 8245:   SD1.5 1.99 + v3_sd15_mm 1.56 + v3_sd15_adapter 0.10 + kokoro-v1_0 0.30\r\n 8246:   (3.94 GB of explicit placements) plus the HF-cache pulls the label omits:\r\n 8247:   **gemma-4-E2B-it 9.57 GB**, musicgen-small 2.21 GB, Kokoro-82M 0.31 GB. The\r\n 8248:   \"~3.7GB\" figure counts only the video lane's own artifacts. It is not false\r\n 8249:   about what it measures; it is read as a total by anyone choosing a profile on\r\n 8250:   a metered connection.\r\n 8251: - bible-worthy: CANDIDATE. The reusable class is the one this drill keeps\r\n 8252:   hitting from new angles: *a lane proven on a hand-prepared box proves the\r\n 8253:   lane, not the install.* Distinct from the kokoro/ffmpeg class (dependency the\r\n 8254:   dev box happens to have) because here NO box could have had it without a\r\n 8255:   manual step nobody wrote down.\r\n 8256: \r\n 8257: ## PBUG-20260829-11 -- every GGUF generate reloads the model: the load path's own eviction makes it look abandoned to itself\r\n 8258: \r\n 8259: - **found:** 2026-08-29 on the 5080, on a LIVE canonical leg (prompt\r\n 8260:   `d2c39f80-c9e0-423a-bd0d-e02c931a64c4`, profile\r\n 8261:   `otr_qwen2507_haunted_proof`). Not a unit test -- this is invisible to one.\r\n 8262: - **observed:** in a single episode's worth of writer calls the server logged\r\n 8263:   **22 GGUF load attempts, 22 \"abandoned\" warnings, and 0 cache adoptions.**\r\n 8264:   The model was re-loaded from disk for every structured call, then the server\r\n 8265:   died with no traceback (hard process death, not an exception).\r\n 8266: - **MECHANISM, confirmed against the code and not inferred from the symptom:**\r\n 8267:   1. `request_slot` snapshots `_my_cache_epoch = _current_cache_epoch()`\r\n 8268:      (`_otr_model_loader.py:1513`) BEFORE any local work.\r\n 8269:   2. The GGUF load path then calls\r\n 8270:      `free_otr_pipeline_residue(reason=\"GGUFNative load preflight\")`\r\n 8271:      (`_otr_gguf_backend.py:1202`), whose step 1 is `unload_llm()`\r\n 8272:      (`_otr_vram_levers.py:143`).\r\n 8273:   3. `unload_llm()` -> `_detach_and_invalidate_locked()` -> **bumps the epoch.**\r\n 8274:   4. `_publish_cache_entry_if_current(_my_cache_epoch, ...)` (`:1751`) then\r\n 8275:      sees a moved epoch, refuses to publish, and logs \"completed after this\r\n 8276:      call was abandoned\".\r\n 8277:   **The call is not abandoned. It advanced the epoch itself, and is then\r\n 8278:   disqualified by its own action.** The guard is doing exactly what it was\r\n 8279:   written to do; the defect is that the GGUF path trips it.\r\n 8280: - **The code already names the right shape.** `unload_llm`'s own docstring:\r\n 8281:   \"the authoritative-invalidator shape used by every caller OUTSIDE\r\n 8282:   request_slot's own control flow ... **NOT** used by request_slot's own\r\n 8283:   self-triggered transitions; see `_self_unload` for why those need a\r\n 8284:   conditional (ownership-checked) claim instead.\" The GGUF preflight calls the\r\n 8285:   unconditional form from INSIDE that control flow.\r\n 8286: - **impact:** every GGUF generate pays a full model load, and nothing is ever\r\n 8287:   cached. That is the entire Mac / AMD / CPU story plus every `*_gguf` profile,\r\n 8288:   because those lanes are GGUF by construction (bitsandbytes NF4 is CUDA-only).\r\n 8289:   **The server death on that leg is NOT explained by this defect and remains\r\n 8290:   OPEN.** It is also NOT PBUG-20260829-12's illegal instruction, and that is\r\n 8291:   ruled out by evidence rather than by architecture: on this box llama.cpp\r\n 8292:   loaded and generated successfully 22 times in that same leg, and a separate\r\n 8293:   standalone load produced coherent prose in 0.6 s. The wheel runs here. The\r\n 8294:   two boxes differ -- 5080: Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX; 4060: i9-13900H -- which\r\n 8295:   is consistent with -12 being 13900H-specific. What killed the 5080's server\r\n 8296:   is still unknown: the log ends mid-load with no traceback, which is a hard\r\n 8297:   process death rather than a Python exception. Worth noting only as a\r\n 8298:   suspicion: 22 llama.cpp contexts that are never adopted are 22 whose release\r\n 8299:   is unaccounted for.\r\n 8300: - **NOT FIXED, deliberately, and this is the reason.** There are at least three\r\n 8301:   defensible repairs -- thread the new epoch back out of the eviction; move the\r\n 8302:   eviction into `request_slot` so the caller owns the bump; or re-capture the\r\n 8303:   epoch after a self-triggered unload -- and they differ in their concurrency\r\n 8304:   guarantees. The guard being tripped is the one that stops an abandoned call\r\n 8305:   from publishing a model a later caller would then generate on concurrently,\r\n 8306:   so a careless fix reintroduces a double-generate race. This is a design item\r\n 8307:   under the 2026-08-17 rule and gets a panel before code, not a solo swing at\r\n 8308:   the end of a long session with the operator away.\r\n 8309: - **bible-worthy: PENDING the fix.** The portable rule is already visible: a\r\n 8310:   guard keyed on \"did the world change under me\" must not be tripped by the\r\n 8311:   guarded call's OWN side effects -- otherwise it converts a correctness check\r\n 8312:   into a permanent cache miss, silently, while every log line looks like a\r\n 8313:   race it is protecting you from.\r\n 8314: \r\n 8315: ## PBUG-20260829-12 -- the GGUF lane's prebuilt wheel dies with ILLEGAL INSTRUCTION on a 13th-gen Intel mobile CPU\r\n 8316: \r\n 8317: > **ID NOTE.** Logged as `-11` in commit `f0b3008f` from the 4060 window and\r\n 8318: > renumbered to `-12` here: the 5080 window had pushed a different `-11`\r\n 8319: > (the GGUF cache-epoch defect above) six minutes earlier. Two boxes\r\n 8320: > appending to the same log picked the same next number. Nothing was lost --\r\n 8321: > `.gitattributes` marks this file `merge=union` precisely so a tail\r\n 8322: > collision keeps BOTH sides -- but the ID had to be made unique by hand.\r\n 8323: > The commit message for `f0b3008f` still says `-11`; this entry is that bug.\r\n 8324: \r\n 8325: - **found:** 2026-08-29 on the 4060 (MRKT), running the operator's own\r\n 8326:   frictionless-install question: which writer dropdowns actually load and finish\r\n 8327:   on 8 GB. Live leg, profile `otr_4060_high_probe` (the 5080's\r\n 8328:   `otr_qwen2507_haunted_proof` with `gguf_n_ctx` lowered to fit 8 GB).\r\n 8329: - **artifact:** a real canonical render. The GGUF writer got FURTHER than any\r\n 8330:   previous attempt -- binding imported through OTR's own path, VRAM preflight\r\n 8331:   PASSED, llama.cpp began initialising -- and then the process took a hardware\r\n 8332:   fault:\r\n 8333: \r\n 8334:       [GGUFNative] VRAM Preflight: Free=6.94 GB | Needed=5.23 GB\r\n 8335:         (weights=2.33 from Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q4_K_M.gguf, kv=2.80 @ n_ctx=4096)\r\n 8336:       [GGUFNative] Initializing llama.cpp Llama instance:\r\n 8337:         n_ctx=4096, n_gpu_layers=-1, n_batch=512\r\n 8338:       File \".../llama_cpp/_internals.py\", line 263, in __init__\r\n 8339:         ctx = llama_cpp.llama_init_from_model(self.model.model, self.params)\r\n 8340:       OSError: [WinError -1073741795] Windows Error 0xc000001d\r\n 8341: \r\n 8342:   `0xc000001d` is `STATUS_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION`. Prompt executed in 2.58 s.\r\n 8343: - **root cause (high confidence, from the hardware):** the wheel contains CPU\r\n 8344:   instructions this processor does not implement. Measured on this box:\r\n 8345: \r\n 8346:       CPU  : 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900H\r\n 8347:       torch: cpu capability == AVX2   (i.e. NO AVX-512)\r\n 8348: \r\n 8349:   Raptor Lake / Alder Lake MOBILE parts have AVX-512 fused off. The wheel is\r\n 8350:   `llama_cpp_python 0.3.35` from `abetlen.github.io/llama-cpp-python/whl/cu124`\r\n 8351:   -- a prebuilt binary, not compiled on the target. It crashes at\r\n 8352:   `llama_init_from_model`, i.e. after the model file is accepted and while the\r\n 8353:   context is being built.\r\n 8354: - **why this is a FRICTIONLESS-INSTALL defect and not a niche crash:** the\r\n 8355:   affected hardware is mainstream consumer laptops -- exactly the machines an\r\n 8356:   8 GB profile exists to serve. And the whole non-NVIDIA story rides this same\r\n 8357:   binding: `otr_mac_mps`, `otr_amd8_rocm`, `otr_amd16_rocm` and `cpu_floor` all\r\n 8358:   run GGUF at `quant_policy: \"none\"` because bitsandbytes NF4 is CUDA-only. A\r\n 8359:   writer dropdown that hard-faults the worker on a common CPU is worse than one\r\n 8360:   that refuses: there is no exception to catch, no loud degradation, and the\r\n 8361:   message a user sees names a Windows error code rather than a cause.\r\n 8362: - **it is NOT a VRAM problem and NOT the model:** the preflight explicitly\r\n 8363:   passed with 1.7 GB of headroom, the weight is byte- and sha256-exact against\r\n 8364:   the pinned row (2,497,281,120 / 3605803b...c67e597), and the failure is a CPU\r\n 8365:   fault raised through ctypes.\r\n 8366: - **fix:** NOT FIXED, and the right fix is not mine to pick. Candidates, in the\r\n 8367:   order I would try them: (a) a wheel built without AVX-512 -- upstream ships\r\n 8368:   variants, and the correct one for this class of CPU needs identifying rather\r\n 8369:   than assuming; (b) build from source on the target with the ISA detected\r\n 8370:   (costs a toolchain, which is itself friction); (c) treat the GGUF lane as\r\n 8371:   UNSUPPORTED on mobile Intel until (a) is proven, and say so in the docs\r\n 8372:   instead of letting a user discover it as a Windows error code.\r\n 8373: - **what this settles for the 8 GB dropdown question, which is what the\r\n 8374:   operator actually asked:** `unsloth/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-GGUF` is NOT a\r\n 8375:   frictionless 8 GB writer on this hardware today. Its size and licence are\r\n 8376:   ideal (2.33 GiB, Apache-2.0, ungated, anonymous fetch in 46 s) and its VRAM\r\n 8377:   fit is proven with headroom -- but it cannot start on this CPU. The proven\r\n 8378:   frictionless writer on this box remains `google/gemma-4-E2B-it`\r\n 8379:   (unquantized, transformers lane), which has three published episodes.\r\n 8380: - bible-worthy: STRONG CANDIDATE. The reusable class is new tonight and is not\r\n 8381:   the dependency-version class: *a prebuilt native wheel encodes an ISA\r\n 8382:   assumption that no dependency declaration expresses -- it installs cleanly,\r\n 8383:   imports cleanly, passes its own preflight, and then takes a hardware fault.*\r\n 8384:   Version pinning cannot express it and a pip resolver cannot catch it.\r\n 8385: \r\n 8386: ### CORRECTION to PBUG-20260829-12 (the illegal-instruction fault) -- root cause DOWNGRADED, 2026-08-29\r\n 8387: \r\n 8388: **The AVX-512 hypothesis in that entry is probably WRONG, and I am saying so\r\n 8389: before it hardens into a fact.** I wrote it as \"high confidence, from the\r\n 8390: hardware\". The 5080's evidence, which arrived after, contradicts it:\r\n 8391: \r\n 8392:     5080  Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX (Arrow Lake)  -- llama.cpp loaded and\r\n 8393:           generated 22 times in one leg; a standalone load wrote prose in 0.6 s\r\n 8394:     4060  Intel i9-13900H (Raptor Lake)          -- 0xc000001d at\r\n 8395:           llama_init_from_model\r\n 8396: \r\n 8397: **Arrow Lake mobile has no AVX-512 either.** Intel has fused AVX-512 off on\r\n 8398: consumer parts since Alder Lake, so BOTH boxes lack it -- yet only one faults.\r\n 8399: An instruction neither CPU implements cannot explain why one works. The\r\n 8400: observation that this CPU is AVX2-max is still true; it is simply no longer\r\n 8401: load-bearing.\r\n 8402: \r\n 8403: **The stronger lead, and it was in front of me the whole time -- the WHEEL\r\n 8404: VERSIONS DIFFER:**\r\n 8405: \r\n 8406:     5080  llama_cpp_python 0.3.33   ggml-cuda.dll ~945 MB   WORKS\r\n 8407:     4060  llama_cpp_python 0.3.35   ggml-cuda.dll  819 MB   FAULTS\r\n 8408: \r\n 8409: Different build, different size, different result. A newer wheel raising its\r\n 8410: ISA baseline (or shipping a differently-tuned CUDA build) explains both\r\n 8411: observations without requiring the two CPUs to differ in a capability they do\r\n 8412: not differ in. **UNTESTED at time of writing** -- the test is to install 0.3.33,\r\n 8413: the version proven on the other box, and re-run through\r\n 8414: `_import_llama_cpp()`. That test is queued behind a live churn leg rather than\r\n 8415: run mid-render, because the running server may hold `llama.dll` open from the\r\n 8416: faulting attempt and swapping a loaded DLL is its own failure mode.\r\n 8417: \r\n 8418: **What is unchanged and still solid in -12:** the fault is real, reproducible,\r\n 8419: and fatal on this box; it is NOT a VRAM problem (preflight passed with 1.7 GB\r\n 8420: headroom) and NOT the artifact (byte- and sha256-exact against the pinned row);\r\n 8421: the user-visible symptom is a bare Windows error code; and the consequence for\r\n 8422: the dropdown question stands -- the GGUF writer is not a frictionless 8 GB\r\n 8423: choice on this machine TODAY.\r\n 8424: \r\n 8425: **What changes:** the reusable class is narrower than I claimed. It is not\r\n 8426: \"prebuilt wheels encode an ISA assumption your CPU may not meet\" as a general\r\n 8427: law -- that is still possible but unproven. What IS demonstrated is the thing\r\n 8428: this drill keeps proving from new angles: *two boxes running the \"same\"\r\n 8429: dependency were running different builds of it, and only the box that had never\r\n 8430: had it installed found out.* Same shape as kokoro 0.7.16 vs 0.9.4 and ffmpeg 9\r\n 8431: vs 8.0.1. The dev box's working version is not the version a fresh install\r\n 8432: receives.\r\n 8433: \r\n 8434: Recorded as a correction rather than an edit: the log is append-only and I\r\n 8435: would rather the wrong confidence stay visible next to what corrected it.\r\n 8436: \r\n 8437: ### CROSS-CUTTING NOTE, 2026-08-29 -- what one extra machine actually bought\r\n 8438: \r\n 8439: Not a bug. A synthesis of the nine logged on 2026-08-29, recorded here because\r\n 8440: the individual entries do not show the shape and the portability plan that\r\n 8441: argues it lives in `docs/2026-*/`, which is gitignored and therefore invisible\r\n 8442: to the other box.\r\n 8443: \r\n 8444: **FOUR ARE THE SAME DEFECT WEARING DIFFERENT CLOTHES:** kokoro 0.9.4 vs the\r\n 8445: 0.7.16 PyPI ships; ffmpeg 8.0.1 vs the 9 that removed `-vsync`; llama-cpp\r\n 8446: 0.3.33 (945 MB CUDA backend, works) vs 0.3.35 (819 MB, faults); and\r\n 8447: ComfyUI-AnimateDiff-Evolved, present on the proving box only because someone\r\n 8448: git-cloned it by hand at 03:00. In each case **the dev box held a version, or\r\n 8449: a file, that a fresh install does not get** -- and in each case the code was\r\n 8450: correct against what was installed locally.\r\n 8451: \r\n 8452: **THE OTHER FOUR ARE WORSE, AND ARE THE REAL ARGUMENT.** PBUG-07's size-tag\r\n 8453: collision, -08's VRAM gate, -10's token resolver and -11's cache epoch are\r\n 8454: arithmetic and logic that are CORRECT on 16 GB and WRONG on 8. -07 is the\r\n 8455: cleanest example: the `>= 12 GiB` branch returns before any size tag is read,\r\n 8456: so a 16 GB card cannot reach the bug by any test, however thorough. That is\r\n 8457: not a diligence gap. It is a gap diligence cannot close from one machine,\r\n 8458: because the second machine takes a different branch.\r\n 8459: \r\n 8460: **TWO CAME FROM THE BOXES CHECKING EACH OTHER, not from either running alone.**\r\n 8461: -09 surfaced when the 4060 asked why its friction numbers were measured on a\r\n 8462: hand-prepared box. And -12's first root cause -- \"no AVX-512\" -- was asserted\r\n 8463: with high confidence and then killed by the other box's contradicting evidence\r\n 8464: (llama.cpp generated 22 times on a CPU that also lacks AVX-512), which\r\n 8465: relocated the cause to a version difference that is now testable by hash.\r\n 8466: Neither the assertion nor the correction was available to one machine.\r\n 8467: \r\n 8468: **The operating consequence, and it is cheap:** any claim of the form \"works on\r\n 8469: consumer hardware\" is worth exactly the number of distinct machines it has run\r\n 8470: on. Ours was one. It is now two, and two found nine.\r\n 8471: \r\n 8472: ## PBUG-20260829-13 -- the SHIPPED workflow's default writer cannot load on an 8 GB card\r\n 8473: \r\n 8474: - **found:** 2026-08-29 by the 4060 window, sharpening a different report;\r\n 8475:   confirmed here by reading the shipped graph and executing the gate.\r\n 8476: - **the shipped graph, read directly from `workflows/otr_canonical.json`:**\r\n 8477:   `OTR_LedgerScriptWriter` carries `google/gemma-4-12b-it (11.9 GB)` in **both**\r\n 8478:   writer slots. Occurrences of Mistral-Nemo in that file: **zero.**\r\n 8479: - **so the constant and the graph disagree, and the graph wins.**\r\n 8480:   `_otr_model_catalog.DEFAULT_LLM` is `mistralai/Mistral-Nemo-Instruct-2407`,\r\n 8481:   but nothing a user does reaches that constant on the out-of-the-box path.\r\n 8482:   Open the shipped workflow, press Run -- the writer is the 12B.\r\n 8483: - **measured against each tier:**\r\n 8484: \r\n 8485:         8 GB card       ceiling  6.8  ->  FAIL   est 11.95 GB\r\n 8486:         12 GB card      ceiling 10.5  ->  WARN   est 11.95 GB\r\n 8487:         16 GB dev box   ceiling 14.5  ->  PASS   est 11.95 GB\r\n 8488: \r\n 8489:   The 8 GB user's exact experience: `VRAMFitFailedError: 'google/gemma-4-12b-it':\r\n 8490:   estimated 11.9 GB peak resident vs 6.8 GB ceiling -- pick a smaller model.`\r\n 8491:   Which is a correct refusal from a correct guard, delivered to someone who\r\n 8492:   never chose the model -- it was the shipped default.\r\n 8493: - **why the dev box cannot see it:** 11.95 against 14.5 is PASS. The identical\r\n 8494:   number is a silent pass here and a hard refusal there. Same class as -07 and\r\n 8495:   -08: correct on 16 GB, wrong on 8.\r\n 8496: - **NOT CHANGED, and this one is genuinely the operator's call, not a defect\r\n 8497:   with one right answer.** Two defensible defaults:\r\n 8498:   * ship the best writer for dev-class hardware and expect small-card users to\r\n 8499:     select a profile (today's behaviour, and the 12B IS the better writer);\r\n 8500:   * ship a default that runs everywhere -- `gemma-4-E2B-it` at 3.0 GB is\r\n 8501:     already proven, ungated, and is what the shipping 8 GB profile uses -- and\r\n 8502:     let 16 GB users move up.\r\n 8503:   The operator's standing rule cuts toward the second (\"a dropdown row is a\r\n 8504:   promise the model will load\"; a shipped DEFAULT is a stronger promise than a\r\n 8505:   dropdown row). But `otr_canonical.json` is the section-0 source of truth,\r\n 8506:   changing it regenerates every variant, and which writer greets a new user is\r\n 8507:   a product decision about first impressions, not a wiring fix. It waits.\r\n 8508: - **open question this does NOT answer, flagged by the 4060:** whether a clean\r\n 8509:   box downloads all 11.9 GB *before* being refused, or is refused first. That\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_88","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=9400; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'docs\\\\PROD_BUG_LOG.md' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 580 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 9400: \r\n 9401:     [OTR_ShotLock] music row shot_000_music is a pre-audio sentinel;\r\n 9402:       the timed music_open mirror owns the timeline\r\n 9403:     [OTR_ShotLock] music row shot_002_music is a pre-audio sentinel;\r\n 9404:       the timed music_close mirror owns the timeline\r\n 9405: \r\n 9406: **Clean-run counters:** `exceeds master audio` 0, cadence refusals 0,\r\n 9407: zero-frame warnings 0.\r\n 9408: \r\n 9409: **Scope of the claim, stated honestly:** this proves the fix on ONE bank, ONE\r\n 9410: act, ONE run. It does not prove the general case, and signal 1 is unanswered.\r\n 9411: What it does close is the specific question that was only falsifiable here --\r\n 9412: the 4060 is the only box producing the sentinel ledger shape, and on that\r\n 9413: shape the excess is now zero rather than 18.93 s.\r\n 9414: \r\n 9415: **Bank status on 8 GB after this leg: 5 of 5 proven.** `scifi_news_pro` was\r\n 9416: the last one failing; it had taken down two 8 GB episodes in a day\r\n 9417: (PBUG-16 at the video stage 72 min in, PBUG-20 at the writer 5.7 min in).\r\n 9418: \r\n 9419: - bible-worthy: YES. Class: *a phantom row that exists to be mirrored must be\r\n 9420:   dropped by the consumer that reads the pre-audio ledger, not by the producer\r\n 9421:   that writes it -- deleting it at the source killed an unrelated engine\r\n 9422:   (`fastwan_8gb`) that depended on the sentinel.*\r\n 9423: \r\n 9424: ### AMENDMENT to PBUG-20260829-16 -- the ROOT is a 39-day ledger hole from commit 59286499, and attempt 1 was actively harmful (2026-08-30, 5080 + 4060)\r\n 9425: \r\n 9426: **Attempt 1 (`_music_cue_dur_s`, resolve a duration through `anchor_line_id`)\r\n 9427: was not merely incomplete. It was harmful exactly where it fired, and it is\r\n 9428: now removed rather than narrowed.**\r\n 9429: \r\n 9430: **How it was caught.** The 5080 predicted an 18.87 s overshoot from its own\r\n 9431: reading; the 4060 then measured **18.9312 s** on different hardware, a different\r\n 9432: bank, and a different episode length -- mechanism reproduced to within 60 ms.\r\n 9433: The leg reached the master mux ~86 minutes in, which it had never done before,\r\n 9434: and was rejected:\r\n 9435: \r\n 9436:     ERROR node 85 (OTR_MasterAudioMux) raised ValueError\r\n 9437:     silent video 140.0400s exceeds master audio 97.6278s by 42.4122s,\r\n 9438:     over the credits-tail budget (23.3610s [declared] + 0.1200s tol = 23.4810s)\r\n 9439:     by 18.9312s\r\n 9440: \r\n 9441: **Why the fix was wrong: the anchor points at the row that must NOT render.**\r\n 9442: A music beat exists in two lifecycle stages, and the 4060 established this from\r\n 9443: `otr_meta_brief_image_prompt.py`'s own comments (L1382, L1450):\r\n 9444: \r\n 9445: * the **SENTINEL** (`shot_000_music`) is authored PRE-audio under the bank's own\r\n 9446:   id, untimed **by design**, and load-bearing -- it is what tells the assembler\r\n 9447:   to mint the mirror and reserve that beat's still;\r\n 9448: * the **MIRROR** (`music_opening_001`) is minted POST-audio under the\r\n 9449:   assembler's deterministic id and carries the real `start_s`/`dur_s`.\r\n 9450: \r\n 9451: The cue's `anchor_line_id` points at the **sentinel**. So attempt 1 timed\r\n 9452: precisely the row that owns no timeline, emitting 10.0 s + 8.0 s a second time\r\n 9453: on top of the mirror that already carried them. 10.0 + 8.0 = 18.0 s; the\r\n 9454: measured 18.93 s is that plus frame-budget padding at 25 fps. These are **not\r\n 9455: duplicates** -- suppressing the bank's emission is what killed `fastwan_8gb`\r\n 9456: twice (PBUG-20260811-02, commit 3446af3f), so the fix excludes the sentinel from\r\n 9457: the **video beat list** only and changes nothing about what a bank emits.\r\n 9458: \r\n 9459: **The separate and larger half: a 39-day unowned-field regression.**\r\n 9460: Commit **`59286499` (\"feat: rip interstitial audio insertion\", 2026-07-22)**\r\n 9461: removed the `interstitial` cue slot and with it the only code that stamped\r\n 9462: timing on `music_inter` rows:\r\n 9463: \r\n 9464:     -_CUE_SLOTS = (\"opening\", \"closing\", \"interstitial\")\r\n 9465:     -    \"dur_s\": float(_seg_len) / float(sample_rate),\r\n 9466:     -        _mrow[\"start_s\"] = float(_p[\"start_s\"])\r\n 9467:     -        _mrow[\"dur_s\"]   = float(_p[\"dur_s\"])\r\n 9468:     -        _mrow[\"start_s_space\"] = \"scene_audio\"\r\n 9469:     -    \"lines_positioned=%d/%d music_inter_positioned=%d\",\r\n 9470: \r\n 9471: The rip was deliberate and stands. What it did not do is **re-home the ledger\r\n 9472: fields it stopped writing**, while the writer kept planning the beats\r\n 9473: (`_otr_episode_budget.py:254` -- `music_inter_count = (act_count - 1) if\r\n 9474: include_act_breaks else 0`). Every act break since has therefore minted a row\r\n 9475: with no cue, no audio, no `start_s` and no `dur_s`. It budgets to ZERO frames\r\n 9476: and the video stage refuses it 40+ minutes in:\r\n 9477: \r\n 9478:     GhostCadenceError: cadence needs a delivered target of at least 1 frame,\r\n 9479:     got 0 -- a 0-frame beat is a planning bug, not something to paper over here\r\n 9480: \r\n 9481: **Measured across all 2,021 ledgers on the 5080:**\r\n 9482: \r\n 9483:     ledgers WITH music_inter : 742\r\n 9484:       ...of those, PUBLISHED : 0\r\n 9485:       ...with ANY timing     : 1   (music_inter_01_001, 2026-07-21, dur_s=4.087)\r\n 9486: \r\n 9487: That single timed survivor is dated **one day before the rip** and is where the\r\n 9488: new `MUSIC_BRIDGE_FALLBACK_DUR_S = 4.0` comes from -- a measurement, not a taste\r\n 9489: call. This is exactly the failure the operator's standing rule names: *a ripped\r\n 9490: pass with an unowned field is a broken render, not a simplification.* Every\r\n 9491: 5-act episode requested since 07-22 has died on it.\r\n 9492: \r\n 9493: **Fix (attempt 2, `b2f36242`)** -- `nodes/otr_shot_lock.py`:\r\n 9494: 1. `_untimed_music_sentinels()` -- a pre-audio sentinel never becomes a video\r\n 9495:    beat while a timed mirror of the same role exists. Keyed on **role**, not cue\r\n 9496:    id, because keying on the cue selects the wrong row of the pair.\r\n 9497: 2. `_music_cue_dur_s` **deleted**. There is no shape in which reading that\r\n 9498:    anchor for a duration is correct.\r\n 9499: 3. `MUSIC_BRIDGE_FALLBACK_DUR_S` for `music_inter` **only** -- an untimed\r\n 9500:    character line still raises the loud zero-frame warning rather than silently\r\n 9501:    receiving a number.\r\n 9502: \r\n 9503: **Blast radius (both machines, `nodes/` is shared):** 5080 banks carry no\r\n 9504: sentinel (`anchor_line_id=None`), so rule 1 is inert there and they gain\r\n 9505: act-break bridges that render instead of crashing; 4060 banks carry the\r\n 9506: sentinel, and rule 1 is what removes their 18.93 s. Both shapes are covered by\r\n 9507: unit fixtures lifted from real ledgers. Full suite 12,457 passed.\r\n 9508: \r\n 9509: **Status: 5080 half in proof on live hardware; 4060 half NOT yet proven.** The\r\n 9510: sentinel rule is only falsifiable on the box that has the sentinel shape. Until\r\n 9511: a `scifi_news_pro` leg runs against `b2f36242`, this entry is half-evidenced and\r\n 9512: must not be read as closed. Per the operator's directive tonight -- *\"don't kill\r\n 9513: a duration mismatch, just let it fly\"* -- a residual mismatch now warns and\r\n 9514: publishes rather than discarding a finished episode, so a green `otr/obs` entry\r\n 9515: carrying a warning is a pass.\r\n 9516: \r\n 9517: ### PBUG-20260829-16 CLOSED on both shapes -- the sentinel half proven on 8 GB (2026-08-30, 4060 leg cc8b0e1d)\r\n 9518: \r\n 9519: The amendment above states the 4060 half was **not** yet proven. It is now, on\r\n 9520: the only box that produces the sentinel shape.\r\n 9521: \r\n 9522: **4060, leg `cc8b0e1d`**, profile `otr_nvidia_8gb_haunted`, `--source-bank\r\n 9523: scifi_news_pro`, `--act-count 1`, 8188 MiB card. Server PID 24536 booted\r\n 9524: 04:03:07 against a commit that landed 03:56:42, so the fix was genuinely in the\r\n 9525: process under test rather than a stale import -- the check that cost this\r\n 9526: project a wasted 27-minute leg earlier in the same session.\r\n 9527: \r\n 9528: **The sentinel rule fired verbatim, on both phantom rows:**\r\n 9529: \r\n 9530:     [OTR_ShotLock] music row shot_000_music is a pre-audio sentinel;\r\n 9531:                    the timed music_open mirror owns the timeline\r\n 9532:     [OTR_ShotLock] music row shot_002_music is a pre-audio sentinel;\r\n 9533:                    the timed music_close mirror owns the timeline\r\n 9534: \r\n 9535: **The 18.93 s is gone, to within one frame:**\r\n 9536: \r\n 9537:     video 155.28 s | audio 130.71 s | delta 24.57 s\r\n 9538:     credits roll declared 24.6 s  ->  excess = -0.03 s\r\n 9539: \r\n 9540: One frame at 25 fps is 0.04 s, so the excess is zero within measurement\r\n 9541: resolution. The mechanism is visible:\r\n 9542: \r\n 9543:     [OTR_SilentComposite] A/V-sync master reconcile: 3293 -> 3268 frames\r\n 9544:                           (positioned ledger cross-check)\r\n 9545:     [OTR_SilentComposite] assembled 15 beats -> 3268 frames @25fps 1920x1080\r\n 9546:                           silent; budget 3268 OK\r\n 9547: \r\n 9548: **Published and independently verified:**\r\n 9549: `signal_lost_the_engineered_bloom_20260830_044129_..._final.mp4`,\r\n 9550: 137,335,578 B, 155.28 s, h264 1920x1080 @25fps + aac, both streams present by\r\n 9551: ffprobe. Counters clean: `exceeds master audio` 0, cadence refusals 0, 9666: check immediately above it, so a successful `ffprobe` printed `OK` 9839: moves. Model the residual as `4.0 x bridges + ~0.9 s` and do not treat the tail 9840: term as stable.\r\n 9841: \r\n 9842: **Neither correction touches the fix.** Both are about how confidently two\r\n 9843: numbers seen twice were described as laws. Recorded because the entry above\r\n 9844: would otherwise send the next reader looking for a bank-specific cause that does\r\n 9845: not exist.\r\n 9846: \r\n 9847: **PBUG-20260830-01 is now proven on the live path** (4060 leg 8ea39846, pushed\r\n 9848: 49c3a570): `duration_check ... OVER_BUDGET by 4.9271s (published anyway)`,\r\n 9849: agreeing with its warning to four decimals, where three hours earlier the same\r\n 9850: node printed `OK`.\r\n 9851: \r\n 9852: ---\r\n 9853: \r\n 9854: ## PBUG-20260830-24 -- a published episode is invisible to the API that published it\r\n 9855: \r\n 9856: **Verified on a live pod leg** (RunPod `m2ukhfg1j6tpki`, prompt\r\n 9857: `27572fc3-3010-46b0-83fe-357895bb1304`, RESULT SUCCESS in 2058s, 2026-08-30).\r\n 9858: The episode published correctly: 77 MB\r\n 9859: `signal_lost_the_silk_of_the_lapel_20260830_225025_..._final.mp4` in\r\n 9860: `otr/obs`, with `obs_publish OK` in the report. Then the pull tool found\r\n 9861: nothing, and it was right to.\r\n 9862: \r\n 9863: `/history` for that prompt records exactly **two** node outputs:\r\n 9864: \r\n 9865:     node 12  gifs  sub=otr/episodes/<ep>/audio  fn=<ep>.mp4      <- INTERMEDIATE\r\n 9866:     node 92  text  {\"ok\": true, \"episode_id\": ..., ...}          <- receipt\r\n 9867: \r\n 9868: The published deliverable is **not in `/history` under any key.**\r\n 9869: `OTR_MasterAudioMux` is `OUTPUT_NODE = True` but returns a bare\r\n 9870: `(final_video_path, report)` tuple with no `ui` payload, so ComfyUI never\r\n 9871: records the artifact it just published. The only video the API advertises is\r\n 9872: the pre-post-processing mux out of `audio/` -- no credits, no captions, no\r\n 9873: procgen blend.\r\n 9874: \r\n 9875: **Why this is worth an entry rather than a shrug.** Any consumer that asks the\r\n 9876: API \"what did this render produce\" is told about the intermediate and not the\r\n 9877: episode. That is how a 27 MB intermediate reached `otr/obs` earlier the same day\r\n 9878: and sat there looking finished: the tool took the largest video in `/history`,\r\n 9879: and `/history` genuinely had nothing better to offer. Tightening that tool to\r\n 9880: require the `otr/obs` subfolder plus the `_final` marker was correct and made it\r\n 9881: permanently empty, because it was filtering a list the deliverable was never on.\r\n 9882: \r\n 9883: **Owner: not yet assigned, and deliberately.** The root fix is for the mux to\r\n 9884: declare its published copy (`{\"ui\": {...}, \"result\": (...)}`), which is the\r\n 9885: conventional ComfyUI shape for an OUTPUT_NODE. It is a return-contract change\r\n 9886: with a real blast radius -- five call sites across\r\n 9887: `tests/test_publication_eligibility.py` and `tests/test_video_render_path_cw4.py`\r\n 9888: unpack the 2-tuple -- and `OTR_OBS_DIR` can point obs outside the server's\r\n 9889: output root, where declaring a `/view`-able path would be a lie. That wants\r\n 9890: deciding, not a drive-by.\r\n 9891: \r\n 9892: **Worked around, honestly:** `scripts/otr_pod_obs_bridge.py` now defaults to an\r\n 9893: SSH route that lists the obs directory, because the directory obs_publish writes\r\n 9894: IS the record. The `--http` route is kept and prints, up front, that it will\r\n 9895: find nothing.\r\n 9896: \r\n 9897: ---\r\n 9898: \r\n 9899: ## PBUG-20260831-01 -- an untimed music_open/close with no mirror budgets to ZERO frames\r\n 9900: \r\n 9901: **Verified against real ledgers on the 5080, 2026-08-31.** The leg dies at the\r\n 9902: VIDEO stage, roughly seventy minutes in, after the writer, cast, voices and the\r\n 9903: full audio master are already done:\r\n 9904: \r\n 9905:     RenderError: shot shot_b006 engine 'animatediff15_v3_haunted_video' failed\r\n 9906:     to render; fallbacks are disabled (FailureKind.CRASH_BEFORE_LOAD)\r\n 9907:     -- Ghost Signal cadence needs a delivered target of at least 1 frame, got 0\r\n 9908:     -- a 0-frame beat is a planning bug, not something to paper over here\r\n 9909: \r\n 9910: **THE GAP IS IN MY OWN PBUG-20260830-16 FIX, which covered two of three cases.**\r\n 9911: An untimed music row can reach the frame budget three ways and only two were\r\n 9912: handled:\r\n 9913: \r\n 9914: | case | handled by | before |\r\n 9915: |---|---|---|\r\n 9916: | untimed `music_open`/`music_close` WITH a timed mirror | `_untimed_music_sentinels` drops it -- the mirror owns the timeline | correct |\r\n 9917: | untimed `music_inter` | `_music_bridge_dur_s` -> 4.0 s | correct |\r\n 9918: | untimed `music_open`/`music_close` with **NO** mirror | **nothing** | **0 frames** |\r\n 9919: \r\n 9920: The third case is invisible to both guards by construction: the sentinel filter\r\n 9921: can only recognise a sentinel when a timed mirror EXISTS to make it one, and the\r\n 9922: bridge helper checked `role != \"music_inter\"` and returned None. So the row\r\n 9923: arrived with `dur_s=None`, `frames = int(round(float(dur) * fps)) if dur else 0`\r\n 9924: gave it zero, and the engine correctly refused inputs it cannot render.\r\n 9925: \r\n 9926: **Proven, not reasoned about.** In `signal_lost_the_apprentices_number` the\r\n 9927: sentinel filter dropped **0** rows while `shot_000_music` (`music_open`) and\r\n 9928: `shot_002_music` (`music_close`) both resolved to an effective duration of\r\n 9929: `None`. Six recent ledgers carry **no `samples` on any row at all**, so\r\n 9930: `compute_clip_budget`'s cumulative-samples path never runs and every beat\r\n 9931: depends entirely on `dur_s` -- which is what makes a single missing duration\r\n 9932: fatal rather than merely imprecise.\r\n 9933: \r\n 9934: **FIX:** `_music_bridge_dur_s` now covers every untimed music role\r\n 9935: (`_UNTIMED_MUSIC_FALLBACK_ROLES`), not just the interstitial. Still not a raise:\r\n 9936: an OOM is the only acceptable killer, and a bank with a genuinely silent beat\r\n 9937: must not be blocked by a budget helper.\r\n 9938: \r\n 9939: **BLAST RADIUS, measured across eight recent ledgers rather than asserted:**\r\n 9940: \r\n 9941:     rows that already had a duration (provably untouched)   223\r\n 9942:     rows rescued from ZERO frames by this change             29\r\n 9943: \r\n 9944: The helper is only consulted when `dur_s` is falsy, so a row with any duration\r\n 9945: cannot change. The only rows affected are ones that currently produce zero\r\n 9946: frames -- i.e. rows that currently kill the leg. Both machines gain; neither can\r\n 9947: regress. 37 shot-lock/budget tests pass.\r\n 9948: \r\n 9949: ---\r\n 9950: \r\n 9951: ## PBUG-20260901-01 -- RunPod provisioner rejects a clean template because its venv is untracked\r\n 9952: \r\n 9953: **Verified on a live secure RunPod** (`jaq27diu24jt1p`, RTX 4090, 2026-09-01).\r\n 9954: The pod passed the actual capacity gate (125 GB API allocation,\r\n 9955: `memory.max=124999999488`) and the OTR checkout was clean at\r\n 9956: `c383e92679d8257e9d9b9bf73dedb6b195bab3be`. Provisioning stopped before pack\r\n 9957: or weight work with:\r\n 9958: \r\n 9959:     FATAL: ComfyUI core has tracked changes; refusing to overwrite them\r\n 9960: \r\n 9961: The core had no tracked or staged changes. The RunPod ComfyUI template normally\r\n 9962: keeps `.venv-cu128/` plus sibling-engine links (`chatterbox`, `dia`, and\r\n 9963: `index-tts`) as untracked entries inside the checkout. The provisioner asked\r\n 9964: `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all`, so its clean-core safety check\r\n 9965: silently widened from �?otracked work that checkout could replace�?? to �?oanything\r\n 9966: the template owns but Git does not.�?? The diagnostic then mislabeled those\r\n 9967: untracked entries as tracked changes.\r\n 9968: \r\n 9969: **FIX:** the guard now uses `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` and\r\n 9970: names both tracked and staged changes in its refusal. This preserves the safety\r\n 9971: boundary that matters while allowing normal template-owned venvs and links.\r\n 9972: Git's checkout operation remains a second fail-closed boundary: it refuses if\r\n 9973: an untracked path would actually be overwritten by the pinned commit.\r\n 9974: \r\n 9975: **VERIFY:** `tests/test_otr_pod_provision_contract.py` pins the exact production\r\n 9976: flag, creates a real temporary Git checkout, proves an untracked `.venv-cu128`\r\n 9977: does not trip the guard, and proves a tracked edit still does. The same paid pod\r\n 9978: must then pass this boundary before the HuMo/LTX qualification continues.\r\n 9979: \r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
