{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_90","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=270; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 100 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"  270: # PHASE 04 �?\" INPUT_TYPES & WIDGETS\r\n  271: # Widget positional pitfalls, hidden inputs, validation, lazy inputs.\r\n  272: # �\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?\r\n  273: \r\n  274: - id: \"04.06\"\r\n  275:   phase: 4\r\n  276:   area: widgets\r\n  277:   symptom: Node widget default value overrides LLM-generated output on every run; file paths, titles, seed values lock to hardcoded defaults.\r\n  278:   cause: Workflow JSON hardcodes widget defaults (e.g. \"The Last Frequency\") on multiple nodes. Title extraction only handles dict format, not list format, so falls through to widget default silently on every run.\r\n  279:   fix: |\r\n  280:     (1) Clear hardcoded defaults in workflow JSON �?\" set to empty string \"\" on writer/video nodes.\r\n  281:     (2) Add multi-tier title resolution: LLM-parsed (first), derived from content (second), widget fallback (third), hard-fail if stuck default (last).\r\n  282:     (3) Log `TITLE_TRACE | raw=\"...\" | parsed=\"...\" | final=\"...\"` so future runs surface the decision.\r\n  283:     (4) In title-extraction code, scan both dict-format top-level `title` and list-format `{\"type\": \"title\", \"value\": ...}` tokens.\r\n  284:   verify: Filename and title vary across 5 consecutive runs. TITLE_TRACE logs show non-stuck values. No fallback to stuck default shows in logs.\r\n  285:   tags: [widget-default, title, stuck, override, workflow-json]\r\n  286:   legacy_id: BUG-LOCAL-035\r\n  287: \r\n  288: - id: \"04.01\"\r\n  289:   phase: 4\r\n  290:   area: widgets\r\n  291:   symptom: Widget shows wrong value after loading saved workflow. No error.\r\n  292:   cause: widgets_values is positional, not named. Adding/removing/reordering INPUT_TYPES optional widgets shifts every position after the change.\r\n  293:   fix: Every time you add, remove, or reorder an optional widget, manually update widgets_values in EVERY saved workflow JSON. Prefer append-only changes. Commit code + workflow JSONs in the same atomic commit.\r\n  294:   verify: For every node, count INPUT_TYPES optional widgets and compare to len(widgets_values). They must match.\r\n  295:   tags: [widgets, json, silent, workflow-json]\r\n  296:   legacy_id: \"2\"\r\n  297: \r\n  298: - id: \"04.02\"\r\n  299:   phase: 4\r\n  300:   area: widgets\r\n  301:   symptom: After removing a widget from INPUT_TYPES, saved workflows load with values mapped into the wrong widgets.\r\n  302:   cause: widgets_values is positional. Removal shifts subsequent positions.\r\n  303:   fix: Manually delete the corresponding positional entry from widgets_values in EVERY saved workflow JSON. Prefer append-only INPUT_TYPES changes. Commit code + JSON together.\r\n  304:   verify: Audit script loads each workflow and checks widget count vs array length.\r\n  305:   tags: [widgets, json, removal, workflow-json]\r\n  306:   legacy_id: \"51\"\r\n  307: \r\n  308: - id: \"04.03\"\r\n  309:   phase: 4\r\n  310:   area: hidden-inputs\r\n  311:   symptom: Multiple instances of same node share cached state and cross-contaminate.\r\n  312:   cause: Module-level cache uses a fixed key, not per-instance.\r\n  313:   fix: '\"Declare `\\\"hidden\\\": {\\\"unique_id\\\": \\\"UNIQUE_ID\\\"}` in INPUT_TYPES and use unique_id as cache key.\"'\r\n  314:   verify: Two instances in one workflow produce different outputs.\r\n  315:   tags: [hidden, cache, unique_id]\r\n  316:   legacy_id: \"23\"\r\n  317: \r\n  318: - id: \"04.04\"\r\n  319:   phase: 4\r\n  320:   area: validation\r\n  321:   symptom: VALIDATE_INPUTS rejects a connected (slot) input as None even though runtime would receive a real value.\r\n  322:   cause: Connected inputs arrive as None during validation.\r\n  323:   fix: \"Always check `if input is None: return True` first. Only validate values that are actually present.\"\r\n  324:   verify: Connect a Primitive to the input; workflow runs.\r\n  325:   tags: [validate_inputs, none]\r\n  326:   legacy_id: \"24\"\r\n  327: \r\n  328: - id: \"04.05\"\r\n  329:   phase: 4\r\n  330:   area: lazy\r\n  331:   symptom: Disabled toggle still triggers expensive upstream model loads.\r\n  332:   cause: Default execution evaluates ALL upstream nodes before calling execute.\r\n  333:   fix: '\"Mark expensive inputs `{\\\"lazy\\\": True}` in INPUT_TYPES. Implement check_lazy_status() to return [] when disabled. For blocking downstream too, return ExecutionBlocker(None).\"'\r\n  334:   verify: Disable toggle; upstream LLM loader does not run.\r\n  335:   tags: [lazy, performance, executionblocker]\r\n  336:   legacy_id: \"28\"\r\n  337: \r\n  338: # �\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?\r\n  339: # PHASE 05 �?\" EXECUTION MODEL\r\n  340: # Coordination, migration, list outputs, interrupts, execution order.\r\n  341: # �\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?\r\n  342: \r\n  343: - id: \"05.01\"\r\n  344:   phase: 5\r\n  345:   area: coordination\r\n  346:   symptom: Multiple node instances need to coordinate (e.g. cooperative camera selection) without a wire between them.\r\n  347:   cause: ComfyUI nodes execute independently with no shared channel.\r\n  348:   fix: Pass the same seed to all instances. Each computes the same shuffled order deterministically. Use sha256(unique_input)[:4] as offset to differentiate. For sequential modes, module-level dict + threading lock.\r\n  349:   verify: Same seed produces same allocation across runs.\r\n  350:   tags: [coordination, determinism, shared-seed]\r\n  351:   legacy_id: \"13\"\r\n  352: \r\n  353: - id: \"05.02\"\r\n  354:   phase: 5\r\n  355:   area: migration\r\n  356:   symptom: After renaming a node class, every saved workflow shows red \"node type not found.\"\r\n  357:   cause: Workflows store the old NODE_CLASS_MAPPINGS key.\r\n  358:   fix: Keep the old key as an alias in NODE_CLASS_MAPPINGS pointing to the new class. One line of code, permanent backward compat. For input renames, accept both names in execute() with fallback.\r\n  359:   verify: Load an old workflow JSON; node resolves.\r\n  360:   tags: [migration, alias, compat]\r\n  361:   legacy_id: \"21\"\r\n  362: \r\n  363: - id: \"05.03\"\r\n  364:   phase: 5\r\n  365:   area: list-execution\r\n  366:   symptom: Returning a list from a node sends the whole list as one payload (downstream runs once).\r\n  367:   cause: Without OUTPUT_IS_LIST=(True,), ComfyUI wraps the return as a single value.\r\n  368:   fix: Set OUTPUT_IS_LIST=(True,) on the node class. For receivers that need ALL items at once, set INPUT_IS_LIST=True on the consumer.\r\n  369:   verify: Downstream runs N times for N-item list.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_91","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=450; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 70 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"  450:   verify: Hash-stable across runs.\r\n  451:   tags: [signature, cache]\r\n  452:   legacy_id: \"35\"\r\n  453: \r\n  454: - id: \"06.04\"\r\n  455:   phase: 6\r\n  456:   area: memory\r\n  457:   symptom: Python process grows multi-GB over a long session and gets killed.\r\n  458:   cause: Unbounded module-level dict cache. torch.cuda.empty_cache() doesn't free Python references.\r\n  459:   fix: Use BoundedCache (OrderedDict + max_size eviction). Provide explicit clear affordance. Document that restart is the only true reset.\r\n  460:   verify: Run 500 prompts; memory plateaus.\r\n  461:   tags: [memory, cache, leak]\r\n  462:   legacy_id: \"34\"\r\n  463: \r\n  464: - id: \"06.05\"\r\n  465:   phase: 6\r\n  466:   area: sync\r\n  467:   symptom: Edits don't take effect; \"fixed\" bug persists after restart.\r\n  468:   cause: Two copies of code (source repo + custom_nodes). Edited one, ComfyUI loaded the other. Or __pycache__ served stale .pyc.\r\n  469:   fix: Sync ALL files from source repo to custom_nodes after every edit. Clear __pycache__ in BOTH locations. Fully restart ComfyUI (re-queue does NOT reload modules).\r\n  470:   verify: MD5-hash every .py in both locations; all must match.\r\n  471:   tags: [sync, cache, pycache, restart]\r\n  472:   legacy_id: \"1\"\r\n  473: \r\n  474: # �\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?\r\n  475: # PHASE 07 �?\" TENSORS, AUDIO, VIDEO\r\n  476: # VRAM, dtype, device, shape, audio contract, crossfades.\r\n  477: # �\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?�\"?\r\n  478: \r\n  479: - id: \"07.01\"\r\n  480:   phase: 7\r\n  481:   area: vram\r\n  482:   symptom: CUDA OOM when running an LLM + a video generation model sequentially even though each fits.\r\n  483:   cause: Models loaded at import time stay resident. Or no explicit unload between models.\r\n  484:   fix: Lazy-load pattern (model=None until first call). Explicit _unload_model() that del's globals + torch.cuda.empty_cache(). Sequence pipeline so each heavy model unloads before the next loads.\r\n  485:   verify: nvidia-smi shows VRAM freed between stages.\r\n  486:   tags: [vram, lazy-load, unload]\r\n  487:   legacy_id: \"3\"\r\n  488: \r\n  489: - id: \"07.02\"\r\n  490:   phase: 7\r\n  491:   area: vram\r\n  492:   symptom: Device mismatch error on second run after switching CUDA device.\r\n  493:   cause: Cached model is on the old device.\r\n  494:   fix: Track device alongside model in cache; reload on device change. Always call .eval() after loading.\r\n  495:   verify: Switch devices; cached model reloads.\r\n  496:   tags: [cache, device-change]\r\n  497:   legacy_id: \"3.4\"\r\n  498: \r\n  499: - id: \"07.03\"\r\n  500:   phase: 7\r\n  501:   area: vram\r\n  502:   symptom: CUDA OOM despite ComfyUI thinking it freed enough VRAM.\r\n  503:   cause: Manually .to(\"cuda\") models are invisible to ComfyUI's LRU registry; it can't evict them.\r\n  504:   fix: Use comfy.model_management.load_model_gpu(model). Or maintain manual unload + empty_cache discipline and unload before next heavy model.\r\n  505:   verify: nvidia-smi shows model freed after manual unload.\r\n  506:   tags: [vram, lru, mm]\r\n  507:   legacy_id: \"40\"\r\n  508: \r\n  509: - id: \"07.04\"\r\n  510:   phase: 7\r\n  511:   area: model-class\r\n  512:   symptom: Model loads without error but ignores conditioning input and produces garbage.\r\n  513:   cause: Wrong transformers class (e.g. AudioModelForConditionalGeneration instead of AudioModelMelodyForConditionalGeneration). Both load from the same checkpoint.\r\n  514:   fix: Read the model card. Verify class matches use case. Log type(model).__name__ on load. Watch for \"unused kwargs\" warnings.\r\n  515:   verify: Output reflects conditioning input (melody-conditioned music actually follows the melody).\r\n  516:   tags: [silent, wrong-class, transformers]\r\n  517:   legacy_id: \"4\"\r\n  518: \r\n  519: - id: \"07.05\"\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_92","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=2710; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 50 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 2710: - id: \"12.40\"\r\n 2711:   phase: 12\r\n 2712:   area: caching\r\n 2713:   symptom: |\r\n 2714:     Long pipeline reports \"LLM cache mismatch (Context Cap: 6144)\"\r\n 2715:     every phase boundary even though every input (model_id,\r\n 2716:     profile, context_cap, etc.) is identical. Each mismatch\r\n 2717:     triggers a full unload + cold reload (~15-17s). Wall-clock\r\n 2718:     thrashes 60-90s per run with zero quality benefit.\r\n 2719:   cause: |\r\n 2720:     Two design weaknesses: (1) the cache check was a single\r\n 2721:     `or`-joined boolean expression, so when it tripped the log\r\n 2722:     only said \"mismatch detected\" without naming the field �?\"\r\n 2723:     debugging required guessing; (2) the device-residency\r\n 2724:     sub-check read `next(model.parameters()).device` and only\r\n 2725:     inspected the FIRST parameter from the iterator. With\r\n 2726:     bitsandbytes 4-bit quantization the parameter iteration\r\n 2727:     order is non-deterministic and a CPU-resident metadata\r\n 2728:     buffer (quant_state, embed_tokens with offload, dequant\r\n 2729:     scratch) frequently lands first. The check then fired a\r\n 2730:     false-positive eviction every phase boundary.\r\n 2731:   fix: |\r\n 2732:     (a) Replace the single-param probe with a scan of up to N\r\n 2733:     parameters (N=8 is enough). Treat the cache as still-resident\r\n 2734:     if ANY scanned param is on `cuda`. Use the model's own\r\n 2735:     `.hf_device_map` attribute when accelerate is in play.\r\n 2736:     (b) Replace the inline boolean cache check with explicit\r\n 2737:     per-field delta collection �?\" each of `device`, `quantized`,\r\n 2738:     `model_id`, `budget_profile`, `VERSION`, `context_cap` is\r\n 2739:     compared individually and recorded as\r\n 2740:     `(name, cached, requested)` tuples.\r\n 2741:     (c) The runtime log line names the specific drifted field(s)\r\n 2742:     so future regressions are diagnosable on first glance.\r\n 2743:   verify: |\r\n 2744:     Across N phase-boundary transitions with identical inputs,\r\n 2745:     the cache-mismatch log line never appears. Wall-clock from\r\n 2746:     one phase to the next drops from ~17s to <1s.\r\n 2747:   tags: [llm, cache-thrash, quantized-param-iter, device-check, opaque-log, bitsandbytes, xphase-06]\r\n 2748:   legacy_id: BUG-LOCAL-065\r\n 2749: \r\n 2750: - id: \"12.41\"\r\n 2751:   phase: 12\r\n 2752:   area: regression\r\n 2753:   symptom: |\r\n 2754:     A pipeline reports \"Success\" with a count (e.g. \"polished\r\n 2755:     19 -> 16 lines\") but the very next stage reports a wildly\r\n 2756:     different count (\"AFTER_PARSE count=1\"). 15 dialogue lines\r\n 2757:     vanished silently between the two stages with no warning.\r\n 2758:   cause: |\r\n 2759:     Three independent structural asymmetries can combine to make\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=3090; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 60 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 3090:     explicit encoding) inherits Windows' legacy cp1252 console codec, not\r\n 3091:     UTF-8. Any print() in a startup-time script (prestartup_script.py, a\r\n 3092:     custom node's module-level code, etc.) that emits a non-ASCII\r\n 3093:     character -- most commonly a status emoji like a checkmark or\r\n 3094:     cross-mark -- raises UnicodeEncodeError the instant it executes,\r\n 3095:     killing the boot before the server ever binds its port.\r\n 3096:   fix: |\r\n 3097:     Set PYTHONUTF8=1 and PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 in the environment of\r\n 3098:     every detached/headless launch path (launcher .cmd/.ps1), not just\r\n 3099:     the interactive one. Any new boot path (soak harness, CI runner,\r\n 3100:     scheduled task) must set both vars before invoking python.exe.\r\n 3101:   verify: |\r\n 3102:     Boot the server via the detached launcher and confirm PYTHONUTF8/\r\n 3103:     PYTHONIOENCODING are present in its environment; the boot log shows\r\n 3104:     no UnicodeEncodeError and the server reaches a listening state within\r\n 3105:     the normal ~20s window. Grep every launcher script for PYTHONUTF8=1.\r\n 3106:   tags: [windows, encoding, cp1252, headless, boot, launcher, unicode]\r\n 3107:   legacy_id: PBUG-20260612-01\r\n 3108: \r\n 3109: - id: \"07.17\"\r\n 3110:   phase: 7\r\n 3111:   area: vram\r\n 3112:   symptom: |\r\n 3113:     A full-episode VRAM soak measures a peak of 15.8GB against a 14.5GB\r\n 3114:     ceiling, on both device modes, in a pipeline stage (LTX-AV) that\r\n 3115:     combines a text encoder with a video generation pass.\r\n 3116:   cause: |\r\n 3117:     The text encoder (Gemma) stayed GPU-resident through the entire video\r\n 3118:     pass instead of being released once its output was consumed, holding\r\n 3119:     VRAM the video stage then had to compete for.\r\n 3120:   fix: |\r\n 3121:     Move the text encoder to CPU after its output is captured (attempted).\r\n 3122:     A later re-measured soak PROVED this offload had NO effect on the\r\n 3123:     measured peak, and the change was reverted. Record the disproof\r\n 3124:     explicitly so the same offload isn't re-attempted blind on a future\r\n 3125:     VRAM-ceiling rip -- re-derive the actual peak-VRAM contributor before\r\n 3126:     trying an \"obvious\" fix.\r\n 3127: \r\n 3128:     AMENDED 2026-08-20 -- SCOPE THE DISPROOF, IT IS NOT UNIVERSAL. The \"no\r\n 3129:     effect\" result above is real for the case it was measured in: the peak\r\n 3130:     was in SAMPLING, so moving the encoder changed nothing. On a lane where\r\n 3131:     the OOM happens AT THE ENCODE NODE ITSELF the same offload is decisive.\r\n 3132:     Measured on ltx25_video (Gemma-4 12B Q5 GGUF): a GPU encode transiently\r\n 3133:     demands ~15.6GB (weights to card + GGML dequant scratch) and OOMs at\r\n 3134:     `node 'neg' (encode)` with 13.76GB allocated / 1.88GB requested on a\r\n 3135:     15.92GB limit; pinning the encoder to CPU took that encode's VRAM cost\r\n 3136:     from ~13,760MB to ~0MB and the OOM stopped.\r\n 3137: \r\n 3138:     THE DISTINGUISHING TEST, and it is one line of the traceback: read WHICH\r\n 3139:     NODE raised. Encoder offload helps iff the OOM is at the encode. If the\r\n 3140:     OOM is in the sampler, this entry's original disproof stands and the\r\n 3141:     offload is a waste of a fix.\r\n 3142: \r\n 3143:     AND THE OFFLOAD HAS A TRAP OF ITS OWN: `initial_device` alone does NOT\r\n 3144:     pin an encoder to CPU. ComfyUI-GGUF's CLIPLoaderGGUF already passes\r\n 3145:     `initial_device = text_encoder_offload_device()`, so \"the loader loads\r\n 3146:     to CPU\" reads TRUE while `load_models_gpu` still pulls the patcher to\r\n 3147:     `patcher.load_device` at encode time. Pinning requires `load_device`\r\n 3148:     AND `offload_device` too. That false signal cost three wrong diagnoses\r\n 3149:     before anyone checked what the loader actually does with the flag.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_94","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=5570; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 75 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 5570:     hardening production against a shape it will never see.\r\n 5571:   verify: |\r\n 5572:     Exercise the line with an object that HAS the primary attribute and assert\r\n 5573:     the alternative never runs: put a counter or a raising sentinel in the\r\n 5574:     fallback position and require it untouched. That assertion is the one that\r\n 5575:     fails on the eager form and passes on the branch form, so it is the proof.\r\n 5576:     Exercise it again with an object that lacks the attribute and assert the\r\n 5577:     fallback returns the correct value. Assert that a genuinely uncomputable\r\n 5578:     diagnostic emits a log line naming the exception and the type, so no None is\r\n 5579:     silent. Grep the tree for eager defaults of the same family -- a getattr or\r\n 5580:     a dict.get whose default position holds a CALL -- and require each to be\r\n 5581:     branch-shaped. Drive the whole check through the real object at least once,\r\n 5582:     because a suite that only ever meets the double can only ever verify the\r\n 5583:     double.\r\n 5584:   tags: [eager-default-argument, getattr, lazy-fallback, bare-except, silent-none, diagnostic-field, test-double-fidelity, false-failure, python-semantics]\r\n 5585:   legacy_id: HANDOFF-L1589\r\n 5586: - id: \"06.07\"\r\n 5587:   phase: 6\r\n 5588:   area: caching\r\n 5589:   symptom: |\r\n 5590:     A QA plan promises a replay leg -- queue the same workflow twice with\r\n 5591:     nothing changed and get the same episode back -- and the leg can never be\r\n 5592:     made to pass. Every requeue authors a fresh story with fresh seeds and\r\n 5593:     different output bytes. Nothing errors, no cache hit is ever logged, and\r\n 5594:     nodes built specifically to FREEZE a decision re-take it on every run. The\r\n 5595:     investigation goes hunting for hidden nondeterminism in the model or the\r\n 5596:     RNG, when the graph has simply been told to discard its cache on purpose.\r\n 5597:   cause: |\r\n 5598:     The node's IS_CHANGED returns time.time(), which can never equal the\r\n 5599:     previous run's value, so ComfyUI's cache always misses. As local advice\r\n 5600:     that is fine, and this guide prescribes it for a live data fetch (see\r\n 5601:     06.01). But a cache signature is not a local property: invalidation\r\n 5602:     propagates to dependents, so one always-refresh node at the head of a\r\n 5603:     subgraph forces its whole downstream cone to re-execute. When that head\r\n 5604:     node is also nondeterministic -- an LLM writer that authors new content on\r\n 5605:     each call -- the subgraph has no stable identity for anything downstream to\r\n 5606:     replay, and a freeze or lock node that returns a wall clock as well pins\r\n 5607:     nothing, because what it froze is re-taken before it is ever read.\r\n 5608:     Replayability is forfeited by the wiring, not lost to chance, so no amount\r\n 5609:     of seed discipline downstream can recover it, and a plan that promises a\r\n 5610:     replay leg over such a subgraph is promising what its own graph forbids.\r\n 5611:   fix: |\r\n 5612:     Derive the signature from what the output actually DEPENDS on, never from a\r\n 5613:     wish to run again. Return a digest of the resolved inputs plus the external\r\n 5614:     state the node reads -- file mtime and size, the environment variables it\r\n 5615:     consults, a version constant you bump when behaviour changes -- so\r\n 5616:     identical conditions produce an identical string and a genuine cache hit\r\n 5617:     becomes possible at all. Where a run truly must be forced, make the forcing\r\n 5618:     an INPUT: a nonce or seed widget the operator bumps, which the graph\r\n 5619:     records and a later run can restate, rather than a clock nobody can\r\n 5620:     reproduce. Reserve time.time() for edge nodes whose output cannot be\r\n 5621:     described by their inputs, and keep those off the deterministic spine,\r\n 5622:     because every node downstream of one inherits its unrepeatability. If a\r\n 5623:     promised replay leg sits under an always-refresh node, either change the\r\n 5624:     signature or move the replay off the live queue onto a captured frozen\r\n 5625:     artifact -- do not ship the promise unchanged.\r\n 5626:   verify: |\r\n 5627:     Call IS_CHANGED twice with identical kwargs and require the two values to\r\n 5628:     be EQUAL, then change one field and require them to differ; failing the\r\n 5629:     first half is a cache defeat, failing the second is a stale-output bug.\r\n 5630:     Statically scan every IS_CHANGED body in the pack for a wall-clock call and\r\n 5631:     assert none appears on any node the deterministic spine is declared to\r\n 5632:     cover. Prove propagation in both directions: perturb one upstream signature\r\n 5633:     and require the dependents to re-execute, leave it untouched and require\r\n 5634:     they do not. Then run the claim end to end -- queue the unchanged workflow\r\n 5635:     twice and require the second run's output digest to equal the first's.\r\n 5636:   tags: [is_changed, cache-signature, determinism, replay, invalidation-propagation, freeze-node, wall-clock, content-derived-signature, xref-06.01]\r\n 5637:   legacy_id: KIBITZ-2026-07-12-replay-leg\r\n 5638: - id: \"07.24\"\r\n 5639:   phase: 7\r\n 5640:   area: audio\r\n 5641:   symptom: |\r\n 5642:     The default output of a generative slot collapses in quality after a\r\n 5643:     release, and the operator can only describe it subjectively -- the music\r\n 5644:     bed no longer sounds like music. The prompt builder's history is clean back\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=9270; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 90 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":" 9270:     (4) When scalar and vision judge disagree, a human looks. Do not ship the\r\n 9271:     scalar's number as evidence until that disagreement is resolved.\r\n 9272:   tags: [metric-inverted, selftest-passes-while-wrong, one-directional-validation-set,\r\n 9273:     false-green-instrument, empty-parse-reports-clean, perceptual-defect-needs-perceptual-judge,\r\n 9274:     vlm-as-metric-police, position-bias, native-pixel-crop, unfalsifiable-scalar]\r\n 9275:   legacy_id: \"OTR-2026-08-20-three-instruments-reported-success-while-doing-nothing\"\r\n 9276: \r\n 9277: - id: \"12.120\"\r\n 9278:   phase: 12\r\n 9279:   area: structural-support-does-not-prove-model-semantic-compatibility\r\n 9280:   symptom: |\r\n 9281:     A reference-conditioning branch exists, every named node is installed, the\r\n 9282:     graph validates, and the executor runs it without an exception -- yet the\r\n 9283:     referenced outputs are materially corrupted while otherwise matched\r\n 9284:     unreferenced outputs are clean. In the live case, every character scene\r\n 9285:     using one clean portrait acquired a square/noise lattice across clothing\r\n 9286:     and walls; unreferenced scenes, portraits, and music cards did not. The\r\n 9287:     generic conditioning node had therefore produced the strongest possible\r\n 9288:     false green: structural support, successful execution, and bad pixels.\r\n 9289:   cause: |\r\n 9290:     NODE AVAILABILITY IS NOT A TRAINING CLAIM. A generic node can construct a\r\n 9291:     tensor shape that a model architecture accepts, and checkpoint headers can\r\n 9292:     contain compatible projection or padding keys, without the exact checkpoint\r\n 9293:     ever having been trained to interpret that tensor as a reference identity.\r\n 9294:     The capability was admitted from graph reachability and weight shape rather\r\n 9295:     than from checkpoint-specific evidence. Validation could prove only that\r\n 9296:     the path was executable; it could not prove what the path meant to the\r\n 9297:     denoiser. Because the branch was advertised as a capability, the dispatcher\r\n 9298:     then selected it automatically and cached its corrupted products under the\r\n 9299:     same engine identity as the previously clean path.\r\n 9300:   fix: |\r\n 9301:     DENY SEMANTIC CAPABILITIES BY DEFAULT. A shipping engine must not set\r\n 9302:     `accepts_reference_image = True` merely because a generic reference node is\r\n 9303:     installed or its graph executes. Admit the capability only for an exact\r\n 9304:     model artifact whose identity is pinned and whose approval record cites a\r\n 9305:     matched-pixel A/B: same checkpoint, prompt, negative, seed, dimensions and\r\n 9306:     delivery path, with only the reference branch changing. Keep the diagnostic\r\n 9307:     graph/harness available even after rejection so a future checkpoint can be\r\n 9308:     evaluated without silently re-enabling production routing.\r\n 9309:     When evidence rejects a previously advertised capability, turn the public\r\n 9310:     capability off, preserve any separately proven identity mechanism such as a\r\n 9311:     portrait-derived seed, and bump the engine/cache version. Otherwise already\r\n 9312:     cached corrupt artifacts survive the code fix and make the disabled path\r\n 9313:     appear to remain live.\r\n 9314:   verify: |\r\n 9315:     (1) Static admission gate: scan class-level capability declarations. Every\r\n 9316:     literal `accepts_reference_image = True` must be accompanied by a literal\r\n 9317:     `reference_image_approval` mapping containing the exact checkpoint SHA-256\r\n 9318:     and the repository-contained, tracked path to a matched-pixel A/B approval\r\n 9319:     receipt. The receipt must repeat the checkpoint hash, identify the model\r\n 9320:     filename and reference hash, record SUCCESS for both arms, prove OFF lacks\r\n 9321:     and ON consumes the branch, hold a complete match signature equal across\r\n 9322:     arms, record distinct graph and native-output pixel hashes, and carry an\r\n 9323:     attributed APPROVED pixel verdict. Malformed, dynamic, missing, untracked,\r\n 9324:     unmatched or negative evidence is a release failure; the presence of\r\n 9325:     `ReferenceLatent` or an equivalent node is never sufficient evidence.\r\n 9326:     (2) Pixel gate: run reference OFF and ON on separate fresh boots with every\r\n 9327:     generation parameter and the delivered crop held equal. Record both graphs,\r\n 9328:     model identity, seeds, native-pixel outputs, and an explicit human or\r\n 9329:     perceptual verdict in the approval artifact.\r\n 9330:     (3) Rejection gate: if ON loses, assert the shipping engine advertises the\r\n 9331:     capability as false and the dispatcher does not supply the reference, while\r\n 9332:     any independent proven identity seed still resolves.\r\n 9333:     (4) Cache gate: assert the engine/cache version differs from the rejected\r\n 9334:     version and an old cache key cannot hit. A clean fresh render is not enough\r\n 9335:     if stale corrupted media can still be returned.\r\n 9336:   tags: [reference-conditioning, semantic-compatibility, structural-support-is-not-training,\r\n 9337:     model-specific-admission, matched-pixel-ab, capability-deny-by-default,\r\n 9338:     cache-version-bump, false-green-graph, checkpoint-sha256, approval-receipt]\r\n 9339:   legacy_id: \"PBUG-20260820-01\"\r\n 9340: - id: \"12.121\"\r\n 9341:   phase: 12\r\n 9342:   area: an-uncontrolled-second-variable-sends-every-arm-of-a-visual-ab-to-the-same-wrong-verdict\r\n 9343:   symptom: |\r\n 9344:     A visual A/B (or a whole elimination campaign) returns the same verdict on\r\n 9345:     every arm, and the verdict is about the wrong stage. Two live instances in\r\n 9346:     one week on one project. (1) Six single-variable knob tests on a video lane\r\n 9347:     all reported \"grid clearly visible\" -- because the GRID WAS IN THE\r\n 9348:     CONDITIONING STILL, minted by the upstream image engine; an I2V lane is a\r\n 9349:     preservation machine, so every arm faithfully reproduced the defect it was\r\n 9350:     being tested for removing. Months of renders chased knobs that could never\r\n 9351:     have worked. (2) An identity-drift probe swapped the conditioning still to\r\n 9352:     a portrait but kept the recipe's text prompt describing a different scene;\r\n 9353:     BOTH arms obediently walked away from the portrait to the prompted scene,\r\n 9354:     and scoring it as written would have condemned a refine stage for\r\n 9355:     executing its prompt correctly.\r\n 9356:   cause: |\r\n 9357:     The contrast was impure: the arms differed by the intended knob, but the\r\n 9358:     SUBJECT carried a second uncontrolled variable that dominated the outcome\r\n 9359:     -- an upstream-minted defect in instance (1), a prompt/conditioning\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$start=10430; $i=$start-1; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'BUG_BIBLE.yaml' | Select-Object -Skip (\"'$start-1) -First 110 | ForEach-Object { $i++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"10430:     matches the interpreters it genuinely supports (python_version,10492:     or classic one.10508:   cause: >-\r\n10509:     The out-of-band model lives in the pack's own cache, outside the host's\r\n10510:     model registry, so the host's eviction logic never sees it and never\r\n10511:     frees it; the pack released it after one earlier stage but reloaded it\r\n10512:     for a later prompt-writing pass and never released it again before the\r\n10513:     render. Every in-process probe missed the defect because the probe never\r\n10514:     had the writer loaded. A generic \"free every registered model\" call is\r\n10515:     also blind to it for the same reason.\r\n10516:   fix: >-\r\n10517:     Give the pipeline ONE canonical residue-freer that releases the\r\n10518:     out-of-band caches (LLM, TTS) first, then surgically detaches any\r\n10519:     host-tracked patcher, then flushes the allocator, and call it in every\r\n10520:     GPU stage's load preflight -- image dispatch included, once per dispatch\r\n10521:     before the first local render, gated so cloud adapters and cache-hit-only\r\n10522:     dispatches never trigger it. Confirm nothing after that stage requests the\r\n10523:     model again, or the release becomes a reload cost.\r\n10524:   verify: >-\r\n10525:     Instrument the render site on the small card to log free VRAM and the\r\n10526:     diffusion model's resident pages at its load; require the log to show the\r\n10527:     out-of-band model released (its allocated bytes dropping to near zero in\r\n10528:     the freer's own report) and the diffusion model loaded with usable\r\n10529:     memory. Then run the same leg on the big card and require the freer's\r\n10530:     report to show the same release there (the bug is present on both; only\r\n10531:     the symptom differs), with the stills rendering afterwards.\r\n10532:   tags: [out-of-band-model, writer-llm-residency, residue-freer,\r\n10533:     stage-preflight, low-vram, host-registry-blind, in-process-probe-miss]\r\n10534:   legacy_id: OTR-PBUG-20260902-02\r\n10535: \r\n10536: - id: \"12.147\"\r\n10537:   phase: 12\r\n10538:   area: registry-publishing\r\n10539:   symptom: >-\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
